//@version=6

// ============================================================================
// std_time: civil calendar, adjusters, zones, market calendars, day counts
//
// DESIGN
//   One canonical conversion, everything else derived from it. The civil
//   <-> epoch pair below is Howard Hinnant's days_from_civil / civil_from_days
//   (the algorithm adopted by C++20 <chrono>): exact, branchless, no loops, no
//   lookup tables, valid for the whole proleptic Gregorian range. Day-of-week,
//   the adjusters, ISO weeks, DST boundaries and the holiday calendar are all
//   computed from that pair rather than reimplemented, so there is no second
//   implementation that can silently disagree with the first.
//
//   Two ideas borrowed from java.time and Temporal drive the API shape:
//
//   1. CIVIL TIME AND EXACT TIME ARE DIFFERENT KINDS OF ARITHMETIC.
//      java.time separates Period (years/months/days) from Duration
//      (milliseconds) because they are not interchangeable: "+1 day" across a
//      daylight-saving boundary is not "+86400000 ms", and "+1 month" has no
//      fixed length at all. Here that split is carried in the names:
//      plus_days / plus_months / plus_years move the calendar and preserve
//      wall-clock time; plus_ms / plus_seconds / plus_minutes / plus_hours
//      move the instant. Reach for the wrong one and you get the classic
//      one-hour bug.
//
//   2. AN OFFSET IS NOT A ZONE.
//      A DateTime carries a fixed offset, which makes it java.time's
//      OffsetDateTime. It is a valid type but it is not a zone: store -5 in
//      March and it is still -5 in July. Anything that needs real zone
//      behaviour goes through the Zone enum and the rule engine.
//
// PROVENANCE
//   * civil conversions ....... H. Hinnant, "chrono-Compatible Low-Level Date
//                               Algorithms" (days_from_civil / civil_from_days)
//   * adjuster semantics ...... java.time TemporalAdjusters.dayOfWeekInMonth
//   * arithmetic semantics .... java.time LocalDate.plusMonths / Period.between
//   * overflow policy ......... TC39 Temporal `overflow: constrain | reject`
//   * ISO week date ........... ISO 8601 §4.1.4
//   * Easter .................. Anonymous Gregorian computus
//                               (Meeus / Jones / Butcher)
//
// SCOPE AND LIMITS
//   * The calling convention is four rules, held with one stated exception,
//     so a
//     signature can be predicted rather than looked up:
//       - A question about a civil date is a method on DateTime:
//         d.is_trading_day(ex), d.iso_week(), d.adjusted(conv). There is no
//         (Year, Month, Day) overload of any of them.
//       - A function that builds a date takes loose ints, and only builders
//         do: new_datetime, nth_weekday_of_month, date_from_iso_week,
//         monthly_expiry_day. A bare int triple always means construction.
//       - A function that takes an instant takes it first, and stays a free
//         function: unix_to_date(t, utc), unix_to_date_zone(t, z),
//         offset_at(t, z), format_time(t, pattern, z), next_fomc_after(t).
//         Zone.to_unix and its gap and overlap probes are the exception,
//         documented at their definitions: they read civil fields as local
//         in a zone, which is the one thing a DateTime cannot express.
//       - `float utc` always means a fixed offset. A zone that follows a
//         daylight-saving rule is always a `Zone`. No parameter in this file
//         is a float that behaves like a rule.
//   * Zones model rules, not history. Each zone declares rules_from(),
//     the first year its rules are exact; before that the oldest known rule is
//     extrapolated backward and should not be trusted.
//   * Exchange holidays are rules plus a table of unscheduled closures. The
//     tables cannot be complete for the future: no rule predicts a hurricane
//     or a state funeral, so completeness is a claim with a date on it. NYSE
//     and LSE sessions and closing times agree with exchange_calendars (XNYS,
//     XLON) on every day from 1976 to 2035, the full range that reference
//     covers; before 1976 the rules run unchecked. EUREX agrees with
//     exchange_calendars (XEUR) over its whole range, 2006-08-14 to
//     2027-08-13, with zero differences; its TARGET2-shaped rule set needs no
//     one-off table. JPX agrees with exchange_calendars (XTKS) over the same
//     range; its rules are exact from 2000, so 2000-2005 follow the statute
//     unchecked, and its equinox holidays use the standard 1980-2099
//     approximation, so answers past 2099 are extrapolation. CME's dates
//     agree with CMES from 2000, except the four days after 11 September
//     2001, which this library closes and that reference does not. CRYPTO
//     has nothing to check: every calendar day trades, weekends included.
//     HKEX agrees with exchange_calendars (XHKG) from 2000 to 2049, and ASX
//     and TSX with XASX and XTSE from 1995 to 2049. Session instants for the
//     two daylight-saving zones hold only to 2037: past the 32-bit boundary
//     the reference itself misapplies daylight saving.
//     is_holiday means "closed by the published calendar", not "nothing else
//     can close the market".
//   * Every calendar states the years it answers exactly, through
//     calendar_from() and calendar_through(). A rule-driven calendar has no
//     horizon and reports na for one. A calendar with a tabled layer stops
//     where the table does: HKEX at 2049 with its lunar festivals, JPX at 2099
//     with the equinox approximation. Past a horizon is_holiday cannot help:
//     bool has no room for "not known", so it reads a tabled holiday as an
//     ordinary trading day. Ask closed_for_holiday() there, which answers
//     Known.UNKNOWN rather than a false NO.
//   * Three calendars are tables rather than rule sets and stop at the end of
//     2026: Shanghai entirely, Bombay and Singapore apart from a handful of
//     fixed dates. Their holidays are lunar, lunisolar or Islamic, or are
//     re-announced annually: Shanghai's block lengths have moved three times
//     in twenty years, so nothing extrapolates them. They need their tables
//     refreshed each year to keep answering; calendar_through says so, and
//     closed_for_holiday answers UNKNOWN past it.
//   * Counting trading days does not walk them. trading_days_between and
//     holidays_between propose the days a calendar could shut on, test only
//     those, and subtract from a closed-form weekday count. The proposal is
//     per-exchange and deliberately generous; a day it never proposed would be
//     invisible, so the anchors stay a superset over each calendar's whole
//     modelled range.
//   * Economic releases are a published list with a hard horizon. Outside it
//     the answer is Known.UNKNOWN, not a false NO. UNKNOWN is a value, not na:
//     check it with is_yes() or against the enum, never with na().
//   * VIX settlement is a rule rather than a table: the Wednesday thirty days
//     before the following month's third Friday, stepped to the previous
//     business day when that Wednesday or that Friday is a Cboe holiday. The
//     rule reproduces every settlement on the published Cboe calendars
//     2021-2026, including all four Tuesday exceptions. Past the last
//     published calendar the same arithmetic runs as projection; Cboe can
//     move any future date by circular, which no rule predicts.
//   * CME sessions are the Globex equity-index schedule. Its daily maintenance
//     halt is not modelled, and other CME products keep different hours. The
//     XETRA preset carries no holiday calendar at all: the German cash
//     market's holidays are unmodelled and it runs on the weekday mask alone.
//   * A session holds at most one intraday break: every market modelled
//     here with a break takes exactly one, and a session that crosses
//     midnight may not carry one at all.
//   * Leap seconds do not exist in Unix time and are not modelled.
//   * The calendar is proleptic Gregorian: it is arithmetic, not history.
//     Dates before the 1582 cutover never happened as written, and year 0
//     exists (astronomical numbering), unlike in common historical usage.
//   * Times inside a spring-forward gap do not exist and times inside a
//     fall-back overlap happen twice. A fixed-offset DateTime cannot express
//     either, which is why the two conversions are different functions and not
//     one with a flag: DateTime.to_unix reads its own offset literally, while
//     Zone.to_unix takes an explicit Resolver and can be asked, via Zone.is_gap
//     and Zone.is_ambiguous, whether the question is even well posed.
//   * Errors follow five clauses, and every @returns in this file names
//     exactly one of them:
//       RAISE ............ the arguments cannot mean anything: 31 February,
//                          nth = 0, an hour of 25, a walk that runs past its
//                          bound. A caller bug, reported at the call site
//                          rather than passed downstream as a value.
//       `na` ............. the call is well formed and no answer exists: the
//                          fifth Friday of a month that has four; a session
//                          window on a day the session does not run; every
//                          parser and every try_* form, since malformed
//                          input is data rather than a caller bug.
//       Known.UNKNOWN .... well formed, the answer exists in the world, and
//                          the table here does not reach it. The economic
//                          calendar and closed_for_holiday return this. It is
//                          a value, never na.
//       `false` .......... well formed, and the answer is no within what the
//                          library models: is_holiday, is_trading_day,
//                          is_open, is_leap_year.
//       in-band number ... only when the number is a real value of the result
//                          range, never as a stand-in for "none". This is why
//                          Session.time_to_open does not return 0 while the
//                          session runs: 0 is a real distance, so it cannot
//                          also be a flag. It returns the distance to the
//                          next open, and `na` when there is none.
//     The date withers are the one place two clauses meet, and Overflow picks:
//     a day past the end of a month clamps under CONSTRAIN and is `na` under
//     REJECT. The time withers raise; there is nothing to clamp an hour of
//     25 to. The two failure kinds side by side: nth_weekday_of_month RAISES
//     on nth = 0 (an invalid argument) and returns `na` for a fifth Friday
//     the month does not contain (a valid question with no answer).
//   * Month names, day names, holiday names and the relative formatter's
//     "in / ago / now" are English only; locale data is CLDR's job. The
//     holiday calendar runs on names: is_holiday is defined as holiday_name
//     being non-na, so the predicate and the name cannot disagree.
// ============================================================================

// @description A calendar, not a bag of helpers. Civil dates, durations and periods, time zones with real daylight-saving rules, twelve exchange calendars with holidays, half days and lunch breaks, trading-day arithmetic, sessions with on-the-bar close markers, option expiries including VIX settlement, ISDA day counts, and an ISO-8601 formatter and parser, every path derived from one civil<->epoch conversion, and every calendar agrees date-by-date with its exchange's published record. Each calendar states the years it answers exactly; outside them it says so instead of guessing.
library("std_time", overlay = false)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- enums --

// @enum            Day of the week. An enum rather than a bare int because 0=Sunday versus 1=Monday confusion is the most common class of date bug. Use `.to_int()` when the numeric code is needed.
// @field SUNDAY    Sunday, numeric code 0.
// @field MONDAY    Monday, numeric code 1.
// @field TUESDAY   Tuesday, numeric code 2.
// @field WEDNESDAY Wednesday, numeric code 3.
// @field THURSDAY  Thursday, numeric code 4.
// @field FRIDAY    Friday, numeric code 5.
// @field SATURDAY  Saturday, numeric code 6.
export enum Weekday
    SUNDAY = "Sunday"
    MONDAY = "Monday"
    TUESDAY = "Tuesday"
    WEDNESDAY = "Wednesday"
    THURSDAY = "Thursday"
    FRIDAY = "Friday"
    SATURDAY = "Saturday"

// @enum            A unit of time, used by the truncation and rounding helpers.
// @field MS        Millisecond.
// @field SECOND    Second.
// @field MINUTE    Minute.
// @field HOUR      Hour.
// @field DAY       Calendar day.
// @field WEEK      Calendar week, starting on a configurable day.
// @field MONTH     Calendar month.
// @field QUARTER   Calendar quarter, three months starting in January.
// @field YEAR      Calendar year.
export enum TimeUnit
    MS = "Millisecond"
    SECOND = "Second"
    MINUTE = "Minute"
    HOUR = "Hour"
    DAY = "Day"
    WEEK = "Week"
    MONTH = "Month"
    QUARTER = "Quarter"
    YEAR = "Year"

// @enum              How to round a date-time to a unit boundary. Modes follow TC39 Temporal, and are applied to the elapsed time since the Unix epoch, so TRUNC means toward the epoch.
// @field FLOOR       Toward the earlier boundary.
// @field CEIL        Toward the later boundary.
// @field TRUNC       Toward the epoch: FLOOR after 1970, CEIL before it.
// @field HALF_EXPAND Nearest boundary; exact ties go away from the epoch.
export enum RoundMode
    FLOOR = "Floor"
    CEIL = "Ceiling"
    TRUNC = "Truncate"
    HALF_EXPAND = "Half expand"

// @enum             What to do when calendar arithmetic lands on a day that does not exist, e.g. 31 January plus one month. Named for TC39 Temporal's `overflow` option.
// @field CONSTRAIN  Clamp to the last valid day of the month, so 31 January plus one month is 28 or 29 February. This is java.time's behaviour and the default.
// @field REJECT     Return na rather than invent a date.
export enum Overflow
    CONSTRAIN = "Constrain"
    REJECT = "Reject"

// @enum            How to resolve a local time that a daylight-saving transition made impossible or ambiguous. Named for Noda Time's ZoneLocalMappingResolver and Temporal's `disambiguation` option.
// @field EARLIER   Take the earlier of the two instants in an overlap; shift backward out of a gap.
// @field LATER     Take the later of the two instants in an overlap; shift forward out of a gap.
// @field COMPATIBLE Gaps shift forward by the gap length, overlaps take the earlier instant. Matches java.time and Temporal defaults.
// @field REJECT    Return na rather than resolve.
export enum Resolver
    EARLIER = "Earlier"
    LATER = "Later"
    COMPATIBLE = "Compatible"
    REJECT = "Reject"

// @enum            A time zone, meaning a standard offset plus a daylight-saving rule, not a fixed offset. Use `z.rules_from()` to ask how far back a zone's rules are exact.
// @field UTC       No offset, no daylight saving, ever.
// @field NEW_YORK  America/New_York. US rules, exact from 1976.
// @field CHICAGO   America/Chicago. US rules, exact from 1976.
// @field LONDON    Europe/London. EU rules, exact from 1996.
// @field FRANKFURT Europe/Berlin. EU rules, exact from 1996.
// @field SYDNEY    Australia/Sydney. Southern hemisphere, exact from 2008.
// @field TORONTO   America/Toronto. Shares the United States rule, and has done since 1976: the tzdb records no offset difference from New York after that year.
// @field SHANGHAI  Asia/Shanghai. No daylight saving since 1991, so exact from 1992; China observed it only from 1986 to 1991.
// @field TOKYO     Asia/Tokyo. No daylight saving since 1952.
// @field HONG_KONG Asia/Hong_Kong. Fixed +8; last observed daylight saving in 1979, exact from 1980.
// @field SINGAPORE Asia/Singapore. Fixed +8 since 1982 (it was +7:30 before), so exact from 1982.
// @field MUMBAI    Asia/Kolkata. Fixed +5:30 since 1945, so exact for the whole of Unix time.
// @field DUBAI     Asia/Dubai. Fixed +4 since 1920, so exact for the whole of Unix time.
export enum Zone
    UTC = "UTC"
    NEW_YORK = "America/New_York"
    CHICAGO = "America/Chicago"
    LONDON = "Europe/London"
    FRANKFURT = "Europe/Berlin"
    SYDNEY = "Australia/Sydney"
    TORONTO = "America/Toronto"
    SHANGHAI = "Asia/Shanghai"
    TOKYO = "Asia/Tokyo"
    HONG_KONG = "Asia/Hong_Kong"
    SINGAPORE = "Asia/Singapore"
    MUMBAI = "Asia/Kolkata"
    DUBAI = "Asia/Dubai"

// @enum            An exchange calendar. These are not variations of one calendar: CME closes on three days a year and trades the other US federal holidays on a shortened session, while the LSE runs on UK bank holidays that share only Good Friday and Christmas with the NYSE.
// @field NYSE      New York Stock Exchange, 09:30-16:00 New York, early close 13:00.
// @field LSE       London Stock Exchange, 08:00-16:30 London, early close 12:30.
// @field CME       CME Globex equity index, 17:00 the previous day to 16:00 Chicago, early close 12:00.
// @field JPX       Tokyo Stock Exchange, 09:00-15:30 Tokyo (15:00 through 2024), lunch 11:30-12:30, closed 31 December through 3 January, no half days.
// @field EUREX     Eurex derivatives, 08:00-22:00 Berlin, TARGET2-shaped holidays, no early closes. The German cash market (Xetra) keeps different hours and more holidays; this is the derivatives calendar.
// @field HKEX      Hong Kong Exchanges, 09:30-16:00 Hong Kong (10:00 through 2010), lunch 12:00-13:00, half days at 12:00 on Christmas Eve, New Year's Eve and Lunar New Year's Eve. The lunar holidays are tabled rather than computed, so this calendar answers exactly from 2000 to 2049 and no further; see calendar_through.
// @field ASX       Australian Securities Exchange, 10:00-16:00 Sydney, half days at 14:10 on the last sessions before Christmas and New Year. New South Wales holidays: no Labour Day, and ANZAC Day does not move off a weekend.
// @field TSX       Toronto Stock Exchange, 09:30-16:00 Toronto, half day at 13:00 on Christmas Eve only. No Easter Monday, and Family Day exists only from 2008.
// @field SSE       Shanghai Stock Exchange, 09:30-15:00 Shanghai, lunch 11:30-13:00, no early closes. Wholly tabled and exact only through 2026; see calendar_through.
// @field BSE       Bombay Stock Exchange, 09:15-15:30 Mumbai, no lunch break, no early closes. Six fixed dates with no substitution at all, and the rest tabled through 2026. The Muhurat evening session on Diwali is not modelled.
// @field SGX       Singapore Exchange, 09:00-17:00 Singapore, no lunch break in the modelled era, no early closes. Four fixed dates with a Sunday-only substitute, and the rest tabled through 2026.
// @field CRYPTO    Cryptocurrency, 24/7: every calendar day trades 00:00-24:00 UTC, no holidays, no weekends, no early closes.
export enum Exchange
    NYSE = "NYSE"
    LSE = "LSE"
    CME = "CME"
    JPX = "JPX"
    EUREX = "EUREX"
    HKEX = "HKEX"
    ASX = "ASX"
    TSX = "TSX"
    SSE = "SSE"
    BSE = "BSE"
    SGX = "SGX"
    CRYPTO = "Crypto"

// @enum            A three-valued answer, for questions a dated table or a bounded calendar cannot answer outside its window.
//
//                  This exists because Pine's bool cannot hold na: int, enum
//                  and object types can, bool cannot, so "return na, never
//                  false" is not expressible for a yes/no question. The enum
//                  is also the stronger contract: UNKNOWN will not type-check
//                  anywhere a bool is expected, so a caller cannot
//                  accidentally read "not known" as "no".
// @field YES       The date is in the table, or inside the calendar and shut.
// @field NO        The date is covered and is not one.
// @field UNKNOWN   The date lies outside the covered window. Not a no.
export enum Known
    YES = "Yes"
    NO = "No"
    UNKNOWN = "Unknown"

// @enum                 ISDA business-day conventions: how to roll a date that lands on a non-trading day.
// @field UNADJUSTED     Leave the date alone even if the market is shut.
// @field FOLLOWING      Move forward to the next trading day.
// @field MOD_FOLLOWING  Move forward, unless that crosses into the next month, in which case move backward instead.
// @field PRECEDING      Move backward to the previous trading day.
// @field MOD_PRECEDING  Move backward, unless that crosses into the previous month, in which case move forward instead.
export enum BusinessDay
    UNADJUSTED = "Unadjusted"
    FOLLOWING = "Following"
    MOD_FOLLOWING = "Modified following"
    PRECEDING = "Preceding"
    MOD_PRECEDING = "Modified preceding"

// @enum                Day-count conventions, per the ISDA 2006 Definitions. The convention is the denominator of every rate and the tau of every option price.
// @field ACT_365F      Actual days over a fixed 365. The usual choice for an option tenor, and the convention crypto derivatives actually quote.
// @field ACT_360        Actual days over 360. Money-market convention.
// @field ACT_ACT_ISDA  Actual days, split so that days in a leap year are divided by 366 and the rest by 365.
// @field D30_360       30/360 Bond Basis: a 31st becomes a 30th, and the end date flattens only when the start already has.
// @field D30E_360      30E/360 Eurobond: both ends flatten unconditionally.
// @field ACT_252       Trading days over 252. The convention behind business-day time in equity volatility. Under Exchange.CRYPTO every calendar day is a trading day, so this degenerates to actual days over 252, a denominator no crypto desk uses. Crypto tenors are ACT_365F.
export enum DayCount
    ACT_365F = "ACT/365F"
    ACT_360 = "ACT/360"
    ACT_ACT_ISDA = "ACT/ACT ISDA"
    D30_360 = "30/360 Bond Basis"
    D30E_360 = "30E/360"
    ACT_252 = "ACT/252"

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- types --

// @type            A civil date-time held together with the fixed UTC offset it is expressed in. This is java.time's OffsetDateTime: a local civil time plus an offset, not a zone.
// @field Year      Calendar year, proleptic Gregorian.
// @field Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @field Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @field Hour      Hour of day, 0-23.
// @field Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59.
// @field Second    Second of minute, 0-59.
// @field MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999.
// @field UTC       Offset from UTC in hours that this civil time is expressed in. A float because real offsets are not all whole hours: India is +5:30, Nepal +5:45, the Chatham Islands +12:45.
export type DateTime
    int Year = 1970
    int Month = 1
    int Day = 1
    int Hour = 0
    int Minute = 0
    int Second = 0
    int MS = 0
    float UTC = 0.0

// @type            A calendar-flavoured amount of time, in the sense of java.time's Period. It is not convertible to milliseconds without a reference date, because months and years have no fixed length.
// @field Years     Whole years.
// @field Months    Whole months.
// @field Days      Whole days.
export type Period
    int Years = 0
    int Months = 0
    int Days = 0

// @type            A half-open span of instants, [FromMS, ToMS).
// @field FromMS    Inclusive start, Unix milliseconds.
// @field ToMS      Exclusive end, Unix milliseconds.
export type Interval
    int FromMS = 0
    int ToMS = 0

// @type              A daylight-saving rule, expressed as data so that adding a zone to the Zone enum is a constant rather than a new code path. It is not an escape hatch for zones the enum does not ship: no high-level entry point takes a DstRule, so one cannot be handed to unix_to_date_zone, Zone.to_unix, a Session or an Exchange. Zones outside the enum are out of scope. The one direct consumer is offset_at_rule, which computes an offset and nothing else.
// @field StartMonth  Month daylight time begins, 1-12.
// @field StartDow    Weekday the transition falls on.
// @field StartNth    Which occurrence: 1 = first, -1 = last, as nth_weekday_of_month.
// @field StartAtHour Local hour of the transition, read as standard time unless StartAtUTC.
// @field StartAtUTC  When true, StartAtHour is UTC rather than local. The EU switches at 01:00 UTC across every member state, so its rule cannot be expressed without this flag.
// @field EndMonth    Month daylight time ends, 1-12. When EndMonth is less than StartMonth the rule is southern-hemisphere and wraps the year boundary.
// @field EndDow      Weekday the transition falls on.
// @field EndNth      Which occurrence.
// @field EndAtHour   Local hour of the transition, read as daylight time unless EndAtUTC.
// @field EndAtUTC    When true, EndAtHour is UTC rather than local.
// @field ShiftHours  How far the clock moves, in hours. A float, and not assumed to be 1: Lord Howe Island shifts 30 minutes. Zero means the zone has no daylight saving.
export type DstRule
    int StartMonth = 3
    Weekday StartDow = Weekday.SUNDAY
    int StartNth = 2
    int StartAtHour = 2
    bool StartAtUTC = false
    int EndMonth = 11
    Weekday EndDow = Weekday.SUNDAY
    int EndNth = 1
    int EndAtHour = 2
    bool EndAtUTC = false
    float ShiftHours = 1.0

// ------------------------------------------------------------- constants --

const int MS_SECOND = 1000
const int MS_MINUTE = 60000
const int MS_HOUR = 3600000
const int MS_DAY = 86400000

// Hinnant civil-algorithm constants
const int SHIFT_DAYS = 719468        // days from 0000-03-01 to 1970-01-01
const int ERA_DAYS = 146097          // days in a 400-year era
const int ERA_DAYS_M1 = 146096
const int ERA_YEARS = 400
const int QUAD_DAYS = 1460           // days in 4 non-leap-corrected years
const int CENT_DAYS = 36524          // days in a 100-year period
const int MONTH_C = 153              // 5-month cycle numerator

// 1970-01-01 was a Thursday; used to phase the day-of-week cycle.
const int EPOCH_DOW = 4

// --------------------------------------------------------------- helpers --

// Non-negative modulo. Pine's % takes the sign of the dividend, so a bare
// (a - b) % n can be negative; this form is non-negative for any inputs.
pmod(int a, int n) =>
    int r = a % n
    if r < 0
        r := r + n
    r

// Floor division for ints. Pine's / promotes to float and int() truncates
// toward zero, which is not flooring for negatives.
fdiv(int a, int b) =>
    int(math.floor(a / b))

// Truncating division for ints, i.e. toward zero. Java's integer / behaves
// this way, and period_between below has to match it exactly to reproduce
// java.time's Period.between.
tdiv(int a, int b) =>
    int(a / b)

// --------------------------------------------------------- weekday codes --

// @function        Numeric code for a weekday, 0 = Sunday through 6 = Saturday.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @returns         Integer code in [0, 6].
export method to_int(Weekday this) =>
    int out = 0
    switch this
        Weekday.SUNDAY => out := 0
        Weekday.MONDAY => out := 1
        Weekday.TUESDAY => out := 2
        Weekday.WEDNESDAY => out := 3
        Weekday.THURSDAY => out := 4
        Weekday.FRIDAY => out := 5
        Weekday.SATURDAY => out := 6
    out

// @function        ISO-8601 numbering for this weekday, 1 = Monday through 7 = Sunday. One of three numbering schemes carried by this type: to_int() is 0-based Sunday-first, to_pine_dow() is Pine's 1-based Sunday-first, and this is ISO's 1-based Monday-first. All three live on the enum so a signature never has to say which scheme a bare int is in.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @returns         ISO weekday in [1, 7].
export method to_iso_dow(Weekday this) =>
    int d = this.to_int()
    int out = d
    if d == 0
        out := 7
    out

// @function        Weekday for a numeric code, 0 = Sunday through 6 = Saturday.
// @param n         Integer code; values outside [0, 6] wrap by non-negative modulo rather than erroring, so arithmetic on codes is safe.
// @returns         The corresponding Weekday.
export weekday_from_int(int n) =>
    Weekday out = Weekday.SUNDAY
    switch pmod(n, 7)
        0 => out := Weekday.SUNDAY
        1 => out := Weekday.MONDAY
        2 => out := Weekday.TUESDAY
        3 => out := Weekday.WEDNESDAY
        4 => out := Weekday.THURSDAY
        5 => out := Weekday.FRIDAY
        6 => out := Weekday.SATURDAY
    out

// Inverse of to_iso_dow, kept private: only parse_iso reads an ISO weekday
// digit, which does not justify a third exported converter next to
// weekday_from_int and weekday_from_pine_dow. ISO 7 is Sunday, 0 in this
// library's numbering, so the modulo does the whole job.
iso_dow_to_weekday(int n) =>
    weekday_from_int(n % 7)

// ----------------------------------------------------------- civil core --

// @function        Days elapsed from 1970-01-01 to a civil date. Hinnant's days_from_civil: exact for any proleptic Gregorian date, no loops or tables.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @returns         Signed day count relative to the Unix epoch. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12] or Day outside [1, 31]. It does not check Day against the length of that month: this is the arithmetic floor of the library, and 31 February is caught one layer up by is_valid_date and new_datetime.
export days_from_civil(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("days_from_civil: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    if Day < 1 or Day > 31
        runtime.error("days_from_civil: Day must be in [1, 31], got " + str.tostring(Day))
    int y = Year
    if Month <= 2
        y := Year - 1
    int era = fdiv(y, ERA_YEARS)
    int yoe = y - era * ERA_YEARS
    int mshift = Month + 9
    if Month > 2
        mshift := Month - 3
    int doy = int((MONTH_C * mshift + 2) / 5) + Day - 1
    int doe = yoe * 365 + int(yoe / 4) - int(yoe / 100) + doy
    era * ERA_DAYS + doe - SHIFT_DAYS

// @function        Whether a year is a leap year in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         true when the year has 366 days.
export is_leap_year(int Year) =>
    Year % 4 == 0 and (Year % 100 != 0 or Year % 400 == 0)

// @function        Number of days in a given month.
// @param Year      Calendar year (needed for February).
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @returns         28, 29, 30 or 31. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12]; there is no month 13 to have a length.
export days_in_month(int Year, int Month) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("days_in_month: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    int out = 31
    if Month == 4 or Month == 6 or Month == 9 or Month == 11
        out := 30
    if Month == 2
        out := 28
        if is_leap_year(Year)
            out := 29
    out

// @function        Number of days in a given year.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         365, or 366 in a leap year.
export days_in_year(int Year) =>
    int out = 365
    if is_leap_year(Year)
        out := 366
    out

// @function        Whether a civil moment exists, without raising. This is the exact predicate new_datetime enforces, time-of-day fields included: the trailing parameters exist so that anything the constructor can refuse can be tested here first. The constructors raise because a wrong date is almost always a bug; use this form, or try_new_datetime, when the input is untrusted and a refusal is expected.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year.
// @param Day       Day of month.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0, so a three-argument call is still a pure date question.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @returns         true when the moment is real.
export is_valid_date(int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0) =>
    bool out = false
    // Nested rather than one chained `and`: days_in_month RAISES on a month
    // outside 1-12, so the month test has to be a gate rather than a conjunct.
    if Month >= 1 and Month <= 12
        if Day >= 1 and Day <= days_in_month(Year, Month)
            out := Hour >= 0 and Hour <= 23 and Minute >= 0 and Minute <= 59 and Second >= 0 and Second <= 59 and MS >= 0 and MS <= 999
    out

// @function        Day of week for a civil date, derived from the same civil algorithm as every other function here.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @returns         The Weekday.
export date_to_weekday(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    weekday_from_int(days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day) + EPOCH_DOW)

// @function        The weekday of this date, computed from the calendar on every call. There is no cached weekday field to go stale, so the answer is correct however the record was built.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         The Weekday.
export method weekday(DateTime this) =>
    date_to_weekday(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)

// @function        Convert a civil date-time to a Unix timestamp. Inverse of unix_to_date.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @param utc       Offset from UTC in hours that the supplied civil time is expressed in. Default 0.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds. RAISES when Hour is outside [0, 23], Minute or Second outside [0, 59], MS outside [0, 999], or when Month and Day are ones days_from_civil refuses. Like that function it does not check Day against the length of its month, so this converts 31 February rather than rejecting it; new_datetime is the validating way in.
export date_to_unix(int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0, float utc = 0.0) =>
    if Hour < 0 or Hour > 23
        runtime.error("date_to_unix: Hour must be in [0, 23], got " + str.tostring(Hour))
    if Minute < 0 or Minute > 59
        runtime.error("date_to_unix: Minute must be in [0, 59], got " + str.tostring(Minute))
    if Second < 0 or Second > 59
        runtime.error("date_to_unix: Second must be in [0, 59], got " + str.tostring(Second))
    if MS < 0 or MS > 999
        runtime.error("date_to_unix: MS must be in [0, 999], got " + str.tostring(MS))
    days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day) * MS_DAY - int(utc * MS_HOUR) + Hour * MS_HOUR + Minute * MS_MINUTE + Second * MS_SECOND + MS

// @function        Convert a Unix timestamp to a civil date-time. Inverse of date_to_unix. Hinnant's civil_from_days for the date part.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param utc       Offset from UTC in hours to express the result in. Default 0.
// @returns         A DateTime carrying the civil fields, the offset used, and the day of week.
export unix_to_date(int unix_ms, float utc = 0.0) =>
    int shifted = unix_ms + int(utc * MS_HOUR)
    int raw_days = fdiv(shifted, MS_DAY)
    int rem = shifted - raw_days * MS_DAY

    int Hour = int(rem / MS_HOUR)
    rem := rem - Hour * MS_HOUR
    int Minute = int(rem / MS_MINUTE)
    rem := rem - Minute * MS_MINUTE
    int Second = int(rem / MS_SECOND)
    rem := rem - Second * MS_SECOND

    int dse = raw_days + SHIFT_DAYS
    int era = fdiv(dse, ERA_DAYS)
    int doe = dse - era * ERA_DAYS
    int yoe = int((doe - int(doe / QUAD_DAYS) + int(doe / CENT_DAYS) - int(doe / ERA_DAYS_M1)) / 365)
    int doy = doe - (365 * yoe + int(yoe / 4) - int(yoe / 100))
    int mp = int((5 * doy + 2) / MONTH_C)
    int Day = doy - int((MONTH_C * mp + 2) / 5) + 1
    int Month = mp - 9
    if mp < 10
        Month := mp + 3
    int Year = yoe + era * ERA_YEARS
    if Month <= 2
        Year := Year + 1

    DateTime.new(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, rem, utc)

// @function        Build a DateTime from civil fields. RAISES on fields that do not describe a real moment: 31 February is neither clamped, which the caller did not ask for, nor na, which would only surface the mistake at some unrelated call site downstream. Use try_new_datetime for untrusted input, or a wither with Overflow.CONSTRAIN to clamp instead.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1 to the length of that month.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @param utc       Offset from UTC in hours the fields are expressed in. Default 0.
// @returns         A populated DateTime. RAISES when the fields do not describe a real moment; is_valid_date is the predicate, so 31 February and hour 24 are both refused. try_new_datetime is the non-raising form.
export new_datetime(int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0, float utc = 0.0) =>
    // One call, not two branches: is_valid_date must answer for everything
    // this constructor refuses, so the predicate and the constructor cannot
    // drift apart.
    if not is_valid_date(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS)
        runtime.error("new_datetime: " + str.tostring(Year) + "-" + str.tostring(Month) + "-" + str.tostring(Day) + "T" + str.tostring(Hour) + ":" + str.tostring(Minute) + ":" + str.tostring(Second) + "." + str.tostring(MS) + " is not a real moment")
    DateTime.new(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, utc)

// @function        The Unix timestamp this DateTime denotes, using the offset it carries.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
export method to_unix(DateTime this) =>
    date_to_unix(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC)

// ------------------------------------------------------------- adjusters --

// @function        Forward distance in days from this weekday to another, never negative. A method so the direction reads off the call: `friday.days_until(monday)`.
// @param this      The starting weekday.
// @param other     The target weekday.
// @returns         Days to advance from this to reach other, in [0, 6]. Zero when they are the same day: a week later is not what "until" means here.
export method days_until(Weekday this, Weekday other) =>
    pmod(other.to_int() - this.to_int(), 7)

// @function        Day of month of the nth given weekday, following java.time's dayOfWeekInMonth semantics.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param dow       Weekday wanted.
// @param nth       Positive counts from the start of the month (1 = first); negative counts back from the end (-1 = last). Zero is invalid.
// @returns         Day of month, or na when that occurrence does not exist in the month (e.g. a fifth Friday that the month does not contain). RAISES when nth is 0, which names no occurrence: an invalid argument, not a missing date.
export nth_weekday_of_month(int Year, int Month, Weekday dow, int nth) =>
    if nth == 0
        runtime.error("nth_weekday_of_month: nth must be non-zero; 1 = first, -1 = last")
    int last = days_in_month(Year, Month)
    int result = na
    if nth > 0
        Weekday first_dow = date_to_weekday(Year, Month, 1)
        int day = 1 + first_dow.days_until(dow) + (nth - 1) * 7
        if day <= last
            result := day
    else
        Weekday last_dow = date_to_weekday(Year, Month, last)
        int day = last - dow.days_until(last_dow) + (nth + 1) * 7
        if day >= 1
            result := day
    result

// ------------------------------------------------------ ordinal / fields --

// @function        Day of the year, 1 for 1 January.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         Ordinal day in [1, 366].
export method day_of_year(DateTime this) =>
    days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day) - days_from_civil(this.Year, 1, 1) + 1

// @function        Civil date for an ordinal day of the year. Inverse of day_of_year. Takes loose ints because it builds a date; see the calling convention in the header.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Doy       Ordinal day in [1, 366].
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight UTC on that date. RAISES when Doy is outside [1, days_in_year(Year)]; a 366th day of a common year is a caller bug.
export date_from_day_of_year(int Year, int Doy) =>
    if Doy < 1 or Doy > days_in_year(Year)
        runtime.error("date_from_day_of_year: Doy must be in [1, " + str.tostring(days_in_year(Year)) + "] for " + str.tostring(Year) + ", got " + str.tostring(Doy))
    unix_to_date((days_from_civil(Year, 1, 1) + Doy - 1) * MS_DAY)

// @function        Calendar quarter containing a month, or the fiscal quarter, when the fiscal year begins in some other month.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param fiscal_start Month the fiscal year begins in, 1-12. Default 1, which makes this the plain calendar quarter every internal caller relies on. Pass 4 for an April fiscal year (Japan, the UK): April answers 1 and March answers 4. Pass 10 for the US federal October year.
// @returns         Quarter in [1, 4]. RAISES when Month or fiscal_start is outside [1, 12]; a 13th month has no quarter.
export quarter(int Month, int fiscal_start = 1) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("quarter: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    if fiscal_start < 1 or fiscal_start > 12
        runtime.error("quarter: fiscal_start must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(fiscal_start))
    fdiv(pmod(Month - fiscal_start, 12), 3) + 1

// @function        Which week-row of its month this date falls in, where week 1 is the possibly-partial week containing the 1st. The answer depends on which day begins a week: the 9th of a month can sit in week 2 or week 3 of that same month, so the convention is a parameter rather than an assumption.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param week_start Which day the week begins on. Default MONDAY, the ISO convention.
// @returns         Week of month in [1, 6].
export method week_of_month(DateTime this, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    int lead = week_start.days_until(date_to_weekday(this.Year, this.Month, 1))
    fdiv(lead + this.Day - 1, 7) + 1

// @function        Which occurrence of its own weekday this date is within its month: the 3rd Friday answers 3. The inverse of nth_weekday_of_month, and unlike week_of_month it needs no week-start convention: it counts only days sharing this date's weekday, so where a week begins cannot matter.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         Ordinal in [1, 5]. Inverse in the exact sense that nth_weekday_of_month(Year, Month, this.weekday(), the answer) lands back on this day.
export method weekday_ordinal_in_month(DateTime this) =>
    fdiv(this.Day - 1, 7) + 1

// Number of ISO weeks in an ISO week-based year: 53 when the year starts on a
// Thursday, or is a leap year starting on a Wednesday. Expressed by the
// standard p(y) dominical-letter test rather than by counting.
iso_weeks_in_year(int Y) =>
    int a = pmod(Y + fdiv(Y, 4) - fdiv(Y, 100) + fdiv(Y, 400), 7)
    int b = pmod((Y - 1) + fdiv(Y - 1, 4) - fdiv(Y - 1, 100) + fdiv(Y - 1, 400), 7)
    int out = 52
    if a == 4 or b == 3
        out := 53
    out

// @function        ISO-8601 week number. Week 1 is the week containing the first Thursday of the year, so early January can belong to week 52 or 53 of the previous week-based year. Pair this with iso_week_year, never with the calendar year.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         ISO week number in [1, 53].
export method iso_week(DateTime this) =>
    int w = fdiv(this.day_of_year() - this.weekday().to_iso_dow() + 10, 7)
    int out = w
    if w < 1
        out := iso_weeks_in_year(this.Year - 1)
    if w > iso_weeks_in_year(this.Year)
        out := 1
    out

// @function        ISO-8601 week-based year, which is not always the calendar year: 2027-01-01 belongs to week-year 2026. Getting this wrong is exactly the "YYYY versus yyyy" formatting bug.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         ISO week-based year.
export method iso_week_year(DateTime this) =>
    int w = fdiv(this.day_of_year() - this.weekday().to_iso_dow() + 10, 7)
    int out = this.Year
    if w < 1
        out := this.Year - 1
    if w > iso_weeks_in_year(this.Year)
        out := this.Year + 1
    out

// @function        Civil date for an ISO-8601 week date. Inverse of the iso_week / iso_week_year / Weekday.to_iso_dow triple. Takes loose ints because it builds a date.
// @param WeekYear  ISO week-based year.
// @param Week      ISO week number in [1, 53].
// @param dow       Which day of that week. Default MONDAY, the first day of an ISO week. A Weekday rather than the raw ISO digit, so 0 and 8 are unrepresentable instead of runtime errors.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight UTC on that date. RAISES when Week is outside [1, iso_weeks_in_year(WeekYear)]: the bound is that week-year's own length, 52 or 53, not a flat 53.
export date_from_iso_week(int WeekYear, int Week, Weekday dow = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    if Week < 1 or Week > iso_weeks_in_year(WeekYear)
        runtime.error("date_from_iso_week: Week must be in [1, " + str.tostring(iso_weeks_in_year(WeekYear)) + "] for week-year " + str.tostring(WeekYear) + ", got " + str.tostring(Week))
    int jan4 = days_from_civil(WeekYear, 1, 4)
    int week1_monday = jan4 - (date_to_weekday(WeekYear, 1, 4).to_iso_dow() - 1)
    unix_to_date((week1_monday + (Week - 1) * 7 + (dow.to_iso_dow() - 1)) * MS_DAY)

// ------------------------------------------------- arithmetic: civil time --
// These move the calendar and preserve wall-clock time. See the header note on
// why they are named apart from the exact-time family below.

// @function        Add whole calendar days, preserving wall-clock time of day.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Days to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime.
export method plus_days(DateTime this, int n) =>
    DateTime d = unix_to_date((days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day) + n) * MS_DAY)
    new_datetime(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC)

// @function        Add whole calendar weeks, preserving wall-clock time of day.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Weeks to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime.
export method plus_weeks(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_days(n * 7)

// @function        Add whole calendar months, following java.time: the day of month is clamped to the length of the target month, so 31 January plus one month is 28 or 29 February. Not commutative with plus_days: adding a month then a day differs from adding a day then a month.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Months to add; may be negative.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method plus_months(DateTime this, int n, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    int total = this.Year * 12 + (this.Month - 1) + n
    int ny = fdiv(total, 12)
    int nm = pmod(total, 12) + 1
    int dim = days_in_month(ny, nm)
    int nd = this.Day
    DateTime out = na
    if nd <= dim
        out := new_datetime(ny, nm, nd, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC)
    else
        if ovf == Overflow.CONSTRAIN
            out := new_datetime(ny, nm, dim, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC)
    out

// @function        Add whole calendar years, with the same clamping rule as plus_months: 29 February plus one year is 28 February.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Years to add; may be negative.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method plus_years(DateTime this, int n, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    this.plus_months(n * 12, ovf)

// @function        Add a Period. Months are applied before days, matching java.time; the two orders can land on different dates.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param p         The Period to add.
// @param ovf       What to do when the month step lands on a day that does not exist. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method plus_period(DateTime this, Period p, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    DateTime m = this.plus_months(p.Years * 12 + p.Months, ovf)
    DateTime out = na
    if not na(m)
        out := m.plus_days(p.Days)
    out

// The minus_* family. Each delegates to its plus_* twin with the sign
// flipped, and carries no logic of its own, so the two directions can never
// drift apart. minus_period alone lives further down the file, after
// Period.negated, because Pine requires a definition to precede its use.

// @function        Subtract whole calendar days, preserving wall-clock time of day. Delegates to plus_days with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Days to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime.
export method minus_days(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_days(-n)

// @function        Subtract whole calendar weeks, preserving wall-clock time of day. Delegates to plus_weeks with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Weeks to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime.
export method minus_weeks(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_weeks(-n)

// @function        Subtract whole calendar months, with plus_months' clamping rule: 31 March minus one month is 28 or 29 February. Delegates to plus_months with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Months to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method minus_months(DateTime this, int n, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    this.plus_months(-n, ovf)

// @function        Subtract whole calendar years, with the same clamping rule: 29 February minus one year is 28 February. Delegates to plus_years with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Years to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method minus_years(DateTime this, int n, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    this.plus_years(-n, ovf)

// ------------------------------------------------- arithmetic: exact time --
// These move the instant. Under a fixed offset they are plain addition; across
// a real daylight-saving transition they are what you want when you mean
// "24 hours later" rather than "tomorrow at the same time".

// @function        Add milliseconds to the instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Milliseconds to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method plus_ms(DateTime this, int n) =>
    unix_to_date(this.to_unix() + n, this.UTC)

// @function        Add seconds to the instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Seconds to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method plus_seconds(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_ms(n * MS_SECOND)

// @function        Add minutes to the instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Minutes to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method plus_minutes(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_ms(n * MS_MINUTE)

// @function        Add hours to the instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Hours to add; may be negative.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method plus_hours(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_ms(n * MS_HOUR)

// @function        Subtract milliseconds from the instant. Delegates to plus_ms with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Milliseconds to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method minus_ms(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_ms(-n)

// @function        Subtract seconds from the instant. Delegates to plus_seconds with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Seconds to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method minus_seconds(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_seconds(-n)

// @function        Subtract minutes from the instant. Delegates to plus_minutes with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Minutes to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method minus_minutes(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_minutes(-n)

// @function        Subtract hours from the instant. Delegates to plus_hours with -n.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param n         Hours to subtract; a negative n adds.
// @returns         A new DateTime in the same offset.
export method minus_hours(DateTime this, int n) =>
    this.plus_hours(-n)

// ------------------------------------------------------------- differences --

// @function        Whole calendar days from this date to another, ignoring time of day.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @returns         Signed day count; negative when other is earlier.
export method days_between(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    days_from_civil(other.Year, other.Month, other.Day) - days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)

// @function        Whole calendar months from this date to another, in the sense of java.time's ChronoUnit.MONTHS.between: a partial month does not count. Note the deliberate asymmetry with plus_months: months_between(31 Jan, 29 Feb) is 0, yet 31 January plus one month clamps to 29 February. Clamping makes plus_months non-injective (29 and 31 January both land on 29 February), so the two are not inverses at a clamped boundary. This matches java.time and is inherent to clamped month arithmetic.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @returns         Signed whole-month count.
export method months_between(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    int total = (other.Year * 12 + other.Month - 1) - (this.Year * 12 + this.Month - 1)
    int dd = other.Day - this.Day
    if total > 0 and dd < 0
        total := total - 1
    if total < 0 and dd > 0
        total := total + 1
    total

// @function        The Period between two dates, following java.time's Period.between: whole months first, then the day remainder, with years and months truncated toward zero so every component carries the same sign.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @returns         A Period p for which this.plus_period(p) always lands on other's date.
export method period_between(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    int total = (other.Year * 12 + other.Month - 1) - (this.Year * 12 + this.Month - 1)
    int dd = other.Day - this.Day
    if total > 0 and dd < 0
        total := total - 1
    if total < 0 and dd > 0
        total := total + 1
    // Deliberate deviation from java.time, the one place this library does
    // not copy it. java.time recomputes the day remainder from the clamped
    // intermediate date in only one of its two branches, so when a month step
    // clamps in the other branch its Period.between does not round-trip:
    // 1971-10-29 to 1969-02-01 yields P-2Y-8M-28D, which replays to
    // 1969-01-31, a day short of the end date, despite the javadoc's promise
    // that start.plus(period) equals end. Recomputing unconditionally from
    // where the month step landed costs one extra conversion and makes the
    // round-trip hold everywhere.
    DateTime calc = this.plus_months(total)
    dd := days_from_civil(other.Year, other.Month, other.Day) - days_from_civil(calc.Year, calc.Month, calc.Day)
    // Truncating, not flooring: the sign of every component must match, which
    // is what integer division toward zero gives and floor division does not.
    Period.new(tdiv(total, 12), total % 12, dd)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ordering --

// @function        Compare two date-times as instants, so differing offsets compare correctly.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         -1 when this is earlier, 0 when they are the same instant, 1 when this is later.
export method compare(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    int ta = this.to_unix()
    int tb = other.to_unix()
    int out = 0
    if ta < tb
        out := -1
    if ta > tb
        out := 1
    out

// @function        Whether this date-time is strictly earlier than another, as instants.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         true when this is earlier.
export method is_before(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    this.compare(other) == -1

// @function        Whether this date-time is strictly later than another, as instants.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         true when this is later.
export method is_after(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    this.compare(other) == 1

// @function        The earlier of two date-times, as instants.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         Whichever is earlier; this when they are equal.
export method earlier(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    DateTime out = this
    if other.is_before(this)
        out := other
    out

// @function        The later of two date-times, as instants.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         Whichever is later; this when they are equal.
export method later(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    DateTime out = this
    if other.is_after(this)
        out := other
    out

// ------------------------------------------------- truncation and rounding --

// Rank the units so truncation can zero fields by threshold rather than by a
// nine-branch switch that repeats itself.
unit_rank(TimeUnit u) =>
    int out = 0
    switch u
        TimeUnit.MS => out := 0
        TimeUnit.SECOND => out := 1
        TimeUnit.MINUTE => out := 2
        TimeUnit.HOUR => out := 3
        TimeUnit.DAY => out := 4
        TimeUnit.WEEK => out := 5
        TimeUnit.MONTH => out := 6
        TimeUnit.QUARTER => out := 7
        TimeUnit.YEAR => out := 8
    out

// @function        The first instant of the unit containing this date-time.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param unit      The unit to truncate to.
// @param week_start Which day a week begins on, used only for TimeUnit.WEEK. Default MONDAY, the ISO-8601 convention; pass SUNDAY for the common US one.
// @returns         A new DateTime at the start of the unit, in the same offset the receiver carried. For a fixed-offset record that is the only reading available, but it means start_of(DAY) is not necessarily local midnight in a zone that changed offset that day. Use today() for that, or re-resolve through Zone.to_unix.
export method start_of(DateTime this, TimeUnit unit, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    int rank = unit_rank(unit)
    int Y = this.Year
    int M = this.Month
    int D = this.Day
    int h = this.Hour
    int mi = this.Minute
    int s = this.Second
    int ms = this.MS
    if rank >= 1
        ms := 0
    if rank >= 2
        s := 0
    if rank >= 3
        mi := 0
    if rank >= 4
        h := 0
    if rank == 5
        int back = pmod(date_to_weekday(Y, M, D).to_int() - week_start.to_int(), 7)
        DateTime b = unix_to_date((days_from_civil(Y, M, D) - back) * MS_DAY)
        Y := b.Year
        M := b.Month
        D := b.Day
    if rank >= 6
        D := 1
    if rank == 7
        M := (quarter(M) - 1) * 3 + 1
    if rank == 8
        M := 1
    new_datetime(Y, M, D, h, mi, s, ms, this.UTC)

// @function        The last instant of the unit containing this date-time, i.e. one millisecond before the next unit begins.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param unit      The unit.
// @param week_start Which day a week begins on, used only for TimeUnit.WEEK. Default MONDAY.
// @returns         A new DateTime at the end of the unit, in the same offset the receiver carried; see start_of for what that means across a daylight-saving transition.
export method end_of(DateTime this, TimeUnit unit, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    DateTime s0 = this.start_of(unit, week_start)
    DateTime nxt = s0
    switch unit
        TimeUnit.MS => nxt := s0.plus_ms(1)
        TimeUnit.SECOND => nxt := s0.plus_seconds(1)
        TimeUnit.MINUTE => nxt := s0.plus_minutes(1)
        TimeUnit.HOUR => nxt := s0.plus_hours(1)
        TimeUnit.DAY => nxt := s0.plus_days(1)
        TimeUnit.WEEK => nxt := s0.plus_weeks(1)
        TimeUnit.MONTH => nxt := s0.plus_months(1)
        TimeUnit.QUARTER => nxt := s0.plus_months(3)
        TimeUnit.YEAR => nxt := s0.plus_years(1)
    nxt.plus_ms(-1)

// @function        Round this date-time to a unit boundary. Modes are applied to elapsed time since the Unix epoch, so TRUNC means toward 1970 and HALF_EXPAND breaks exact ties away from it.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param unit      The unit to round to.
// @param mode      The rounding mode. Default FLOOR.
// @param week_start Which day a week begins on, used only for TimeUnit.WEEK. Default MONDAY, matching start_of and end_of: without it a Sunday-week user could floor and ceil to a Sunday week but not round to one.
// @returns         A new DateTime on a unit boundary, in the same offset the receiver carried; see start_of for what that means across a daylight-saving transition.
export method round_to(DateTime this, TimeUnit unit, RoundMode mode = RoundMode.FLOOR, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    DateTime lo = this.start_of(unit, week_start)
    DateTime hi = this.end_of(unit, week_start).plus_ms(1)
    int t = this.to_unix()
    int tlo = lo.to_unix()
    int thi = hi.to_unix()
    DateTime out = lo
    switch mode
        RoundMode.FLOOR => out := lo
        RoundMode.CEIL =>
            out := hi
            if t == tlo
                out := lo
        RoundMode.TRUNC =>
            out := lo
            if t < 0 and t != tlo
                out := hi
        RoundMode.HALF_EXPAND =>
            int down = t - tlo
            int up = thi - t
            out := lo
            if down > up
                out := hi
            if down == up
                out := hi
                if t < 0
                    out := lo
    out

// -------------------------------------------------------------- intervals --

// @function        Whether an instant falls inside this interval. The interval is half-open, [FromMS, ToMS), so back-to-back intervals never both claim the same instant.
// @param this      The Interval.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @returns         true when FromMS <= unix_ms < ToMS.
export method contains(Interval this, int unix_ms) =>
    unix_ms >= this.FromMS and unix_ms < this.ToMS

// @function        Whether two intervals share any instant. Half-open, so intervals that merely touch do not overlap.
// @param this      The first Interval.
// @param other     The second Interval.
// @returns         true when they intersect.
export method overlaps(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    this.FromMS < other.ToMS and other.FromMS < this.ToMS

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- easter --

// @function        Easter Sunday, by the anonymous Gregorian computus (Meeus / Jones / Butcher).
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight UTC on Easter Sunday of that year.
export easter_sunday(int Year) =>
    int a = Year % 19
    int b = int(Year / 100)
    int c = Year % 100
    int d = int(b / 4)
    int e = b % 4
    int f = int((b + 8) / 25)
    int g = int((b - f + 1) / 3)
    int h = (19 * a + b - d - g + 15) % 30
    int i = int(c / 4)
    int k = c % 4
    int l = (32 + 2 * e + 2 * i - h - k) % 7
    int m = int((a + 11 * h + 22 * l) / 451)
    // `month` and `day` are Pine built-ins; these locals are deliberately named
    // e_month / e_day so they cannot shadow them.
    int e_month = int((h + l - 7 * m + 114) / 31)
    int e_day = ((h + l - 7 * m + 114) % 31) + 1
    new_datetime(Year, e_month, e_day)

// @function        Good Friday, the Friday before Easter Sunday.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight UTC on Good Friday of that year.
export good_friday(int Year) =>
    easter_sunday(Year).plus_days(-2)

// ----------------------------------------------------------- zone engine --
// One engine, rules as data. The built-ins can tell you the offset at an
// instant; nothing built in can tell you when a transition happens. That is
// what dst_start / dst_end are for.

// US rule, era-aware: the Energy Policy Act 2005 rule from 2007, the 1987-2006
// rule, and the 1976-1986 rule before that.
us_rule(int Year) =>
    int sm = 4
    int snth = -1
    int em = 10
    int enth = -1
    if Year >= 2007
        sm := 3
        snth := 2
        em := 11
        enth := 1
    else if Year >= 1987
        snth := 1
    DstRule.new(sm, Weekday.SUNDAY, snth, 2, false, em, Weekday.SUNDAY, enth, 2, false, 1.0)

// EU rule, harmonised since 1996: last Sunday of March to last Sunday of
// October, both at 01:00 UTC. The whole union switches at the same instant,
// not at the same local time, which is what the AtUTC flag expresses.
eu_rule() =>
    DstRule.new(3, Weekday.SUNDAY, -1, 1, true, 10, Weekday.SUNDAY, -1, 1, true, 1.0)

// Australian rule since 2008: first Sunday of October at 02:00 standard, to
// first Sunday of April at 03:00 daylight. The start month is after the end
// month: the southern-hemisphere case offset_at_rule handles explicitly.
au_rule() =>
    DstRule.new(10, Weekday.SUNDAY, 1, 2, false, 4, Weekday.SUNDAY, 1, 3, false, 1.0)

// A zone with no daylight saving: ShiftHours zero short-circuits everything.
no_dst_rule() =>
    DstRule.new(1, Weekday.SUNDAY, 1, 0, false, 1, Weekday.SUNDAY, 1, 0, false, 0.0)

// @function        The standard, i.e. winter, offset of a zone in hours.
// @param this      The zone.
// @returns         Offset from UTC in hours, ignoring daylight saving. RAISES on a Zone the switch does not handle, a library bug rather than a caller one. The switch is total so a new member cannot silently read as UTC.
export method std_offset(Zone this) =>
    // Seeded na and closed with an error arm, not seeded 0.0: a 0.0 seed
    // would let an unhandled Zone read as UTC, the worst available failure
    // here.
    float out = na
    switch this
        Zone.UTC => out := 0.0
        Zone.NEW_YORK => out := -5.0
        Zone.CHICAGO => out := -6.0
        Zone.LONDON => out := 0.0
        Zone.FRANKFURT => out := 1.0
        Zone.SYDNEY => out := 10.0
        Zone.TORONTO => out := -5.0
        Zone.SHANGHAI => out := 8.0
        Zone.TOKYO => out := 9.0
        Zone.HONG_KONG => out := 8.0
        Zone.SINGAPORE => out := 8.0
        // Not every standard offset is a whole hour: India is +5:30.
        Zone.MUMBAI => out := 5.5
        Zone.DUBAI => out := 4.0
        => runtime.error("std_offset: unhandled Zone. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        The daylight-saving rule in force for a zone in a given year. Rules change by legislation, so this takes a year rather than being a constant.
// @param this      The zone.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         The DstRule; one with ShiftHours zero when the zone does not observe daylight saving. RAISES on a Zone the switch does not handle, a library bug. A no_dst_rule() seed would instead claim that an unhandled zone observes no daylight saving.
export method rule(Zone this, int Year) =>
    DstRule out = na
    switch this
        // UTC and Tokyo are spelled out rather than left to a no_dst_rule()
        // seed, so that adding a Zone and forgetting this function is an
        // error rather than a claim that the new zone has no daylight saving.
        Zone.UTC => out := no_dst_rule()
        Zone.TOKYO => out := no_dst_rule()
        Zone.NEW_YORK => out := us_rule(Year)
        Zone.CHICAGO => out := us_rule(Year)
        Zone.LONDON => out := eu_rule()
        Zone.FRANKFURT => out := eu_rule()
        Zone.SYDNEY => out := au_rule()
        // Toronto has kept step with New York since 1976; the two zones'
        // offsets differ nowhere in the tzdb after that, so they share a rule
        // rather than carrying a second copy of it.
        Zone.TORONTO => out := us_rule(Year)
        Zone.SHANGHAI => out := no_dst_rule()
        // None of the four Asian and Gulf zones observes daylight saving
        // inside its rules_from window: Hong Kong last shifted in 1979,
        // Singapore, India and the UAE not at all in the modelled era.
        Zone.HONG_KONG => out := no_dst_rule()
        Zone.SINGAPORE => out := no_dst_rule()
        Zone.MUMBAI => out := no_dst_rule()
        Zone.DUBAI => out := no_dst_rule()
        => runtime.error("rule: unhandled Zone. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        The first year from which a zone's rules are modelled exactly. Before it, results are the oldest rule extrapolated backward and should not be trusted.
// @param this      The zone.
// @returns         Calendar year. RAISES on a Zone the switch does not handle, a library bug. A 1970 seed would instead assert exactness for a zone nobody has checked.
export method rules_from(Zone this) =>
    int out = na
    switch this
        // UTC has no rules to be exact about; Tokyo has observed no daylight
        // saving since 1952, so anything inside Unix time is exact.
        Zone.UTC => out := 1970
        Zone.TOKYO => out := 1970
        Zone.NEW_YORK => out := 1976
        Zone.CHICAGO => out := 1976
        Zone.LONDON => out := 1996
        Zone.FRANKFURT => out := 1996
        Zone.SYDNEY => out := 2008
        Zone.TORONTO => out := 1976
        // China observed daylight saving only from 1986 to 1991.
        Zone.SHANGHAI => out := 1992
        // Per the IANA tzdb: Hong Kong's last daylight
        // period ended 21 October 1979; Singapore moved from +7:30 to +8 at
        // the start of 1982; India has been +5:30 since 1945 and the UAE +4
        // since 1920, so like Tokyo their whole Unix era is exact.
        Zone.HONG_KONG => out := 1980
        Zone.SINGAPORE => out := 1982
        Zone.MUMBAI => out := 1970
        Zone.DUBAI => out := 1970
        => runtime.error("rules_from: unhandled Zone. This is a library bug")
    out

// The instant of one transition. Start times are quoted in local standard
// time and end times in local daylight time, an asymmetry that comes from
// the tzdb source, unless the rule says the hour is UTC.
rule_transition(int Year, DstRule r, bool is_start, float std) =>
    int mo = r.EndMonth
    Weekday dw = r.EndDow
    int nth = r.EndNth
    int at = r.EndAtHour
    bool at_utc = r.EndAtUTC
    float off = std + r.ShiftHours
    if is_start
        mo := r.StartMonth
        dw := r.StartDow
        nth := r.StartNth
        at := r.StartAtHour
        at_utc := r.StartAtUTC
        off := std
    if at_utc
        off := 0.0
    date_to_unix(Year, mo, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, mo, dw, nth), at, 0, 0, 0, off)

// @function        Offset in force at an instant under an arbitrary rule. This computes an offset and nothing more; it is not a way to run the rest of the library against a zone the Zone enum does not ship, because nothing else here accepts a DstRule.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param std_off   The zone's standard offset in hours.
// @param dr        The daylight-saving rule.
// @returns         Offset from UTC in hours at that instant.
export offset_at_rule(int unix_ms, float std_off, DstRule dr) =>
    float out = std_off
    if dr.ShiftHours != 0.0
        DateTime probe = unix_to_date(unix_ms, std_off)
        int s = rule_transition(probe.Year, dr, true, std_off)
        int e = rule_transition(probe.Year, dr, false, std_off)
        bool in_dst = unix_ms >= s and unix_ms < e
        if dr.StartMonth > dr.EndMonth
            // Southern hemisphere: daylight time spans the year boundary, so
            // the window is the complement of the northern test.
            in_dst := unix_ms >= s or unix_ms < e
        if in_dst
            out := std_off + dr.ShiftHours
    out

// @function        Offset from UTC in force in a zone at an instant, daylight saving applied.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         Offset in hours.
export offset_at(int unix_ms, Zone z) =>
    float std = z.std_offset()
    offset_at_rule(unix_ms, std, z.rule(unix_to_date(unix_ms, std).Year))

// @function        The instant daylight saving begins in a zone in a year. There is no built-in equivalent: Pine can tell you the offset at a timestamp, but not when it changes. No `_at` suffix: in offset_at, offset_at_rule and window_at, `_at` means "the value in force at an instant", and this takes a year and returns an instant.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when the zone has no daylight saving.
export dst_start(int Year, Zone z) =>
    DstRule r = z.rule(Year)
    int out = na
    if r.ShiftHours != 0.0
        out := rule_transition(Year, r, true, z.std_offset())
    out

// @function        The instant daylight saving ends in a zone in a year. For a southern-hemisphere zone this is EARLIER in the calendar year than dst_start, because the daylight period spans the year boundary.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when the zone has no daylight saving.
export dst_end(int Year, Zone z) =>
    DstRule r = z.rule(Year)
    int out = na
    if r.ShiftHours != 0.0
        out := rule_transition(Year, r, false, z.std_offset())
    out

// @function        Whether daylight saving is in force in a zone at an instant.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         true when the offset differs from the zone's standard offset.
export is_dst(int unix_ms, Zone z) =>
    offset_at(unix_ms, z) != z.std_offset()

// @function        Convert an instant to civil time in a zone, daylight saving applied.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         A DateTime whose UTC field carries the offset actually in force.
export unix_to_date_zone(int unix_ms, Zone z) =>
    unix_to_date(unix_ms, offset_at(unix_ms, z))

// The two candidate instants for a local civil time, one read as standard and
// one as daylight. Exactly one is normally self-consistent; in a spring-forward
// gap neither is, and in a fall-back overlap both are. This is the standard way
// to detect both conditions without a transition table.
local_map(int Y, int M, int D, int h, int mi, int s, int ms, Zone z) =>
    float std = z.std_offset()
    float shift = z.rule(Y).ShiftHours
    int t_std = date_to_unix(Y, M, D, h, mi, s, ms, std)
    int t_dst = date_to_unix(Y, M, D, h, mi, s, ms, std + shift)
    bool ok_std = offset_at(t_std, z) == std
    bool ok_dst = offset_at(t_dst, z) == std + shift
    [t_std, t_dst, ok_std, ok_dst, shift]

// Why the zone is the receiver: these three read civil fields as local time
// in a zone, which is the one thing a DateTime cannot express: a DateTime
// carries a fixed offset, and an operation that ignored that field would be a
// trap on the type whose job is to carry it. Putting the zone on the left
// puts "these fields are local to this zone" in the syntax rather than in a
// docstring. (The missing type is java.time's LocalDateTime; not worth
// introducing for three functions.)
//   The second reason is Pine's trailing-default rule. With the zone in
// argument position 8, every time field would have to be positional, so
// asking for midnight in New York would mean writing four literal zeros. On
// the receiver, the defaults are legal:
//   Zone.NEW_YORK.to_unix(2026, 8, 21)      // local midnight
// unix_to_date_zone stays a free function despite the asymmetry: everything
// in this file that takes an instant takes it first.


// @function        Whether a unit boundary in a zone falls between two instants: the "is this a new day?" test, answered against a real calendar instead of the chart's own session grid. Pass Pine's `time[1]` and `time`. Because the boundary is resolved in a named zone, this is how you detect a new Tokyo day on a chart quoted in New York, which a bar-index comparison cannot do.
// @param prev_ms   The earlier instant, Unix milliseconds. Pine's `time[1]`.
// @param cur_ms    The later instant, Unix milliseconds. Pine's `time`.
// @param unit      The unit whose boundary is being watched.
// @param z         The zone the boundary is resolved in. Default UTC.
// @param week_start Which day a week begins on, used only for TimeUnit.WEEK. Default MONDAY.
// @returns         true when the two instants sit in different units. Equal instants return false, and so does an na on either side: bool cannot hold na, so there is no third answer to give; guard the first bar with nz() or a bar_index test rather than reading false as "no boundary". The repeated hour of an autumn transition is one civil hour and reports no boundary: 01:30 happens twice in London that morning, and both truncate to the same 01:00.
export changed(int prev_ms, int cur_ms, TimeUnit unit, Zone z = Zone.UTC, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    bool out = false
    if not na(prev_ms) and not na(cur_ms)
        DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(prev_ms, z).start_of(unit, week_start)
        DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(cur_ms, z).start_of(unit, week_start)
        // Civil fields only, deliberately. Across a daylight-saving transition
        // the two records name the same local boundary while carrying
        // different offsets, and both equals() and an instant comparison would
        // read that as a change.
        out := a.Year != b.Year or a.Month != b.Month or a.Day != b.Day or a.Hour != b.Hour or a.Minute != b.Minute or a.Second != b.Second or a.MS != b.MS
    out

// @function        Whether a local civil time never happened, because a spring-forward transition skipped over it.
// @param this      The zone the civil fields are local to.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @returns         true when the local time does not exist.
export method is_gap(Zone this, int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0) =>
    [t_std, t_dst, ok_std, ok_dst, shift] = local_map(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, this)
    shift != 0.0 and not ok_std and not ok_dst

// @function        Whether a local civil time happened twice, because a fall-back transition repeated it.
// @param this      The zone the civil fields are local to.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @returns         true when the local time is ambiguous.
export method is_ambiguous(Zone this, int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0) =>
    [t_std, t_dst, ok_std, ok_dst, shift] = local_map(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, this)
    shift != 0.0 and ok_std and ok_dst

// @function        Convert a local civil time in this zone to an instant, resolving gaps and overlaps explicitly rather than silently. This is the zone-aware inverse of unix_to_date_zone; DateTime.to_unix is the fixed-offset one and answers a different question.
// @param this      The zone the civil fields are local to.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month, 1-31.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond of second, 0-999. Default 0.
// @param res       How to resolve a gap or an overlap. Default COMPATIBLE, matching java.time and Temporal.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when res is REJECT and the local time is impossible or ambiguous.
export method to_unix(Zone this, int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0, Resolver res = Resolver.COMPATIBLE) =>
    [t_std, t_dst, ok_std, ok_dst, shift] = local_map(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, this)
    int out = t_std
    if shift != 0.0
        if ok_std and not ok_dst
            out := t_std
        else if ok_dst and not ok_std
            out := t_dst
        else
            // Neither valid is a gap; both valid is an overlap. In both cases
            // the daylight reading is the earlier instant, because a larger
            // offset subtracts more from the same wall clock.
            switch res
                Resolver.EARLIER => out := t_dst
                Resolver.LATER => out := t_std
                Resolver.REJECT => out := na
                Resolver.COMPATIBLE =>
                    // Gap: move forward by the gap length. Overlap: take the
                    // first of the two. These are opposite candidates.
                    out := t_std
                    if ok_std and ok_dst
                        out := t_dst
    out

// ------------------------------------------------------ NYSE holiday rules --
// Private, and deliberately so: this is the rule half of the NYSE calendar,
// not the whole answer. is_holiday adds the dated closure table on top, so a
// caller reaching the rules directly would be told that 11 September 2001 and
// 29 October 2012 were ordinary trading days.
//
// It exists as a separate helper because monthly_expiry_day genuinely wants
// the rules alone: an option expiry rolls back off a scheduled holiday, which
// the exchange knew about when it listed the contract, and no listing rolls
// off a hurricane.

// The name of the holiday closing the NYSE by rule on a date, or na,
// including weekday observation of the fixed-date holidays. New Year's Day is
// the documented exception: a Saturday 1 January does not close the preceding
// 31 December. The name is the source of truth: nyse_rule_holiday below is
// defined as this being non-na, so the predicate and the name cannot
// disagree.
nyse_rule_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday dow = date_to_weekday(Year, Month, Day)
    bool weekend = dow == Weekday.SATURDAY or dow == Weekday.SUNDAY
    string out = na

    // Fixed-date holidays, observed Monday when they fall on Sunday and Friday
    // when they fall on Saturday. New Year's Day is the documented exception:
    // when 1 January falls on a Saturday the NYSE does not close the preceding
    // 31 December.
    if Month == 1 and Day == 1 and not weekend
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 1 and Day == 2 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 7 and Day == 4 and not weekend
        out := "Independence Day"
    if Month == 7 and Day == 3 and dow == Weekday.FRIDAY
        out := "Independence Day"
    if Month == 7 and Day == 5 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
        out := "Independence Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 25 and not weekend
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 24 and dow == Weekday.FRIDAY
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 26 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
        out := "Christmas Day"
    // Juneteenth, a market holiday from 2022.
    if Year >= 2022 and Month == 6
        if Day == 19 and not weekend
            out := "Juneteenth"
        if Day == 18 and dow == Weekday.FRIDAY
            out := "Juneteenth"
        if Day == 20 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
            out := "Juneteenth"

    // Floating holidays.
    if Month == 1 and Year >= 1998 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 1, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
        out := "Martin Luther King Jr. Day"
    if Month == 2 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 2, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
        out := "Washington's Birthday"
    if Month == 5 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, -1)
        out := "Memorial Day"
    if Month == 9 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 1)
        out := "Labor Day"
    if Month == 11 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 11, Weekday.THURSDAY, 4)
        out := "Thanksgiving Day"

    // Good Friday.
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"

    out

// The predicate form of the rule half of the NYSE calendar, derived from the
// name rather than restated, so there is one rule set and not two.
nyse_rule_holiday(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    not na(nyse_rule_holiday_name(Year, Month, Day))

// @function        Standard US equity monthly option expiry: the third Friday of the month, moved to the preceding Thursday when that Friday is a market holiday.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @returns         Day of month on which the monthly contract expires.
export monthly_expiry_day(int Year, int Month) =>
    int d = nth_weekday_of_month(Year, Month, Weekday.FRIDAY, 3)
    int out = d
    if nyse_rule_holiday(Year, Month, d)
        out := d - 1
    out

// @function        Unix timestamp of a standard US equity monthly option expiry at a chosen local close time.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the close. Default 16 (16:00 New York).
// @param z         The zone the close time is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK. A Zone rather than a `float utc`, because the New York close follows daylight-saving rules that no single fixed offset can express; in this file `float utc` always means a fixed offset.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, resolved through Resolver.COMPATIBLE. At the 16:00 default no US transition is reachable, so the resolver never fires.
export monthly_expiry(int Year, int Month, int Hour = 16, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    z.to_unix(Year, Month, monthly_expiry_day(Year, Month), Hour)

// ============================================================================
// VALUE-TYPE ERGONOMICS
//
// READ THIS BEFORE ASSIGNING TO A FIELD.
//
// 1. ALIASING: applies to every UDT here, without exception.
//    `DateTime b = a` does not copy, it aliases: `b.Month := 3` silently
//    changes `a` too, and the same holds for Period, Interval, Session and
//    DstRule. Use `.copy()`, or the withers below, which copy for you.
//
// 2. CONSTRAINED FIELDS: a short, closed list.
//    Most fields accept every value their type can hold, and assigning one on
//    an instance you own is safe. These are the exceptions, because other
//    functions read them as a promise:
//      DateTime .... Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS. Together they
//                    have to name a real moment. Set Month to 2 on a 31st and
//                    the record holds 31 February, a date the constructor
//                    would have refused, and every adjuster downstream
//                    inherits it. Use the withers, or new_datetime, or
//                    normalized() to repair a record that has already gone
//                    wrong.
//      Session ..... StartMin and EndMin must be minutes within a day, DayMask
//                    a 7-bit mask, and the break pair set together and ordered
//                    strictly inside the window. new_session enforces all of
//                    it; a negative mask makes day_allowed's modulo test
//                    nonsense, and a hand-set break can put a hole where the
//                    market trades.
//      Interval .... FromMS must not exceed ToMS. new_interval orders them.
//    DateTime.UTC, Session.Name/Tz/Cal, Period and DstRule in full carry no
//    cross-field promise, so any value of the right type is safe. EarlyClose
//    is the odd one out: meaningful only with a Cal to read closes from,
//    which is why new_session refuses the flag alone.
//
//    There is no derived weekday field to keep in sync: weekday() computes
//    from the calendar on every call and cannot go stale.
//
// 3. BUILDING ONE WITHOUT POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS.
//    Pine's `.new()` takes named arguments, and every field here has a
//    default, so a partially-specified record needs no wrapper function and no
//    long positional call:
//      Period.new(Months = 3)                  // three months, no years, no days
//      DstRule.new(StartMonth = 4, EndMonth = 9)
//      Interval.new(ToMS = t)
//    DateTime is the exception: prefer new_datetime, which validates the
//    field set as a whole.
//
// Also note: `==` does not compile on two UDTs in Pine: it is a type error,
// not reference equality, so equals() is the only way to compare two
// DateTimes, Periods or Intervals.
// ============================================================================

// Rebuild a DateTime from a full field set, clamping the day into the target
// month. Every wither routes through this so none of them can produce a record
// the constructor would have refused.
with_fields(int Y, int M, int D, int h, int mi, int s, int ms, float utc, Overflow ovf) =>
    DateTime out = na
    if M >= 1 and M <= 12 and D >= 1 and h >= 0 and h <= 23 and mi >= 0 and mi <= 59 and s >= 0 and s <= 59 and ms >= 0 and ms <= 999
        int dim = days_in_month(Y, M)
        if D <= dim
            out := new_datetime(Y, M, D, h, mi, s, ms, utc)
        else if ovf == Overflow.CONSTRAIN
            out := new_datetime(Y, M, dim, h, mi, s, ms, utc)
    out

// @function        A copy of this DateTime with the day clamped into the length of its month. Repairs a record whose fields were assigned directly, which is the only way this library can hold a date its constructor would have refused. Routes through the same repair as the withers.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         A new, self-consistent DateTime, or na when a field other than the day is out of range: a day past the end of its month clamps, but a Month of 13 is a typo, not an overflow, and is refused.
export method normalized(DateTime this) =>
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        Whether two date-times denote the same civil fields and the same offset. Distinct from compare, which asks whether they are the same instant: 12:00-05:00 and 17:00Z are equal as instants but not as records.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         true when every field matches.
export method equals(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    bool out = false
    if not na(this) and not na(other)
        out := this.Year == other.Year and this.Month == other.Month and this.Day == other.Day and this.Hour == other.Hour and this.Minute == other.Minute and this.Second == other.Second and this.MS == other.MS and this.UTC == other.UTC
    out

// @function        Whether two date-times are the same instant, regardless of the offset each is expressed in.
// @param this      The first DateTime.
// @param other     The second DateTime.
// @returns         true when they denote the same moment.
export method same_instant(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    this.compare(other) == 0

// ------------------------------------------------------------- withers --

// @function        A copy with the year replaced. The day is clamped, so 29 February in a leap year becomes 28 February in a common one.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Year      The new calendar year.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na under REJECT when the day does not exist.
export method with_year(DateTime this, int Year, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    with_fields(Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, ovf)

// @function        A copy with the month replaced, clamping the day into the new month.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Month     The new month, 1-12.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day does not exist in the target. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when the month is out of range or the day does not exist under REJECT.
export method with_month(DateTime this, int Month, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    with_fields(this.Year, Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, ovf)

// @function        A copy with the day of month replaced.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Day       The new day of month, 1 or greater.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day is past the end of this month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na. Overflow governs one direction only: a day past the end of the month clamps under CONSTRAIN and is na under REJECT, but a day below 1 is na under both, because it is not an overflow at all: nothing sensible to clamp it to.
export method with_day(DateTime this, int Day, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, ovf)

// The time withers RAISE rather than returning na, and Overflow does not
// apply: only the date withers have something to clamp to. Returning na for
// an hour of 25 would make a caller bug indistinguishable from "no such
// date", the distinction the error clauses in the header exist to keep.
with_time_check(string fn, string field, int v, int hi) =>
    bool bad = v < 0 or v > hi
    if bad
        runtime.error(fn + ": " + field + " must be in [0, " + str.tostring(hi) + "], got " + str.tostring(v))
    bad

// @function        A copy with the hour replaced.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Hour      The new hour, 0-23.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when the hour is out of range.
export method with_hour(DateTime this, int Hour) =>
    with_time_check("with_hour", "Hour", Hour, 23)
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy with the minute replaced.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Minute    The new minute, 0-59.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when the minute is out of range.
export method with_minute(DateTime this, int Minute) =>
    with_time_check("with_minute", "Minute", Minute, 59)
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy with the second replaced.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Second    The new second, 0-59.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when the second is out of range.
export method with_second(DateTime this, int Second) =>
    with_time_check("with_second", "Second", Second, 59)
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, Second, this.MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy with the millisecond replaced.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param MS        The new millisecond, 0-999.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when it is out of range.
export method with_ms(DateTime this, int MS) =>
    with_time_check("with_ms", "MS", MS, 999)
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy with the date replaced and the time of day kept.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Year      The new year.
// @param Month     The new month, 1-12.
// @param Day       The new day of month, 1 or greater.
// @param ovf       What to do when the day is past the end of the target month. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na. As in with_day, Overflow decides only what happens past the end of the month; a Month outside 1-12 or a Day below 1 is na whichever Overflow you pass.
export method with_date(DateTime this, int Year, int Month, int Day, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    with_fields(Year, Month, Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC, ovf)

// @function        A copy with the time of day replaced and the date kept.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Hour      The new hour, 0-23.
// @param Minute    The new minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    The new second, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        The new millisecond, 0-999. Default 0.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when any component is out of range.
export method with_time(DateTime this, int Hour, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0) =>
    with_time_check("with_time", "Hour", Hour, 23)
    with_time_check("with_time", "Minute", Minute, 59)
    with_time_check("with_time", "Second", Second, 59)
    with_time_check("with_time", "MS", MS, 999)
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, this.UTC, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy expressed in a different fixed offset, keeping the civil fields and therefore changing the instant. To keep the instant instead, use to_zone_same_instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param utc       The new offset from UTC in hours.
// @returns         A new DateTime denoting a different moment.
export method with_offset(DateTime this, float utc) =>
    with_fields(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, utc, Overflow.CONSTRAIN)

// @function        A copy moved to a given ordinal day of the year, keeping the time of day.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param Doy       Ordinal day, 1-366.
// @returns         A new DateTime. RAISES when the ordinal does not exist in this year: a 366th day of a common year is a caller bug, not a date the calendar declines to have.
export method with_day_of_year(DateTime this, int Doy) =>
    if Doy < 1 or Doy > days_in_year(this.Year)
        runtime.error("with_day_of_year: Doy must be in [1, " + str.tostring(days_in_year(this.Year)) + "] for " + str.tostring(this.Year) + ", got " + str.tostring(Doy))
    DateTime d = date_from_day_of_year(this.Year, Doy)
    new_datetime(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, this.UTC)

// @function        A copy moved to a given weekday within the same week, where the week begins on week_start. Never moves the date by more than six days.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param dow       The weekday wanted.
// @param week_start Which day the week begins on. Default MONDAY, the ISO convention.
// @returns         A new DateTime.
export method with_weekday(DateTime this, Weekday dow, Weekday week_start = Weekday.MONDAY) =>
    DateTime s0 = this.start_of(TimeUnit.WEEK, week_start)
    s0.plus_days(pmod(dow.to_int() - week_start.to_int(), 7)).with_time(this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS)

// The three java.time TemporalAdjusters that move to a weekday relative to
// this date rather than within a framed week: with_weekday above answers "the
// Friday of this week", these answer "the next Friday from here", which no
// choice of week_start can express.

// @function        The nearest date strictly after this one that falls on the given weekday, java.time's TemporalAdjusters.next. Strictly: asked from a Friday, the next Friday is a full week away, because "next" excludes today.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param dow       The weekday wanted.
// @returns         A new DateTime, 1 to 7 days forward, keeping the time of day.
export method next_weekday(DateTime this, Weekday dow) =>
    int step = this.weekday().days_until(dow)
    if step == 0
        step := 7
    this.plus_days(step)

// @function        The nearest date strictly before this one that falls on the given weekday, java.time's TemporalAdjusters.previous. Strictly: asked from a Friday, the previous Friday is a full week back.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param dow       The weekday wanted.
// @returns         A new DateTime, 1 to 7 days back, keeping the time of day.
export method previous_weekday(DateTime this, Weekday dow) =>
    int step = dow.days_until(this.weekday())
    if step == 0
        step := 7
    this.plus_days(-step)

// @function        This date when it already falls on the given weekday, otherwise the nearest one after it that does, java.time's TemporalAdjusters.nextOrSame, and the natural spelling of "the coming Friday, counting today".
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param dow       The weekday wanted.
// @returns         A new DateTime, 0 to 6 days forward, keeping the time of day.
export method next_or_same_weekday(DateTime this, Weekday dow) =>
    this.plus_days(this.weekday().days_until(dow))

// ------------------------------------------------- predicates and shape --

// @function        Whether this date falls on a Saturday or Sunday. Says nothing about whether a market is open; ask is_trading_day for that.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true at the weekend.
export method is_weekend(DateTime this) =>
    Weekday d = this.weekday()
    d == Weekday.SATURDAY or d == Weekday.SUNDAY

// @function        Whether this date falls Monday to Friday.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on a weekday.
export method is_weekday(DateTime this) =>
    not this.is_weekend()

// @function        Whether this date's year is a leap year.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true in a leap year.
export method is_leap(DateTime this) =>
    is_leap_year(this.Year)

// @function        Number of days in this date's month.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         28, 29, 30 or 31.
export method length_of_month(DateTime this) =>
    days_in_month(this.Year, this.Month)

// @function        Number of days in this date's year.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         365 or 366.
export method length_of_year(DateTime this) =>
    days_in_year(this.Year)

// @function        Whether this is the first day of its month.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on the 1st.
export method is_month_start(DateTime this) =>
    this.Day == 1

// @function        Whether this is the last day of its month.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on the final day.
export method is_month_end(DateTime this) =>
    this.Day == days_in_month(this.Year, this.Month)

// @function        Whether this is the first day of its quarter.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on 1 January, 1 April, 1 July or 1 October.
export method is_quarter_start(DateTime this) =>
    this.Day == 1 and (this.Month == 1 or this.Month == 4 or this.Month == 7 or this.Month == 10)

// @function        Whether this is the last day of its quarter.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on the final day of March, June, September or December.
export method is_quarter_end(DateTime this) =>
    this.is_month_end() and (this.Month == 3 or this.Month == 6 or this.Month == 9 or this.Month == 12)

// @function        Whether this is 1 January.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on the first day of the year.
export method is_year_start(DateTime this) =>
    this.Month == 1 and this.Day == 1

// @function        Whether this is 31 December.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @returns         true on the last day of the year.
export method is_year_end(DateTime this) =>
    this.Month == 12 and this.Day == 31

// @function        Whether this instant falls in the half-open span [lo, hi). Half-open so that adjacent spans tile without overlapping.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param lo        Inclusive lower bound.
// @param hi        Exclusive upper bound.
// @returns         true when lo <= this < hi, as instants.
export method is_between(DateTime this, DateTime lo, DateTime hi) =>
    int t = this.to_unix()
    t >= lo.to_unix() and t < hi.to_unix()

// @function        This instant confined to a range: lo when earlier than lo, hi when later than hi, otherwise itself.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param lo        Lower bound.
// @param hi        Upper bound.
// @returns         The clamped DateTime.
export method clamp_to(DateTime this, DateTime lo, DateTime hi) =>
    DateTime out = this
    if this.is_before(lo)
        out := lo
    if this.is_after(hi)
        out := hi
    out

// @function        The whole number of units from this date-time to another, the generic form of days_between and months_between, so a unit can be chosen at runtime.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @param unit      The unit to count in.
// @returns         Signed whole count; partial units do not count.
export method until(DateTime this, DateTime other, TimeUnit unit) =>
    int out = 0
    int ms = other.to_unix() - this.to_unix()
    switch unit
        TimeUnit.MS => out := ms
        TimeUnit.SECOND => out := tdiv(ms, MS_SECOND)
        TimeUnit.MINUTE => out := tdiv(ms, MS_MINUTE)
        TimeUnit.HOUR => out := tdiv(ms, MS_HOUR)
        TimeUnit.DAY => out := this.days_between(other)
        TimeUnit.WEEK => out := tdiv(this.days_between(other), 7)
        TimeUnit.MONTH => out := this.months_between(other)
        TimeUnit.QUARTER => out := tdiv(this.months_between(other), 3)
        TimeUnit.YEAR => out := tdiv(this.months_between(other), 12)
    out

// ------------------------------------------------------ zone conversion --
// The classic confusion pair, named as java.time names it. One keeps the
// moment and changes the wall clock; the other keeps the wall clock and
// changes the moment. Picking the wrong one is a silent error, so neither is
// the default and both say which they are.

// @function        The same moment expressed in another zone. 12:00 in New York becomes 17:00 in London; it is still the same instant.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param z         The target zone.
// @returns         A new DateTime with the target zone's offset in force.
export method to_zone_same_instant(DateTime this, Zone z) =>
    unix_to_date_zone(this.to_unix(), z)

// @function        The same wall clock reinterpreted in another zone, which denotes a different moment. 12:00 in New York becomes 12:00 in London, an hour that arrives five hours earlier.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param z         The target zone.
// @param res       How to resolve a local time the target zone skipped or repeated. Default COMPATIBLE.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when res is REJECT and the local time is impossible or ambiguous.
export method to_zone_same_local(DateTime this, Zone z, Resolver res = Resolver.COMPATIBLE) =>
    int t = z.to_unix(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second, this.MS, res)
    DateTime out = na
    if not na(t)
        out := unix_to_date_zone(t, z)
    out

// --------------------------------------------------- factories and bounds --

// @function        The current moment, in a zone.
// @param z         The zone to express it in. Default UTC.
// @returns         A DateTime for now.
export now(Zone z = Zone.UTC) =>
    unix_to_date_zone(timenow, z)

// @function        The current date at true local midnight in a zone, meaning the record's own to_unix() is the instant that midnight actually happened. The second resolution matters: start_of() truncates the civil fields while holding the offset in force at the original instant, which is correct for a fixed-offset record and wrong for a zone. At 15:00 New York on 1 November 2026 the record carries -05:00, but local midnight that day was still -04:00, so the un-resolved answer would be off by an hour on both transition days in every zone that observes daylight saving.
// @param z         The zone to resolve the date in. Required, with no default: "today" is a different date either side of the dateline, so there is no zone this function could pick that is right for a caller who did not think about it.
// @returns         A DateTime at the start of today, carrying the offset in force at that midnight.
export today(Zone z) =>
    DateTime d = unix_to_date_zone(timenow, z).start_of(TimeUnit.DAY)
    unix_to_date_zone(z.to_unix(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day), z)

// @function        The Unix epoch, 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.
// @returns         A DateTime at the epoch.
export epoch() =>
    new_datetime(1970, 1, 1)

// There are no datetime_min / datetime_max sentinels: `na` is this library's
// absent-value convention and needs no constructor, and a 0001-9999 range
// would assert a bound nothing here enforces: the Hinnant core handles year
// 20000 perfectly well.

// @function        Build a DateTime, returning na instead of raising when the fields do not describe a real moment. The non-throwing counterpart of new_datetime, for input you did not write yourself. A third behaviour is also available: the withers take an Overflow and can clamp an impossible day into the month rather than refusing it.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param Day       Day of month.
// @param Hour      Hour of day, 0-23. Default 0.
// @param Minute    Minute of hour, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param Second    Second of minute, 0-59. Default 0.
// @param MS        Millisecond, 0-999. Default 0.
// @param utc       Offset from UTC in hours. Default 0.
// @returns         A DateTime, or na when the input is not a real moment.
export try_new_datetime(int Year, int Month, int Day, int Hour = 0, int Minute = 0, int Second = 0, int MS = 0, float utc = 0.0) =>
    DateTime out = na
    if is_valid_date(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS)
        out := with_fields(Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute, Second, MS, utc, Overflow.REJECT)
    out

// ------------------------------------------------------------- weekday --

// @function        The weekday n days later in the weekly cycle, wrapping.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @param n         Days to advance; may be negative.
// @returns         The resulting Weekday.
export method plus(Weekday this, int n) =>
    weekday_from_int(this.to_int() + n)

// @function        The weekday n days earlier in the weekly cycle, wrapping.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @param n         Days to go back; may be negative.
// @returns         The resulting Weekday.
export method minus(Weekday this, int n) =>
    weekday_from_int(this.to_int() - n)

// -------------------------------------------------------------- period --

// @function        The sum of two periods, field by field. Not normalised, because 1 month plus 30 days is not a fixed quantity; call normalized() if you want months folded into years.
// @param this      The first Period.
// @param other     The second Period.
// @returns         A new Period.
export method plus(Period this, Period other) =>
    Period.new(this.Years + other.Years, this.Months + other.Months, this.Days + other.Days)

// @function        The difference of two periods, field by field.
// @param this      The Period to subtract from.
// @param other     The Period to subtract.
// @returns         A new Period.
export method minus(Period this, Period other) =>
    Period.new(this.Years - other.Years, this.Months - other.Months, this.Days - other.Days)

// @function        This period with every field's sign flipped.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         A new Period.
export method negated(Period this) =>
    Period.new(-this.Years, -this.Months, -this.Days)

// @function        Subtract a Period from a DateTime. Lives here rather than beside its eight minus_* siblings because it delegates through negated() just above, and Pine requires a definition to precede its use. Months are still applied before days: the period is negated first, exactly java.time's minus.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param p         The Period to subtract.
// @param ovf       What to do when the month step lands on a day that does not exist. Default CONSTRAIN.
// @returns         A new DateTime, or na when ovf is REJECT and the day does not exist.
export method minus_period(DateTime this, Period p, Overflow ovf = Overflow.CONSTRAIN) =>
    this.plus_period(p.negated(), ovf)

// @function        This period with every field scaled.
// @param this      The Period.
// @param n         The multiplier.
// @returns         A new Period.
export method multiplied_by(Period this, int n) =>
    Period.new(this.Years * n, this.Months * n, this.Days * n)

// @function        This period with surplus months folded into years, as java.time's Period.normalized: 1 year 15 months becomes 2 years 3 months. Days are left alone, because a day is not a fraction of a month.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         A new Period.
export method normalized(Period this) =>
    int total = this.Years * 12 + this.Months
    Period.new(tdiv(total, 12), total % 12, this.Days)

// @function        The years and months of this period expressed as a month count. Days are excluded, since they do not convert.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         Total whole months.
export method to_total_months(Period this) =>
    this.Years * 12 + this.Months

// @function        Whether every field of this period is zero.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         true for a zero period.
export method is_zero(Period this) =>
    this.Years == 0 and this.Months == 0 and this.Days == 0

// @function        Whether any field of this period is negative. A period can be mixed, e.g. 1 year minus 5 days, so this asks whether any component points backward.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         true when some field is negative.
export method is_negative(Period this) =>
    this.Years < 0 or this.Months < 0 or this.Days < 0

// @function        Whether two periods have identical fields. 12 months and 1 year are not equal under this test; normalise both first if that is what you meant.
// @param this      The first Period.
// @param other     The second Period.
// @returns         true when the fields match.
export method equals(Period this, Period other) =>
    bool out = false
    if not na(this) and not na(other)
        out := this.Years == other.Years and this.Months == other.Months and this.Days == other.Days
    out

// @function        This period in ISO-8601 form, e.g. "P1Y2M3D". A zero period is "P0D", as in java.time.
// @param this      The Period.
// @returns         The ISO-8601 period string.
export method to_iso(Period this) =>
    string out = "P"
    if this.Years != 0
        out := out + str.tostring(this.Years) + "Y"
    if this.Months != 0
        out := out + str.tostring(this.Months) + "M"
    if this.Days != 0
        out := out + str.tostring(this.Days) + "D"
    if str.length(out) == 1
        out := "P0D"
    out

// @function        Parse an ISO-8601 period such as "P1Y2M3D", "P3M", "P1W", "-P10D" or "P-1Y-2M-3D". Both negative forms are accepted because java.time emits per-component signs from toString but also parses a leading minus, and a parser that took only one of them would fail to read back its own output. Only the date part is accepted: a time component is not a Period, because months and hours are different kinds of quantity.
// @param s         The string to parse.
// @returns         The Period, or na when the string is not a valid date-only ISO period. A week form becomes days: "P1W" parses as 7 days, not a fourth field, because a week is exactly seven days, unlike a month.
export parse_iso_period(string s) =>
    string t = str.upper(s)
    int sign = 1
    if str.startswith(t, "-")
        sign := -1
        t := str.substring(t, 1, str.length(t))
    Period out = na
    if str.startswith(t, "P") and not str.contains(t, "T")
        int y = 0
        int mo = 0
        int d = 0
        int num = 0
        int csign = 1
        bool seen_digit = false
        bool ok = true
        // ISO-8601 lets a period be written in weeks, and forbids combining
        // that designator with any other; both halves are enforced here. The
        // informal parse_tenor("1W") also accepts a week, so the two parsers
        // agree on the form.
        bool saw_w = false
        bool saw_ymd = false
        // Guarded: a bare "P" makes this "for i = 1 to 0", which Pine walks
        // backwards rather than skipping, so i = 1 runs and asks for a
        // substring past the end of a one-character string.
        if str.length(t) > 1
            for i = 1 to str.length(t) - 1
                string ch = str.substring(t, i, i + 1)
                float v = str.tonumber(ch)
                if ch == "-" and not seen_digit
                    csign := -1
                else if ch == "+" and not seen_digit
                    csign := 1
                else if not na(v)
                    num := num * 10 + int(v)
                    seen_digit := true
                else if not seen_digit
                    ok := false
                else
                    if ch == "Y"
                        y := csign * num
                        saw_ymd := true
                    else if ch == "M"
                        mo := csign * num
                        saw_ymd := true
                    else if ch == "D"
                        d := csign * num
                        saw_ymd := true
                    else if ch == "W"
                        d := csign * num * 7
                        saw_w := true
                    else
                        ok := false
                    num := 0
                    csign := 1
                    seen_digit := false
        if saw_w and saw_ymd
            ok := false
        if ok and not seen_digit and str.length(t) > 1
            out := Period.new(sign * y, sign * mo, sign * d)
    out

// ------------------------------------------------------------ interval --

// @function        Build an interval from two instants, ordering them so the result is never reversed.
// @param a         One endpoint, Unix milliseconds.
// @param b         The other endpoint, Unix milliseconds.
// @returns         A half-open Interval [min, max).
export new_interval(int a, int b) =>
    Interval.new(math.min(a, b), math.max(a, b))

// @function        The length of this interval in milliseconds.
// @param this      The Interval.
// @returns         Milliseconds spanned; zero when empty.
export method duration(Interval this) =>
    math.max(0, this.ToMS - this.FromMS)

// @function        Whether this interval contains no instants at all.
// @param this      The Interval.
// @returns         true when the end is not after the start.
export method is_empty(Interval this) =>
    this.ToMS <= this.FromMS

// @function        Whether two intervals have identical endpoints.
// @param this      The first Interval.
// @param other     The second Interval.
// @returns         true when both endpoints match.
export method equals(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    bool out = false
    if not na(this) and not na(other)
        out := this.FromMS == other.FromMS and this.ToMS == other.ToMS
    out

// @function        Whether this interval wholly contains another.
// @param this      The outer Interval.
// @param other     The inner Interval.
// @returns         true when other lies entirely within this.
export method encloses(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    other.FromMS >= this.FromMS and other.ToMS <= this.ToMS

// @function        The overlapping part of two intervals.
// @param this      The first Interval.
// @param other     The second Interval.
// @returns         The shared Interval, or na when they do not overlap.
export method intersection(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    int lo = math.max(this.FromMS, other.FromMS)
    int hi = math.min(this.ToMS, other.ToMS)
    Interval out = na
    if hi > lo
        out := Interval.new(lo, hi)
    out

// @function        Whether two intervals meet exactly end to start without overlapping: the condition under which they can be joined into one without a hole or a double count.
// @param this      The first Interval.
// @param other     The second Interval.
// @returns         true when one ends precisely where the other begins.
export method abuts(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    this.ToMS == other.FromMS or other.ToMS == this.FromMS

// @function        The empty span between two intervals that neither overlap nor touch.
// @param this      The first Interval.
// @param other     The second Interval.
// @returns         The gap Interval, or na when they overlap or abut.
export method gap(Interval this, Interval other) =>
    Interval out = na
    if this.ToMS < other.FromMS
        out := Interval.new(this.ToMS, other.FromMS)
    if other.ToMS < this.FromMS
        out := Interval.new(other.ToMS, this.FromMS)
    out

// @function        Sort an array of intervals in place by start, ties by end, and return the same array so a call can chain. In place because that is what array.sort does; a caller who needs the original untouched copies first. Hand-rolled because Pine's array.sort does not accept UDT arrays. Insertion sort: stable, so equal intervals keep their input order, and quadratic, which at the sizes an indicator holds does not matter.
// @param intervals The intervals to sort.
// @returns         The same array, now ascending by FromMS then ToMS. RAISES when intervals is na: there is no array even to return.
export sort_intervals(array<Interval> intervals) =>
    if na(intervals)
        runtime.error("sort_intervals: intervals must not be na")
    int n = intervals.size()
    if n > 1
        for i = 1 to n - 1
            Interval key = intervals.get(i)
            int j = i - 1
            bool placed = false
            while j >= 0 and not placed
                Interval cur = intervals.get(j)
                if cur.FromMS > key.FromMS or (cur.FromMS == key.FromMS and cur.ToMS > key.ToMS)
                    intervals.set(j + 1, cur)
                    j := j - 1
                else
                    placed := true
            intervals.set(j + 1, key)
    intervals

// @function        The union of a set of intervals: overlapping or exactly abutting spans are joined and empty ones are dropped, so the result is the instants the inputs cover, and nothing about how raggedly they covered them. The input is left untouched: the sweep runs on a copy, and every returned Interval is newly built, so mutating an input interval afterwards cannot corrupt the union.
// @param intervals The intervals to merge.
// @returns         A new array of non-empty, pairwise disjoint, non-abutting intervals in ascending order; empty when the input is empty or holds only empty intervals. RAISES when intervals is na.
export merge_intervals(array<Interval> intervals) =>
    if na(intervals)
        runtime.error("merge_intervals: intervals must not be na")
    array<Interval> ordered = intervals.copy()
    sort_intervals(ordered)
    array<Interval> out = array.new<Interval>()
    int n = ordered.size()
    int cur_from = 0
    int cur_to = 0
    // "started", not "open": `open` is Pine's opening-price built-in, and
    // shadowing it draws a compiler warning.
    bool started = false
    // Guarded: Pine counts down through an empty range rather than skipping it.
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Interval it = ordered.get(i)
            // An empty interval contains no instants, so it is dropped rather
            // than swept along: [9, 9) in the output would read as a region
            // that contains nothing.
            if it.ToMS > it.FromMS
                if not started
                    cur_from := it.FromMS
                    cur_to := it.ToMS
                    started := true
                else if it.FromMS <= cur_to
                    cur_to := math.max(cur_to, it.ToMS)
                else
                    out.push(Interval.new(cur_from, cur_to))
                    cur_from := it.FromMS
                    cur_to := it.ToMS
    if started
        out.push(Interval.new(cur_from, cur_to))
    out

// Interval's ISO form lives in the formatting section, because it needs
// DateTime.to_iso and Pine requires a definition to precede its use.

// ------------------------------------------------------ market calendars --
// Rules where a rule exists, a dated table where one does not. Unscheduled
// closures (a hurricane, a state funeral) are not derivable from anything,
// so they live in a table and are complete only for the past. For that reason
// is_holiday must never be read as "and nothing else can close the market".
// Every closure is named, and the name is the source of truth: is_holiday
// derives membership from holiday_name, so a date cannot be closed and
// nameless, or named and open.

const string B36_DIGITS = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"

// The three base-36 digits of a day number, the form the dated tables
// store. A yyyymmdd key would read better, but string data compiles at two
// tokens per character, and twelve hundred dates at eight digits each is
// five figures of compiled weight for keys the code only ever compares
// whole. Outside [0, 46655] (before 1970 or after September 2097), the key is "-",
// which no table entry can equal, so an out-of-range date is never a member.
day_key36(int dayno) =>
    string out = "-"
    if dayno >= 0 and dayno <= 46655
        int a = fdiv(dayno, 1296)
        int b = fdiv(dayno, 36) % 36
        int c = dayno % 36
        out := str.substring(B36_DIGITS, a, a + 1) + str.substring(B36_DIGITS, b, b + 1) + str.substring(B36_DIGITS, c, c + 1)
    out

// The yyyymmdd key a closure-name switch matches on.
date_key(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    str.format("{0,number,0000}{1,number,00}{2,number,00}", Year, Month, Day)

// Membership test for a dated table. Every table carries a leading and a
// trailing comma of its own, so a date can never match as a substring of a
// neighbour and this builds only the five-byte needle rather than a fresh
// copy of the whole table on each call: on the kilobyte-scale tables, the
// difference between a search and a memcpy per date tested. A table written
// without its sentinel commas would silently fail to match its own first and
// last entries.
in_date_table(string table, int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    str.contains(table, "," + day_key36(days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)) + ",")

// NYSE unscheduled closures, named. Retrospective by nature: no rule predicts
// these. The two November dates are presidential Election Days: the NYSE
// observed those until 1980 and then stopped, so dated entries cost less than
// a rule with an era gate that could never fire again.
nyse_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "19761102" => out := "Election Day"
        "19770714" => out := "New York City blackout"
        "19801104" => out := "Election Day"
        "19850927" => out := "Hurricane Gloria"
        "19940427" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20010911" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010912" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010913" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010914" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20040611" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20070102" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20121029" => out := "Hurricane Sandy"
        "20121030" => out := "Hurricane Sandy"
        "20181205" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20250109" => out := "National day of mourning"
    out

// NYSE early closes that no rule generates. Two July shapes live here, and
// they contradict each other, which is why neither became a rule. The 3 July
// entries predate the standing policy the is_early_close branch below applies
// from 2003; before it the NYSE shortened that day only sometimes: 3 July
// 1996 and 2002 were full sessions on the very weekday 2013, 2019 and 2024
// closed early on. Those two years instead shortened the Friday after a
// Thursday 4 July, a shape the NYSE has not repeated since. The two
// 26 December entries are the Friday after Christmas, and 19991231 is the Y2K
// half day, a one-off in both directions. A table rather than a name switch
// because an early close has no holiday to name: the day trades.
const string NYSE_EARLY_ADHOC = ",76q,7gy,7r1,7vx,8gc,8lh,8vm,95t,9ks,"

// CME unscheduled closures, named. Compare the NYSE list above: CME traded
// through Hurricane Sandy, which shut the NYSE for two days, so the two
// exchanges' unscheduled closures are not the same set and must not share a
// table. September 2001 they do share, but for different lengths: the floor
// and Globex were down from the 11th, the industry observed the 12th as a day
// of recognition, and while most CME products resumed on the 13th, the
// equity-index complex (the schedule this library models) did not come back
// until the 17th.
cme_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "20010911" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010912" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010913" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010914" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20040611" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20070102" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20181205" => out := "National day of mourning"
        "20250109" => out := "National day of mourning"
    out

// LSE one-off closures, named: royal weddings, jubilees, a funeral, a
// coronation, a millennium. In several of these years the spring bank holiday
// also moved, which is why the moved-away dates appear in the open-anyway
// table below.
lse_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "19770607" => out := "Silver Jubilee"
        "19810729" => out := "Royal wedding"
        "19950508" => out := "VE Day 50th anniversary"
        "19991231" => out := "Millennium Eve"
        "20020603" => out := "Golden Jubilee"
        "20020604" => out := "Golden Jubilee"
        "20110429" => out := "Royal wedding"
        "20120604" => out := "Diamond Jubilee"
        "20120605" => out := "Diamond Jubilee"
        "20200508" => out := "VE Day 75th anniversary"
        "20220602" => out := "Platinum Jubilee"
        "20220603" => out := "Platinum Jubilee"
        "20220919" => out := "State funeral of Queen Elizabeth II"
        "20230508" => out := "Coronation of King Charles III"
    out

// Days the LSE rules would call a holiday but which actually traded, because
// the bank holiday was displaced to one of the closure dates above. 1995 is
// the VE Day 50th anniversary and 2020 the 75th; both moved the early May
// holiday from the Monday to the Friday of that week. The other three are
// jubilee years, where the late spring holiday moved into June. A table
// rather than a name switch: these suppress a name, they do not carry one.
const string LSE_OPEN_ANYWAY = ",74z,94q,by8,e6q,erq,"
// Days the LSE half-day rule would call an early close but which ran a full
// session. Only one date qualifies: the rule pulls the half day back onto the
// last trading day before New Year, and when 31 December 1999 became a full
// closure the exchange did not move the half day onto the 30th with it.
const string LSE_FULL_DAY_ANYWAY = ",8gb,"

// The two December 2022 sessions Sydney ran to the close, against its own
// standing policy. The reference calendar gates its half-day rule to end in
// 2022 and restart in 2023, so this is the source's claim rather than an
// inference from a gap; it is tabled the same way the London exception is.
const string ASX_FULL_DAY_ANYWAY = ",exh,exo,"

// Shanghai, Bombay and Singapore are tables, not rule sets, and the tables
// stop at the end of 2026. Shanghai's holiday blocks are re-announced by the
// State Council each autumn and their lengths genuinely move: Labour Day has
// run five weekdays, then one or two, and three since 2019, so no rule
// extrapolates them. Bombay and Singapore carry a handful of real fixed dates
// and the rest of their calendars are lunar, lunisolar or Islamic. Past the
// horizon closed_for_holiday answers UNKNOWN; is_holiday cannot, and would
// call Chinese New Year an ordinary session.
const string SSE_CLOSED = ",a5a,a5b,a5y,a5z,a62,a63,a64,a65,a66,a8l,a8m,a8n,a8o,a8p,acv,acw,acx,acy,acz,afe,aff,afg,agr,ags,agt,agu,agv,aiq,air,ais,ait,aiw,amz,an0,an1,an2,an3,api,apj,aqj,aqk,aql,aqo,aqp,as5,asw,asx,atz,awp,ax3,ax4,ax5,ax6,ax7,azp,azq,b0e,b0f,b0g,b0h,b0i,b2c,b31,b3s,b3t,b7a,b7b,b7e,b7f,b7g,b7h,b9u,bb3,bb4,bb5,bb6,bb7,bcg,bd8,bee,bef,beg,bh6,bh7,bh8,bhf,bhi,bhj,bhk,bhl,bk1,bkv,bkw,bkx,bl0,bl1,bmk,bml,bnc,bob,br1,brm,brn,bro,brp,brq,bu5,bu6,buq,bur,bus,but,buu,bwo,bwp,bwq,bxg,bxh,byx,c1q,c1r,c1s,c1t,c1u,c4a,c4b,c4c,c5f,c5g,c5h,c5i,c5j,c6v,c6w,c7k,c7l,c7m,c8q,c8r,c8s,cbj,cbk,cbv,cbw,cbx,cby,cc1,cef,cf9,cfc,cfd,cfe,cff,ch3,chr,chs,cin,cld,cm0,cm1,cm2,cm5,cm6,cok,col,cpw,cpx,cpy,cq1,cq2,cr7,crw,ctc,cvd,cve,cw5,cw6,cw9,cwa,cwb,cyp,czr,czs,czt,czu,czv,d1b,d23,d35,d36,d5v,d5w,d6d,d6e,d6f,d6g,d6h,d8w,d9l,d9o,d9p,d9q,d9r,dbf,dbg,dc7,dcz,dd0,dgh,dgi,dgj,dgk,dgl,dj0,dk9,dka,dkd,dke,dkf,dlm,dln,dmb,dmc,dno,dqe,dql,dqm,dqn,dqo,dqp,dt4,dt5,du3,du4,du5,du6,du7,dvr,dwh,dwi,dwj,dxi,e08,e0q,e0r,e0s,e0t,e0w,e3a,e3x,e40,e41,e42,e43,e44,e5y,e6n,e6q,e6r,e86,e87,eaw,eax,eb0,eb1,eb2,eb3,edg,eel,eem,eep,eeq,eer,eg2,egu,egv,egw,ei0,ekq,ekr,el1,el4,el5,el6,el7,enn,eof,eog,eoh,eoi,eoj,eq6,eq7,eqy,eqz,er0,eru,eun,ev8,ev9,eva,evb,evc,exr,eyc,eyd,eye,eyf,eyg,f0c,f12,f13,f14,f2i,f2j,f59,f5c,f5d,f5e,f5f,f5g,f7v,f8y,f91,f92,f93,f94,f95,fah,fai,fb8,fb9,fba,fcc,ff2,ff3,ffh,ffi,ffj,ffk,ffn,fi1,fis,fit,fiu,fiv,fiy,fiz,fkm,fld,fle,flh,fm9,fpm,fpn,fpo,fpr,fps,fpt,fs6,fs7,ftg,fth,fti,ftj,ftk,ftn,fut,fvi,fvl,fvm,fwv,fzl,fzr,fzs,fzv,fzw,fzx,"
const string BSE_CLOSED = ",a5j,a6c,a7a,a7w,a81,adh,adi,afe,ag7,ago,ahr,ai1,air,ap8,arc,arq,arr,asj,ate,awd,axd,axy,ayc,ayq,az2,azw,b16,b1l,b1m,b29,b2d,b2g,b30,b70,b77,b7m,b7s,b86,b9q,b9u,bb0,bbh,bc4,bcd,bgu,biq,bjk,bln,bms,bn2,bqp,bqq,brp,bsa,bsl,bso,bte,bvi,bvz,bwr,bws,c0k,c1e,c2d,c2y,c3c,c6n,c6p,c7a,c7f,cae,cb9,cca,cct,cd3,cg0,cgi,ch4,che,chk,ck8,cl3,cm2,cm5,cme,cmm,cmn,cmy,cn0,cpv,cqc,cr3,cr4,cvr,cvz,cwq,cxa,cxb,cxo,d0j,d10,d11,d1m,d1q,d3w,d5l,d5t,d6l,d6m,d75,d7j,dad,dau,dbg,ddr,dff,dgy,dgz,dk7,dko,dlf,dlg,dph,dq3,dqa,dr2,drm,drn,ds2,duv,dvc,dw3,dw5,dwf,dxg,dzc,dzx,e05,e0x,e1a,e1h,e1w,e4p,e57,e5u,e5y,e62,e7b,ec6,eck,efd,efv,efz,egi,eh4,ej1,eju,ekg,elf,elz,em0,eme,ep8,epp,eqh,eqz,etp,eub,eva,evt,evv,ew8,ezj,f06,f0b,f0e,f2p,f4z,f5y,f6j,f6w,f8g,f9q,fa7,fab,fao,fau,fbr,fcj,fdd,fgc,fgq,fgv,fjl,fk1,fki,fks,fl0,fon,fq6,fq7,fql,fsk,ftv,fui,fun,fuq,fw9,fx2,fza,g0a,g0v,g19,"
// The two Saturdays the Bombay exchange traded, both special sessions. A
// weekday mask alone is wrong on these, so they are a positive table rather
// than an absence: is_trading_day consults it before the weekend gate.
const string BSE_OPEN_ANYWAY = ",f8e,fiw,"
const string SGX_CLOSED = ",a5i,a62,a63,a84,a8w,adh,aff,agr,ags,ai1,ajk,ao1,ap7,aqk,aql,arr,ate,ax5,axv,az1,b0e,b0f,b2g,b70,b8v,bb3,bb4,bcd,bdx,bgu,bie,biq,bkw,bkx,bn2,bnr,bqo,bs9,bsl,buq,bur,bws,c0k,c2f,c2x,c5f,c5g,c6p,c89,cad,cc9,cf9,che,ci3,ck7,cm5,cml,cpx,cpy,cr4,csr,cu1,cum,cvl,cvy,cx9,czr,czs,d11,d3w,d5s,d9o,dbq,dcg,ddr,dfm,dgx,dka,dlg,dn4,dnl,dph,drl,du4,du5,dw5,dx0,dxg,dzc,e1h,e40,e62,e6t,e7b,e8l,e96,eem,efz,eh4,ehh,ej0,elz,eog,eoh,eqh,eqz,erc,esw,evt,eyc,eyd,f0e,f1y,f2p,f4h,f6i,f91,fab,fan,fbt,fcj,fgb,fit,fiu,fki,fl0,flo,fq5,fth,fti,fuq,fw8,fwd,g0u,"

// JPX one-off national holidays, named: the 2019 imperial transition pair, and
// the 2020/2021 Olympic moves of Marine Day, Sports Day and Mountain Day. Only
// the moved-to dates live here; the vacated rule dates are era-gated out in
// jpx_statutory_holiday_name, because in those years the rule days genuinely
// traded: 12 October 2020 was an open Monday. 2019-05-01 alone closes three
// days: 30 April and 2 May follow from it via the sandwich rule, so they are
// not tabled separately. 2021-08-08 is a Sunday, and its Monday substitute
// likewise falls out of the general rule rather than the table.
jpx_one_off_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "20190501" => out := "Enthronement Day"
        "20191022" => out := "Enthronement Ceremony Day"
        "20200723" => out := "Marine Day"
        "20200724" => out := "Sports Day"
        "20200810" => out := "Mountain Day"
        "20210722" => out := "Marine Day"
        "20210723" => out := "Sports Day"
        "20210808" => out := "Mountain Day"
    out

// @function        The zone an exchange's local session times are quoted in.
// @param this      The exchange.
// @returns         The Zone. RAISES on an Exchange the switch does not handle, a library bug, and a loud one by design: every session boundary in this file resolves through here, so an unhandled member falling through to New York would move them all at once.
export method zone(Exchange this) =>
    Zone out = na
    switch this
        Exchange.NYSE => out := Zone.NEW_YORK
        Exchange.LSE => out := Zone.LONDON
        Exchange.CME => out := Zone.CHICAGO
        Exchange.JPX => out := Zone.TOKYO
        Exchange.EUREX => out := Zone.FRANKFURT
        Exchange.HKEX => out := Zone.HONG_KONG
        Exchange.ASX => out := Zone.SYDNEY
        Exchange.TSX => out := Zone.TORONTO
        Exchange.SSE => out := Zone.SHANGHAI
        Exchange.BSE => out := Zone.MUMBAI
        Exchange.SGX => out := Zone.SINGAPORE
        // Crypto's day boundary is a convention rather than a location, and
        // the convention the venues settle on is the UTC midnight.
        Exchange.CRYPTO => out := Zone.UTC
        => runtime.error("zone: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")
    out

// UK substitute-day rule for New Year: a weekend New Year's Day moves to the
// following Monday. Returns the name or na rather than a bool, like every
// holiday rule here, so the predicate and the name cannot disagree.
lse_new_year_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday d1 = date_to_weekday(Year, 1, 1)
    int obs = 1
    if d1 == Weekday.SATURDAY
        obs := 3
    if d1 == Weekday.SUNDAY
        obs := 2
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == obs
        out := "New Year's Day"
    out

// UK substitute-day rule for Christmas and Boxing Day. Both are holidays, and
// each weekend one pushes a substitute into the following week, so the pair
// can land on 25/26, 25/28, 26/27 or 27/28 depending on the weekday. The
// substitute keeps the name of the day it observes: a is Christmas Day
// observed, b is Boxing Day observed.
lse_christmas_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday d25 = date_to_weekday(Year, 12, 25)
    int a = 25
    int b = 26
    if d25 == Weekday.FRIDAY
        b := 28
    if d25 == Weekday.SATURDAY
        a := 27
        b := 28
    if d25 == Weekday.SUNDAY
        a := 26
        b := 27
    string out = na
    if Month == 12 and Day == a
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == b
        out := "Boxing Day"
    out

// ----- JPX holiday rules. Rules exact from 2000; they agree with
// exchange_calendars XTKS across that reference's whole range, 2006-08-14 to
// 2027-08-13. The 1 October 2020 Arrowhead outage, which no rule generates,
// is the one-off handled at the end of the chain. Three
// layers, applied in order: statutory holidays here, then the two derived
// kinds below, which read only this layer as input.

// The name of the Japanese statutory national holiday on a date by rule, plus
// the one-off names, or na. This is the base layer only: no sandwiches, no
// substitutes, because the derived rules must see exactly the statute:
// a substitute that counted another substitute as its "already a holiday"
// would chain, and a sandwich between two substitutes is not a thing the law
// defines.
jpx_statutory_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    // Fixed dates. 29 April is one date under two successive names (Greenery
    // Day through 2006, Showa Day from 2007), and the name switch carries
    // exactly that era gate; 4 May became a statutory holiday only in 2007;
    // before that the sandwich rule covered it, which is why a Sunday
    // 4 May 2003 produced no substitute and 6 May 2003 traded. December 23 is
    // Akihito's birthday, gone with the abdication: 2019 had no Emperor's
    // Birthday at all, and Naruhito's 23 February starts in 2020.
    if Month == 1 and Day == 1
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 2 and Day == 11
        out := "National Foundation Day"
    if Month == 2 and Day == 23 and Year >= 2020
        out := "Emperor's Birthday"
    if Month == 4 and Day == 29
        out := "Showa Day"
        if Year <= 2006
            out := "Greenery Day"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 3
        out := "Constitution Memorial Day"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 4 and Year >= 2007
        out := "Greenery Day"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 5
        out := "Children's Day"
    // Mountain Day exists from 2016; in 2020 and 2021 it moved for the
    // Olympics, and the moved dates carry their names in the one-offs instead.
    if Month == 8 and Day == 11 and Year >= 2016 and Year != 2020 and Year != 2021
        out := "Mountain Day"
    if Month == 11 and Day == 3
        out := "Culture Day"
    if Month == 11 and Day == 23
        out := "Labour Thanksgiving Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 23 and Year <= 2018
        out := "Emperor's Birthday"
    // Happy Monday holidays. Coming of Age Day and Sports Day are nth-Monday
    // from exactly 2000, which is what anchors the rules-exact-from-2000
    // claim; Marine Day and Respect for the Aged Day kept fixed dates through
    // 2002. The Olympic years vacate the July and October Mondays entirely.
    // The October Monday was renamed by statute in 2020, so its name carries
    // an era gate the date rule does not need.
    if Month == 1 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 1, Weekday.MONDAY, 2)
        out := "Coming of Age Day"
    if Month == 7
        if Year >= 2003 and Year != 2020 and Year != 2021 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 7, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
            out := "Marine Day"
        if Year <= 2002 and Day == 20
            out := "Marine Day"
    if Month == 9
        if Year >= 2003 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
            out := "Respect for the Aged Day"
        if Year <= 2002 and Day == 15
            out := "Respect for the Aged Day"
    if Month == 10 and Year != 2020 and Year != 2021 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 10, Weekday.MONDAY, 2)
        out := "Sports Day"
        if Year <= 2019
            out := "Health and Sports Day"
    // The equinoxes, by the standard integer approximation, valid 1980-2099.
    // It reproduces every officially announced date inside the verified
    // 2006-2027 range; past 2099 it is extrapolation, as the header says.
    if Month == 3 and Day == int(math.floor(20.8431 + 0.242194 * (Year - 1980))) - fdiv(Year - 1980, 4)
        out := "Vernal Equinox Day"
    if Month == 9 and Day == int(math.floor(23.2488 + 0.242194 * (Year - 1980))) - fdiv(Year - 1980, 4)
        out := "Autumnal Equinox Day"
    // Gated on the three years the one-offs occupy, so the common path never
    // builds a date key.
    if Year >= 2019 and Year <= 2021
        string one_off = jpx_one_off_name(Year, Month, Day)
        if not na(one_off)
            out := one_off
    out

// The predicate form of the statutory layer, derived from the name rather
// than restated. The sandwich and substitute rules below read this, so the
// statute they derive from is the same one the names describe.
jpx_statutory_holiday(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    not na(jpx_statutory_holiday_name(Year, Month, Day))

// Citizens' holiday, the "sandwich" rule: a day whose previous and next days
// are both statutory holidays, which is not itself one and is not a Sunday,
// is a holiday. Silver-week Tuesdays (22 September of 2009, 2015, 2026) and
// the 2019 enthronement bridge days are this rule at work. It reads the
// statutory layer only, and the neighbours carry their own year, so the
// year-boundary case needs no special handling here.
jpx_sandwich_holiday(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    bool out = false
    if not jpx_statutory_holiday(Year, Month, Day) and date_to_weekday(Year, Month, Day) != Weekday.SUNDAY
        int here = days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)
        DateTime p = unix_to_date((here - 1) * MS_DAY)
        DateTime nx = unix_to_date((here + 1) * MS_DAY)
        out := jpx_statutory_holiday(p.Year, p.Month, p.Day) and jpx_statutory_holiday(nx.Year, nx.Month, nx.Day)
    out

// Substitute holiday: a statutory holiday falling on Sunday observes on the
// next day that is not already a holiday, walking forward past consecutive
// ones: May 3 on a Sunday substitutes on Wednesday the 6th, past two
// statutory days in between. Read backwards from the candidate: this date is
// a substitute exactly when the contiguous run of holidays immediately
// before it contains a Sunday statutory holiday. This is the post-2007
// statute; the pre-2007 "immediately following day" form never diverges from
// it in 2000-2006, because no Sunday holiday in that span is followed by
// another holiday, so one rule serves the whole scope.
jpx_substitute_holiday(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    bool out = false
    if not jpx_statutory_holiday(Year, Month, Day) and not jpx_sandwich_holiday(Year, Month, Day)
        int here = days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)
        bool walking = true
        // A fixed bound, not a computed range, so no down-count guard is
        // needed. The longest real run is the seven-day 2019 Golden Week;
        // ten clears any the statute can build.
        for k = 1 to 10
            if walking
                DateTime c = unix_to_date((here - k) * MS_DAY)
                bool stat = jpx_statutory_holiday(c.Year, c.Month, c.Day)
                if stat and c.weekday() == Weekday.SUNDAY
                    out := true
                    walking := false
                else if not stat and not jpx_sandwich_holiday(c.Year, c.Month, c.Day)
                    walking := false
    out

// From here to the end of the expiry section, a civil date is a DateTime and
// sits left of the dot. Not a loose (Year, Month, Day) triple: three bare
// ints of the same type are three chances to transpose two of them with no
// chance for the compiler to notice, and the library already has a type whose
// entire job is to hold a date its constructor did not refuse. Loose
// (Year, Month, Day) appears only where a function builds a date rather than
// asking about one: days_from_civil, new_datetime, nth_weekday_of_month,
// monthly_expiry_day, date_from_iso_week and their kin.
//   The bodies read this.Year / this.Month / this.Day directly, so taking a
// receiver adds no validation step and no raising path.

// The full NYSE calendar's name: the rule layer plus the named closures.
nyse_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = nyse_rule_holiday_name(Year, Month, Day)
    string closed = nyse_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// CME closes on three days a year. It trades the other US federal holidays on
// a shortened session (see is_early_close) and does not observe Juneteenth
// at all.
cme_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday dow = date_to_weekday(Year, Month, Day)
    bool weekend = dow == Weekday.SATURDAY or dow == Weekday.SUNDAY
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == 1 and not weekend
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 1 and Day == 2 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 25 and not weekend
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 24 and dow == Weekday.FRIDAY
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 26 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    string closed = cme_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// The LSE calendar's name: bank-holiday rules, then the open-anyway table
// suppressing a displaced rule name, then the named one-off closures. The
// order matters because a jubilee year does both at once: the rule's Monday
// traded and the moved days closed.
lse_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    DateTime em = easter_sunday(Year).plus_days(1)
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = lse_new_year_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    if Month == em.Month and Day == em.Day
        out := "Easter Monday"
    if Month == 5 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, 1)
        out := "Early May bank holiday"
    if Month == 5 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, -1)
        out := "Spring bank holiday"
    if Month == 8 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 8, Weekday.MONDAY, -1)
        out := "Summer bank holiday"
    string xmas = lse_christmas_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(xmas)
        out := xmas
    if in_date_table(LSE_OPEN_ANYWAY, Year, Month, Day)
        out := na
    string closed = lse_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// The JPX calendar's name. Closed 31 December through 3 January every year
// regardless of weekday or holiday status: 30 December is the year's last
// session and 4 January its first; then the national holidays with their
// sandwich and substitute rules, then the outage. Unlike the NYSE rules these
// are not weekday-gated: a Japanese holiday landing on a weekend stays a
// holiday (that is what creates the Monday substitute), and reporting it
// costs nothing because is_trading_day tests the weekend first. The statute
// names win over the year-end closure label, so 1 January reads as New
// Year's Day rather than as the closure that contains it.
jpx_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = jpx_statutory_holiday_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if na(out)
        if Month == 12 and Day == 31
            out := "Year-end closure"
        if Month == 1 and Day <= 3
            out := "New Year closure"
    if na(out) and jpx_sandwich_holiday(Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Citizens' Holiday"
    if na(out) and jpx_substitute_holiday(Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Substitute Holiday"
    // The 1 October 2020 Arrowhead system outage, a full-day unscheduled
    // trading halt. Deliberately outside the statutory layer the derived
    // rules read as input, because a system failure is not a holiday: it must
    // close the market without ever sandwiching a citizens' day or absorbing
    // a substitute.
    if na(out) and Year == 2020 and Month == 10 and Day == 1
        out := "Arrowhead system outage"
    out

// TARGET2-shaped: New Year, Good Friday, Easter Monday, May Day, Christmas
// Eve through Boxing Day, New Year's Eve. The whole set agrees with
// exchange_calendars XEUR over its full range, and there is no one-off
// closure to table: Eurex traded through 11 September 2001 and trades every
// German-only holiday. The fixed dates are not weekday-gated because TARGET2
// holidays do not move off a weekend; a weekend hit changes no trading day.
eurex_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    DateTime em = easter_sunday(Year).plus_days(1)
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == 1
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    if Month == em.Month and Day == em.Day
        out := "Easter Monday"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 1
        out := "Labour Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 24
        out := "Christmas Eve"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 25
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 26
        out := "Boxing Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 31
        out := "New Year's Eve"
    out

// The Hong Kong lunar holidays, tabled rather than computed. A lunisolar
// computus is a large piece of machinery to carry for six festivals a year,
// and the dates it produced would still need the substitution and collision
// rules layered over them. These tables hold the observed dates instead, so
// the Sunday substitutions, the Ching Ming that steps aside when it lands on
// Easter Monday and the Mid-Autumn day that steps aside for National Day are
// already inside the data and no rule has to reproduce them. Read out of
// exchange_calendars XHKG over the whole range that reference publishes,
// which is what fixes this calendar's horizon at 2049.
const string HKEX_LNY = ",8hb,8he,8r6,8r7,8r8,91u,91v,91w,9bn,9bq,9lj,9lk,9w7,9w8,9w9,a62,a63,agr,ags,aqk,aql,b0e,b0f,b0g,bb3,bb4,bkw,bkx,buq,bur,bus,c5f,c5g,c5h,cf9,cfc,cpx,cpy,czr,czs,czt,d9o,d9p,dka,dkd,du4,du5,du6,e40,e41,eem,eep,eog,eoh,eoi,eyc,eyd,eye,f91,f92,fit,fiu,fiv,fth,fti,ftj,g3d,g3e,gd5,gd6,gd7,gnt,gnu,gnv,gxp,gxq,gxr,h7i,h7j,hi6,hi7,hi8,hs1,hs2,hs3,i2q,i2r,i2s,icj,ick,imd,ime,imf,ix2,ix3,ix4,j6v,j6w,jgp,jgq,jgr,jre,jrf,jrg,k18,k1b,kb3,kb4,kb5,klr,kls,klt,kvn,kvo,l69,l6c,lg3,lg4,lg5,lpz,lq0,m0l,m0o,maf,mag,mah,"
const string HKEX_CHING_MING = ",8iz,8t5,93a,9nl,9xq,a7v,ai0,as5,bch,bml,bwq,c6v,cr8,d1b,dbg,dlm,dvr,eg3,eq7,f0c,fah,fkm,fuu,g4x,gf2,gp7,gzd,hjo,hts,i3y,ie3,io8,j8j,jio,jst,k2y,kna,kxe,l7j,lho,lru,mc5,"
const string HKEX_BUDDHA = ",8k0,8tu,94j,9ec,9p0,9yv,a8p,ajd,at7,bdq,bnk,c82,chw,csk,dc9,dmx,dwt,e6m,eha,er5,f1r,fbm,flh,fw6,g5z,gfu,gqi,h0b,haz,hku,huo,i4j,if7,izp,j9j,ju1,k3v,kej,ky9,l8x,lis,lsl,m39,md4,"
const string HKEX_TUEN_NG = ",8kq,8ve,9f3,9pr,a9f,ak3,atz,b3s,beg,bob,c8s,cin,d35,dd0,dno,dxi,e86,ei0,eru,f2i,fcc,fwv,g6q,ggl,h12,hbq,hve,i59,ify,ips,j0g,jaa,jk4,jus,k4m,kfb,kp4,kyz,l9n,ljh,ltc,m3z,mdt,"
const string HKEX_MID_AUTUMN = ",8nh,8y5,9hv,9si,a2d,amu,awp,bh7,br2,c1r,cbk,cle,cw2,d5w,dgk,dqf,eax,eks,eun,ff4,fps,g9h,gk5,h3u,hei,hoc,hy6,i80,iio,j37,jd1,jnp,jxj,k7e,ki1,krv,l2j,lce,m6r,"
const string HKEX_CHUNG_YEUNG = ",8o4,8ys,98m,9t5,a2z,adn,anh,axb,b7z,bro,c2c,cc8,cm1,cwp,d6k,dr1,e0w,ebl,ele,ev9,f5x,ffr,fqe,g09,ga3,gkr,gum,hf4,hoy,i8n,ija,ity,jdo,joc,jy7,k80,kio,ksj,ld0,lmv,lxk,m7e,mh8,"

// Lunar New Year's Eve half days. The market first shortened this session in
// 2013, so the table starts there rather than at the top of the lunar range,
// and the eves before it ran full sessions: 6 February 2008 and 2 February
// 2011 were both weekday eves and both traded to 16:00.
const string HKEX_LNY_EVE = ",cf8,cpw,d9l,dk9,du3,e3x,eel,eof,f8y,fis,ftg,g3a,gd4,gns,h7h,hi5,ici,j6u,k17,kb2,klq,kvk,l68,lg2,lpw,m0k,mae,"

// Hong Kong unscheduled closures, named. Typhoon and black-rainstorm signals
// shut the market on hours of notice, so these are retrospective in exactly
// the way the New York and London tables are. The individual storm names are
// not modelled: the reference records the closure, not its cause, and
// inventing a name per date would assert more than the source carries.
hkex_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "20010706" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20010725" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20080806" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20080822" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20110929" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20130814" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        // The seventieth anniversary of the end of the Second World War, a
        // one-off public holiday granted across Hong Kong and the mainland.
        "20150903" => out := "Commemoration of the allied victory"
        "20160802" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20161021" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20170823" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20201013" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20211013" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20230717" => out := "Severe weather closure"
        "20240906" => out := "Severe weather closure"
    out

// A fixed-date Hong Kong holiday, plus the Monday standing in for it when it
// falls on a Sunday. Saturday earns no substitute at all: 1 January 2011,
// 1 May 2010, 1 July 2017 and 1 October 2011 were Saturdays and the following
// Mondays all traded.
hkex_fixed_hit(int Year, int Month, int Day, int hm, int hd) =>
    bool out = Month == hm and Day == hd
    if Month == hm and Day == hd + 1 and date_to_weekday(Year, hm, hd) == Weekday.SUNDAY
        out := true
    out

// The HKEX calendar's name: four fixed dates with their Sunday substitutes,
// the Easter pair, the Christmas window, then the six lunar tables, then the
// unscheduled closures last so a storm outranks whatever else the day was.
hkex_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    DateTime em = easter_sunday(Year).plus_days(1)
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = na
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 1, 1)
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 5, 1)
        out := "Labour Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 7, 1)
        out := "HKSAR Establishment Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 10, 1)
        out := "National Day"
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    if Month == em.Month and Day == em.Day
        out := "Easter Monday"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 25
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 26
        out := "Boxing Day"
    // One displaced day, not two: Christmas on a Saturday gives only the 27th,
    // because Boxing Day is then the Sunday and the pair collapses into it.
    Weekday x25 = date_to_weekday(Year, 12, 25)
    if Month == 12 and Day == 27 and (x25 == Weekday.SATURDAY or x25 == Weekday.SUNDAY)
        out := "Christmas holiday"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_LNY, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Lunar New Year"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_CHING_MING, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Ching Ming Festival"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_BUDDHA, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Buddha's Birthday"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_TUEN_NG, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Tuen Ng Festival"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_MID_AUTUMN, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Day after Mid-Autumn Festival"
    if in_date_table(HKEX_CHUNG_YEUNG, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Chung Yeung Festival"
    string closed = hkex_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// The closure dates again, as tables the enumerator can read. The switches
// above are the source of the names and cannot be enumerated from Pine, so
// these carry the same dates in the form holiday_tables needs. Two spellings
// of one fact is a drift risk, so each table is held equal to the key set of
// the switch it shadows: a date added to one and not the other is an error,
// not a preference.
const string NYSE_CLOSED_TABLE = ",1xd,24f,320,4fo,6uq,8xk,8xl,8xm,8xn,9pg,aff,c2i,c2j,dse,fi9,"
const string CME_CLOSED_TABLE = ",8xk,8xl,8xm,8xn,9pg,aff,dse,fi9,"
const string LSE_CLOSED_TABLE = ",23e,39f,756,8gc,94x,94y,bn9,byf,byg,e6u,ert,eru,euu,f19,"
const string JPX_ONE_OFF_TABLE = ",dwh,e1b,e8y,e8z,e9g,ej2,ej3,ejj,"
const string HKEX_CLOSED_TABLE = ",8vp,8w8,avl,aw1,bri,caj,cvd,d4n,d6v,dfd,eb8,eld,f37,fes,"
const string ASX_CLOSED_TABLE = ",8gc,bd1,bn6,eux,"
const string TSX_CLOSED_TABLE = ",8xk,8xl,"

// A fixed date pushed off a weekend onto the following Monday, returned as a
// day of the month. Every holiday it is used for falls on the 1st or the 26th,
// so the shifted day cannot run past the end of its month.
observed_monday_day(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday d = date_to_weekday(Year, Month, Day)
    int out = Day
    if d == Weekday.SATURDAY
        out := Day + 2
    if d == Weekday.SUNDAY
        out := Day + 1
    out

// Christmas and Boxing Day, which move as a pair in Sydney and Toronto rather
// than collapsing the way Hong Kong's do: a Saturday Christmas puts the two on
// the Monday and Tuesday, and a Friday Christmas leaves Christmas alone and
// pushes Boxing Day past the weekend to the Monday.
xmas_pair_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    Weekday w = date_to_weekday(Year, 12, 25)
    int c = 25
    int b = 26
    if w == Weekday.FRIDAY
        b := 28
    if w == Weekday.SATURDAY
        c := 27
        b := 28
    if w == Weekday.SUNDAY
        c := 26
        b := 27
    string out = na
    if Month == 12 and Day == c
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if Month == 12 and Day == b
        out := "Boxing Day"
    out

// Australian one-off closures. The two April dates are ANZAC Day substitutes
// the exchange granted once each and has not repeated: 25 April 2010 was a
// Sunday and 2021 and 2027 are Sundays that get nothing, while 2011 is the
// collision with Easter Monday, which next recurs in 2095.
asx_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "19991231" => out := "New Year's Eve closure"
        "20100426" => out := "ANZAC Day substitute"
        "20110426" => out := "ANZAC Day substitute"
        "20220922" => out := "National day of mourning"
    out

// Canadian one-off closures. Toronto shut for two days after the September 11
// attacks and has closed unscheduled on no other day its reference records;
// it stayed open for the 2022 national day of mourning that shut Sydney.
tsx_closure_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    switch date_key(Year, Month, Day)
        "20010911" => out := "September 11 attacks"
        "20010912" => out := "September 11 attacks"
    out

// The ASX calendar's name, on New South Wales holidays. Two things a reader
// coming from another Australian state will expect and not find: there is no
// Labour Day, because the New South Wales October one is not an exchange
// holiday, and ANZAC Day does not move off a weekend: 2009, 2015, 2020 and
// 2026 are Saturdays and 2021 and 2027 Sundays, all traded on the neighbouring
// Monday. The two substitutes that do exist are one-offs in the table above.
asx_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    DateTime em = easter_sunday(Year).plus_days(1)
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == observed_monday_day(Year, 1, 1)
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if Month == 1 and Day == observed_monday_day(Year, 1, 26)
        out := "Australia Day"
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    if Month == em.Month and Day == em.Day
        out := "Easter Monday"
    if Month == 4 and Day == 25
        out := "ANZAC Day"
    if Month == 6 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 6, Weekday.MONDAY, 2)
        out := "King's Birthday"
    string xmas = xmas_pair_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(xmas)
        out := xmas
    string closed = asx_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// The TSX calendar's name. Good Friday but not Easter Monday, which is the
// difference from both London and Sydney. Victoria Day is the Monday on or
// before 24 May rather than the third Monday in May: the two formulations
// part whenever 24 May is itself a Monday, as in 2010, 2021 and 2027.
tsx_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    DateTime gf = good_friday(Year)
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == observed_monday_day(Year, 1, 1)
        out := "New Year's Day"
    // Family Day is a 2008 statute. Before it the third Monday in February was
    // an ordinary session, and 19 February 2007 is the date that proves it.
    if Month == 2 and Year >= 2008 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 2, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
        out := "Family Day"
    if Month == gf.Month and Day == gf.Day
        out := "Good Friday"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 24 - pmod(date_to_weekday(Year, 5, 24).to_int() - Weekday.MONDAY.to_int(), 7)
        out := "Victoria Day"
    if Month == 7 and Day == observed_monday_day(Year, 7, 1)
        out := "Canada Day"
    if Month == 8 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 8, Weekday.MONDAY, 1)
        out := "Civic Holiday"
    if Month == 9 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 1)
        out := "Labour Day"
    if Month == 10 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 10, Weekday.MONDAY, 2)
        out := "Thanksgiving"
    string xmas = xmas_pair_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(xmas)
        out := xmas
    string closed = tsx_closure_name(Year, Month, Day)
    if not na(closed)
        out := closed
    out

// Shanghai's calendar in full. Every closure is tabled, including the ones a
// fixed date sits inside: the exchange shuts for a block around New Year, Labour
// Day and National Day whose length is set each year rather than by rule, so
// "1 January" is not a rule here even though 1 January is always in a block.
// The names are the block a date belongs to, which is as fine a distinction as
// an unlabelled table supports.
sse_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    if in_date_table(SSE_CLOSED, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Exchange holiday"
    out

// The Bombay calendar: six fixed dates, then the table. India substitutes
// nothing at all: not one of the weekend occurrences in the modelled range moves to a
// neighbouring weekday, so the fixed dates are bare, and reporting them on a
// weekend costs nothing because is_trading_day tests the weekend first. The
// remaining two thirds of the calendar is lunar, lunisolar and Islamic, none of
// which any rule here can generate.
bse_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    if Month == 1 and Day == 26
        out := "Republic Day"
    if Month == 4 and Day == 14
        out := "Dr Ambedkar Jayanti"
    if Month == 5 and Day == 1
        out := "Maharashtra Day"
    if Month == 8 and Day == 15
        out := "Independence Day"
    if Month == 10 and Day == 2
        out := "Gandhi Jayanti"
    if Month == 12 and Day == 25
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if in_date_table(BSE_CLOSED, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Exchange holiday"
    out

// The Singapore calendar: four fixed dates with a Sunday-only substitute, then
// the table. Saturday earns nothing, as in Hong Kong. The substitution is a
// rule for these four only; the tabled lunar and Islamic holidays carry their
// observed dates, so 4 November 2013 stays a trading day even though Deepavali
// fell on the Sunday before it, which a blanket Sunday rule would have shut.
sgx_holiday_name(int Year, int Month, int Day) =>
    string out = na
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 1, 1)
        out := "New Year's Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 5, 1)
        out := "Labour Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 8, 9)
        out := "National Day"
    if hkex_fixed_hit(Year, Month, Day, 12, 25)
        out := "Christmas Day"
    if in_date_table(SGX_CLOSED, Year, Month, Day)
        out := "Exchange holiday"
    out

// @function        The name of the holiday or closure shutting an exchange on a date. This is the calendar's source: is_holiday is defined as this being non-na, so the name and the predicate cannot disagree, and the tabled unscheduled closures are named too: "September 11 attacks", "Hurricane Sandy", "National day of mourning". Names are English only, per the header. Weekends carry no name, exactly as they are not holidays.
// @param this      The date to ask about.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         The holiday's name, or na when the exchange holds a session that day and there is nothing to name.
export method holiday_name(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    string out = na
    switch ex
        Exchange.NYSE => out := nyse_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.CME => out := cme_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.LSE => out := lse_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.JPX => out := jpx_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.EUREX => out := eurex_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.HKEX => out := hkex_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.ASX => out := asx_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.TSX => out := tsx_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.SSE => out := sse_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.BSE => out := bse_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.SGX => out := sgx_holiday_name(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        Exchange.CRYPTO =>
            // Never closed, so never a name. The arm exists to say so, rather
            // than leaving a 24/7 market's answer to the switch seed.
            out := na
    out

// @function        Whether an exchange is closed for a scheduled holiday. Defined as holiday_name being non-na: one source of truth, so the predicate can never contradict the name. Weekends are not holidays; ask is_trading_day for that. Unscheduled closures are included for the past but cannot be for the future; see the closure names in the source.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         true when the exchange holds no session that day.
export method is_holiday(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    not na(this.holiday_name(ex))

// @function        The first year an exchange's calendar answers exactly. Before it the rules still run, but nothing has checked them and the tabled layers are empty, so a holiday there is the rules extrapolated backwards rather than the exchange's own record.
// @param this      The exchange.
// @returns         Calendar year, or na for a calendar with no lower bound to state. RAISES on an Exchange the switch does not handle, a library bug.
export method calendar_from(Exchange this) =>
    int out = na
    switch this
        Exchange.NYSE => out := 1976
        Exchange.LSE => out := 1976
        Exchange.CME => out := 2000
        Exchange.JPX => out := 2000
        Exchange.EUREX => out := 2006
        Exchange.HKEX => out := 2000
        Exchange.ASX => out := 1995
        Exchange.TSX => out := 1995
        Exchange.SSE => out := 2006
        Exchange.BSE => out := 2006
        Exchange.SGX => out := 2006
        // Every calendar day trades, so there is no era to bound.
        Exchange.CRYPTO => out := na
        => runtime.error("calendar_from: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        The last year an exchange's calendar answers exactly, or na when its rules extrapolate without a horizon. A calendar built from rules keeps generating dates forever and only its unscheduled closures are unpredictable; one built from a table stops when the table does, and past that it would report every tabled holiday as an ordinary trading day. That is the difference this reports.
// @param this      The exchange.
// @returns         Calendar year of the last covered date, or na when no horizon applies. RAISES on an Exchange the switch does not handle, a library bug. Comparing a year against na yields na, which is false, so `if Year > ex.calendar_through()` reads correctly as "never past it" for the unbounded calendars.
export method calendar_through(Exchange this) =>
    int out = na
    switch this
        // Rule-driven: these answer for any year. What they cannot do is
        // predict an unscheduled closure, which the header states separately.
        Exchange.NYSE => out := na
        Exchange.LSE => out := na
        Exchange.CME => out := na
        // The equinox holidays use the standard 1980-2099 approximation, so
        // the statute runs out with it.
        Exchange.JPX => out := 2099
        Exchange.EUREX => out := na
        // The lunar festivals are tabled, and the table ends where the source
        // it was read from ends.
        Exchange.HKEX => out := 2049
        Exchange.ASX => out := na
        Exchange.TSX => out := na
        // Wholly tabled, and the tables end with 2026.
        Exchange.SSE => out := 2026
        Exchange.BSE => out := 2026
        Exchange.SGX => out := 2026
        Exchange.CRYPTO => out := na
        => runtime.error("calendar_through: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        Whether an exchange is shut for a holiday, as a three-valued answer that says so when the question falls outside the calendar's range. This is what is_holiday cannot do: bool has no room for "not known", so is_holiday reads a date past the horizon as an ordinary trading day. On a tabled calendar that is a wrong answer rather than a missing one, which is the whole reason this exists. No is_ prefix, for the reason fomc_decision_day carries none: every is_ name here promises a bool.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         YES, NO, or UNKNOWN when the year lies outside calendar_from to calendar_through. UNKNOWN is a value and not na: test it against the enum or with is_yes(), never with na().
export method closed_for_holiday(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int lo = ex.calendar_from()
    int hi = ex.calendar_through()
    bool inside = true
    if not na(lo) and this.Year < lo
        inside := false
    if not na(hi) and this.Year > hi
        inside := false
    Known out = Known.UNKNOWN
    if inside
        out := Known.NO
        if this.is_holiday(ex)
            out := Known.YES
    out

// @function        Whether an exchange holds a regular session on a date: not a weekend, not a scheduled holiday. CRYPTO trades every calendar day, weekends included.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         true when the exchange is open, whether or not it closes early.
export method is_trading_day(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    bool out = false
    if ex == Exchange.CRYPTO
        // The weekend gate below is a fact about markets that close, not part
        // of the question, so crypto answers before reaching it, and Saturday
        // is an ordinary trading day rather than a special case each caller
        // has to remember.
        out := true
    else if ex == Exchange.BSE and in_date_table(BSE_OPEN_ANYWAY, this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        // Bombay has run two Saturday sessions. A weekday mask alone answers
        // no on both, and no rule generates them, so they are a positive table
        // consulted ahead of the weekend gate rather than an exception buried
        // inside it.
        out := true
    else
        Weekday dow = this.weekday()
        out := dow != Weekday.SATURDAY and dow != Weekday.SUNDAY and not this.is_holiday(ex)
    out

// @function        The next date an exchange is closed for a holiday, strictly after the date an instant falls on in that exchange's zone. Strictly after, like next_trading_day and next_fomc_after: at noon on Good Friday the answer is the next holiday, not the one in progress. Ask holiday_name what the returned date is closed for.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on the next holiday, or na when no holiday falls within a 400-calendar-day scan. na rather than a raise, because "no holiday ahead" is an answer and not a caller bug: CRYPTO never closes, so it always answers na, and every other shipped calendar closes at least once inside any 400-day window.
export next_holiday_after(int unix_ms, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, ex.zone())
    int base = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    DateTime out = na
    // The bound is fixed, so no down-count guard is needed.
    for k = 1 to 400
        if na(out)
            DateTime c = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
            if c.is_holiday(ex)
                out := c
    out

// Cumulative weekday count: weekdays from the epoch-phase Monday (day 4,
// 1970-01-05) up to but not including day number d, signed, so a difference
// of two of these counts the weekdays in any half-open day-number span. Five
// per whole week plus the clipped partial week; fdiv and pmod keep it exact
// on both sides of the epoch.
weekdays_to(int d) =>
    5 * fdiv(d - 4, 7) + math.min(pmod(d - 4, 7), 5)

// ================================ HOLIDAY ENUMERATION =======================
// Counting trading days by walking every day and asking the calendar costs one
// full holiday-chain call per day. Over a thirty-year span that is eleven
// thousand of them, and on the tabled calendars each one searches a table of a
// few kilobytes, enough to reach Pine's per-loop time limit, which is a
// runtime error rather than merely slow.
//
// The functions below invert it: propose the handful of days a calendar could
// possibly shut on, then ask the calendar about only those. The proposal is
// per-exchange and deliberately generous: every anchor is widened by the
// window its calendar can move a holiday into, and it is allowed to be wrong
// in one direction only. A day proposed that is not a holiday is discarded by
// the filter; a day never proposed is invisible, so the anchors have to be a
// superset, and they are one over every calendar's whole modelled range,
// not only the years a reference reaches.
//
// Membership itself stays where it was: every survivor is tested with the same
// is_holiday every other accessor uses, so this cannot become a second opinion
// about what a holiday is. It only decides which days are worth asking about.

// Day numbers of a dated table's entries inside a half-open day span. Split
// once for the whole span rather than once per year: a per-year split of the
// Shanghai table would cost more than the walk this replaces.
table_days_between(string table, int lo, int hi, array<int> into, int back = 0, int fwd = 0) =>
    array<string> parts = str.split(table, ",")
    if parts.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to parts.size() - 1
            string k = parts.get(i)
            // The sentinel commas give an empty first and last element, and
            // the length test drops both without a special case; real entries are
            // three characters.
            if str.length(k) == 3
                // The tables store day numbers, so membership needs no civil
                // parse at all: three digit lookups reassemble the day.
                int dn = str.pos(B36_DIGITS, str.substring(k, 0, 1)) * 1296 + str.pos(B36_DIGITS, str.substring(k, 1, 2)) * 36 + str.pos(B36_DIGITS, str.substring(k, 2, 3))
                // Widened where a calendar's derived rules can carry a
                // tabled date further, as Japan's substitute rule does to the
                // Olympic moves: 8 August 2021 is tabled and the Monday after
                // it is the holiday actually observed.
                for w = -back to fwd
                    if dn + w >= lo and dn + w < hi
                        into.push(dn + w)
    // Returns a count purely so that every arm of the switches below has the
    // same type; Pine requires it and nothing reads the number.
    into.size()

// One anchor, widened. `back` and `fwd` are how far a calendar can carry the
// holiday off its nominal date: a Sunday substitute is +1, a Saturday one +2,
// the London Christmas run reaches +3, and Victoria Day sits up to six days
// before its anchor, not after it.
push_window(array<int> into, int Year, int Month, int Day, int back, int fwd) =>
    int base = days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)
    for k = -back to fwd
        into.push(base + k)
    into.size()

// The two Japanese equinoxes, by the same approximation the statutory chain
// uses, widened for the substitute a Sunday equinox earns.
push_jpx_equinoxes(array<int> into, int Year) =>
    push_window(into, Year, 3, int(math.floor(20.8431 + 0.242194 * (Year - 1980))) - fdiv(Year - 1980, 4), 1, 2)
    push_window(into, Year, 9, int(math.floor(23.2488 + 0.242194 * (Year - 1980))) - fdiv(Year - 1980, 4), 1, 2)

// Good Friday through Easter Monday, plus a day either side.
push_easter(array<int> into, int Year) =>
    int es = days_from_civil(easter_sunday(Year).Year, easter_sunday(Year).Month, easter_sunday(Year).Day)
    for k = -3 to 2
        into.push(es + k)
    into.size()

// Every day an exchange might be shut on in one year. Generous by design: the
// filter downstream removes what is not a holiday, and only a missing day
// would be a bug.
holiday_anchors(Exchange ex, int Year, array<int> into) =>
    int n = 0
    switch ex
        Exchange.NYSE =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 1, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 1, Weekday.MONDAY, 3), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 2, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 2, Weekday.MONDAY, 3), 0, 0)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, -1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 6, 19, 1, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 7, 4, 1, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 9, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 11, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 11, Weekday.THURSDAY, 4), 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 1, 1)
        Exchange.CME =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 1, 1)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 1, 1)
        Exchange.LSE =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 3)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, 1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, -1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 8, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 8, Weekday.MONDAY, -1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 3)
        Exchange.JPX =>
            // Every statutory anchor widened by two, which is what the
            // sandwich and substitute rules can add on top of one, plus the
            // year-end closure that runs 31 December to 3 January.
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 1, 4)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 1, Weekday.MONDAY, 2), 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 2, 11, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 2, 23, 0, 2)
            n := push_jpx_equinoxes(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 4, 29, 0, 3)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 3, 0, 4)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 7, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 7, Weekday.MONDAY, 3), 0, 2)
            // Marine Day and Respect for the Aged Day were fixed dates until
            // the Happy Monday law moved them in 2003. Both forms are proposed
            // rather than era-gated here, because an anchor costs one filtered
            // test and a missing one is invisible.
            n := push_window(into, Year, 7, 20, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 9, 15, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 8, 11, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 9, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 3), 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 10, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 10, Weekday.MONDAY, 2), 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 11, 3, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 11, 23, 0, 2)
            // The Arrowhead outage is a literal in the name chain rather
            // than a table entry, so it needs an anchor of its own.
            n := push_window(into, Year, 10, 1, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 23, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 31, 1, 0)
        Exchange.EUREX =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 0)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 1, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 24, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 31, 0, 0)
        Exchange.HKEX =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 7, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 10, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 2)
        Exchange.ASX =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 26, 0, 2)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 4, 25, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 6, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 6, Weekday.MONDAY, 2), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 3)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 31, 0, 0)
        Exchange.TSX =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 2, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 2, Weekday.MONDAY, 3), 0, 0)
            n := push_easter(into, Year)
            // Victoria Day is the Monday on or before 24 May, so its window
            // runs backwards from the anchor rather than forwards.
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 24, 6, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 7, 1, 0, 2)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 8, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 8, Weekday.MONDAY, 1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 9, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 1), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 10, nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 10, Weekday.MONDAY, 2), 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 3)
        // Shanghai has no rules at all: its whole calendar is the table, and
        // the table is added for every exchange below.
        Exchange.SSE => n := into.size()
        Exchange.BSE =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 26, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 4, 14, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 1, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 8, 15, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 10, 2, 0, 0)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 0)
        Exchange.SGX =>
            n := push_window(into, Year, 1, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 5, 1, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 8, 9, 0, 1)
            n := push_window(into, Year, 12, 25, 0, 1)
        Exchange.CRYPTO => n := into.size()
        => runtime.error("holiday_anchors: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")

    n
// Every dated table an exchange's name chain consults. Kept beside
// holiday_anchors rather than inside it because a table is exact: no widening
// applies, and because forgetting one here is the same class of bug as
// forgetting an anchor.
holiday_tables(Exchange ex, int lo, int hi, array<int> into) =>
    int n = 0
    switch ex
        Exchange.NYSE => table_days_between(NYSE_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.CME => table_days_between(CME_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.LSE => table_days_between(LSE_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.JPX => table_days_between(JPX_ONE_OFF_TABLE, lo, hi, into, 0, 2)
        Exchange.EUREX => n := into.size()
        Exchange.HKEX =>
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_LNY, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_CHING_MING, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_BUDDHA, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_TUEN_NG, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_MID_AUTUMN, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_CHUNG_YEUNG, lo, hi, into)
            n := table_days_between(HKEX_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.ASX => table_days_between(ASX_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.TSX => table_days_between(TSX_CLOSED_TABLE, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.SSE => table_days_between(SSE_CLOSED, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.BSE => table_days_between(BSE_CLOSED, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.SGX => table_days_between(SGX_CLOSED, lo, hi, into)
        Exchange.CRYPTO => n := into.size()
        => runtime.error("holiday_tables: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")

    n
// The holidays an exchange observes in a half-open span of day numbers,
// ascending and without repeats. Candidates in, filtered by the calendar, out.
holiday_days_between_days(Exchange ex, int lo, int hi) =>
    array<int> out = array.new<int>()
    if hi > lo and ex != Exchange.CRYPTO
        array<int> cand = array.new<int>()
        // No modelled calendar carries a holiday out of its own year: the
        // New Year rules place even their observed Mondays inside it, so the
        // year loop needs no margin either side.
        int y0 = unix_to_date(lo * MS_DAY).Year
        int y1 = unix_to_date((hi - 1) * MS_DAY).Year
        for y = y0 to y1
            holiday_anchors(ex, y, cand)
        holiday_tables(ex, lo, hi, cand)
        cand.sort()
        int prev = na
        if cand.size() > 0
            for i = 0 to cand.size() - 1
                int d = cand.get(i)
                if d >= lo and d < hi and (na(prev) or d != prev)
                    prev := d
                    if unix_to_date(d * MS_DAY).is_holiday(ex)
                        out.push(d)
    out

// @function        Every date an exchange is closed for a holiday within a span. The market-calendar sibling of expiries_between, with the same date-keyed contract, and the walk routes through is_holiday, so this function cannot disagree with the predicate it enumerates. Weekends are not holidays and are not reported.
// @param from_ms   Start of the span, Unix milliseconds. Inclusive by date: a holiday on the civil date this instant falls on in the exchange's zone is reported, whatever the time of day.
// @param to_ms     End of the span, Unix milliseconds. Exclusive by date: a holiday on the civil date this instant falls on is not reported.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Array of DateTimes at midnight, ascending, possibly empty: CRYPTO's answer is always empty, as is any empty span's. RAISES when to_ms is before from_ms, which is not a span, and when the span exceeds 20000 calendar days, refused outright rather than silently truncated.
export holidays_between(int from_ms, int to_ms, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    if to_ms < from_ms
        runtime.error("holidays_between: the span runs backward (to_ms is before from_ms)")
    Zone tz = ex.zone()
    DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(from_ms, tz)
    DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(to_ms, tz)
    int lo = days_from_civil(a.Year, a.Month, a.Day)
    int hi = days_from_civil(b.Year, b.Month, b.Day)
    if hi - lo > 20000
        runtime.error("holidays_between: span of " + str.tostring(hi - lo) + " days exceeds the 20000-day bound")
    array<DateTime> out = array.new<DateTime>()
    array<int> days = holiday_days_between_days(ex, lo, hi)
    if days.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to days.size() - 1
            out.push(unix_to_date(days.get(i) * MS_DAY))
    out

// Whether a date is the last trading day strictly before a target day number:
// how the LSE picks its two half days. The target is a day count rather than
// a (Year, Month, Day) triple because it frequently belongs to the next year:
// the last trading day before 1 January is in the previous December, which a
// single-year triple cannot express.
last_trading_day_before(int target, DateTime d, Exchange ex) =>
    bool out = false
    if d.is_trading_day(ex)
        int here = days_from_civil(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day)
        if here < target
            bool any_later = false
            for k = 1 to 6
                DateTime c = unix_to_date((here + k) * MS_DAY)
                if here + k < target and c.is_trading_day(ex)
                    any_later := true
            out := not any_later
    out

// @function        Whether an exchange closes early on a date. Returns false on a day the exchange is shut: a closed market is not an early close.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         true when the session ends before its usual time.
export method is_early_close(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int Year = this.Year
    int Month = this.Month
    int Day = this.Day
    bool out = false
    if this.is_trading_day(ex)
        Weekday dow = this.weekday()
        int tg_day = nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 11, Weekday.THURSDAY, 4)
        switch ex
            Exchange.NYSE =>
                // Day after Thanksgiving, 3 July, and Christmas Eve: each only
                // when it is genuinely a trading day, which the guard above has
                // already established.
                // The Friday half day is standing policy only from 1992; before
                // that the NYSE ran a full session on it, so the era gate here
                // is the same kind of gate the 3 July branch below carries.
                if Month == 11 and Day == tg_day + 1 and Year >= 1992
                    out := true
                // The 3 July half day is standing policy only from 2003; the
                // handful of earlier occurrences are in the table above.
                if Month == 7 and Day == 3 and Year >= 2003
                    out := true
                if Month == 12 and Day == 24
                    out := true
                if in_date_table(NYSE_EARLY_ADHOC, Year, Month, Day)
                    out := true
            Exchange.CME =>
                // The US federal holidays CME trades through, plus the two
                // eves. Juneteenth is deliberately absent: CME runs a full
                // session on it.
                if Month == 1 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 1, Weekday.MONDAY, 3) and Year >= 1998
                    out := true
                if Month == 2 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 2, Weekday.MONDAY, 3)
                    out := true
                if Month == 5 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 5, Weekday.MONDAY, -1)
                    out := true
                if Month == 9 and Day == nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 9, Weekday.MONDAY, 1)
                    out := true
                if Month == 11 and (Day == tg_day or Day == tg_day + 1)
                    out := true
                if Month == 7 and Day == 4
                    out := true
                if Month == 7 and Day == 3 and dow == Weekday.FRIDAY
                    out := true
                if Month == 7 and Day == 5 and dow == Weekday.MONDAY
                    out := true
                if Month == 12 and Day == 24
                    out := true
            Exchange.LSE =>
                // Not "24 and 31 December" but the last trading day before
                // Christmas and the last of the year, which is why the dates
                // wander when those fall on a weekend.
                out := last_trading_day_before(days_from_civil(Year, 12, 25), this, ex) or last_trading_day_before(days_from_civil(Year + 1, 1, 1), this, ex)
                if Month < 12
                    out := false
                if in_date_table(LSE_FULL_DAY_ANYWAY, Year, Month, Day)
                    out := false
            Exchange.JPX =>
                // No half days in the modelled era: the old half-day first
                // and last sessions of the year predate 2000.
                out := false
            Exchange.EUREX =>
                // The exchange_calendars XEUR reference shows exactly one
                // close time on every session it covers: 22:00 Berlin.
                out := false
            Exchange.HKEX =>
                // Christmas Eve and New Year's Eve, on the date itself and
                // never moved off it: 24 December 2022 fell on a Saturday and
                // the Friday before it ran a full session. The is_trading_day
                // guard above has already excluded the weekend. Lunar New
                // Year's Eve joined them in 2013 and is tabled, because its
                // date is lunisolar like the holiday it precedes.
                if Month == 12 and (Day == 24 or Day == 31)
                    out := true
                if in_date_table(HKEX_LNY_EVE, Year, Month, Day)
                    out := true
            Exchange.ASX =>
                // Not "24 and 31 December" but the last session before each,
                // the London shape: 23 December 2011 and 22 December 2017 are
                // half days because the 24th fell on a weekend. Standing
                // policy from 2010; before that the eves ran full sessions.
                out := last_trading_day_before(days_from_civil(Year, 12, 25), this, ex) or last_trading_day_before(days_from_civil(Year + 1, 1, 1), this, ex)
                if Month < 12 or Year < 2010
                    out := false
                if in_date_table(ASX_FULL_DAY_ANYWAY, Year, Month, Day)
                    out := false
            Exchange.TSX =>
                // Christmas Eve alone, on the date itself: Toronto does not
                // roll the half day back to the Friday when the 24th falls on
                // a weekend, and it shortens no session at the year end.
                // Standing policy from 2010.
                if Month == 12 and Day == 24 and Year >= 2010
                    out := true
            Exchange.SSE =>
                out := false
            Exchange.BSE =>
                // The Muhurat evening session on Diwali is a separate special
                // session rather than a shortened one, and is not modelled.
                out := false
            Exchange.SGX =>
                out := false
            Exchange.CRYPTO =>
                // A market that never closes cannot close early.
                out := false
    out

// The NYSE half day ended at 14:00 until 1993 and at 13:00 since. Both eras
// are inside the range the calendar tables cover, so the hour has to be a
// function of the year rather than a constant.
nyse_early_minute(int Year) =>
    int out = 13 * 60
    if Year < 1993
        out := 14 * 60
    out

// The TSE close moved from 15:00 to 15:30 on 5 November 2024. A year is the
// granularity here, as with the NYSE early-close hour above, so 2024 is
// modelled at the old close and is wrong by 30 minutes from 5 November to
// the year's last session, the smaller error than carrying the new close
// back through ten months that ended at 15:00.
jpx_close_minute(int Year) =>
    int out = 15 * 60 + 30
    if Year <= 2024
        out := 15 * 60
    out

// The Hong Kong open moved from 10:00 to 09:30 on 7 March 2011 and the half
// day moved with it, from 12:30 to 12:00. A year is the granularity here, the
// same trade jpx_close_minute makes: the 43 sessions from 1 January to 4 March
// 2011 are modelled half an hour early, which is the smaller error than
// carrying the old open through the nine months of that year that used the new
// one.
hkex_open_minute(int Year) =>
    int out = 9 * 60 + 30
    if Year < 2011
        out := 10 * 60
    out

hkex_early_minute(int Year) =>
    int out = 12 * 60
    if Year < 2011
        out := 12 * 60 + 30
    out

// Session boundaries in local minutes past midnight: regular open, regular
// close, early close. Takes the year because the NYSE early close and the
// JPX regular close both moved. Every member has an explicit arm and the
// default RAISES, for the same reason zone() does: with NYSE as an implicit
// seed, a future Exchange member would silently inherit 09:30-16:00 New York
// hours.
session_minutes(Exchange ex, int Year) =>
    int o = 0
    int c = 0
    int e = 0
    switch ex
        Exchange.NYSE =>
            o := 9 * 60 + 30
            c := 16 * 60
            e := nyse_early_minute(Year)
        Exchange.LSE =>
            o := 8 * 60
            c := 16 * 60 + 30
            e := 12 * 60 + 30
        Exchange.CME =>
            // CME Globex equity index: opens 17:00 Chicago on the previous
            // calendar day and runs to 16:00. Expressed as a negative open so
            // the rest of the arithmetic stays uniform.
            o := -7 * 60
            c := 16 * 60
            e := 12 * 60
        Exchange.JPX =>
            o := 9 * 60
            c := jpx_close_minute(Year)
            // No half days, so is_early_close never selects e for JPX; it is
            // set to the close so that if that ever changes it degrades to
            // "no shortening" rather than to a NYSE hour.
            e := c
        Exchange.EUREX =>
            o := 8 * 60
            c := 22 * 60
            e := c
        Exchange.HKEX =>
            o := hkex_open_minute(Year)
            c := 16 * 60
            e := hkex_early_minute(Year)
        Exchange.ASX =>
            o := 10 * 60
            c := 16 * 60
            // 14:10, not 14:00: the closing auction runs to ten past.
            e := 14 * 60 + 10
        Exchange.TSX =>
            o := 9 * 60 + 30
            c := 16 * 60
            e := 13 * 60
        // None of the three has a half day, so e aliases c rather than
        // naming a time no session here ever ends at.
        Exchange.SSE =>
            o := 9 * 60 + 30
            c := 15 * 60
            e := c
        Exchange.BSE =>
            o := 9 * 60 + 15
            c := 15 * 60 + 30
            e := c
        Exchange.SGX =>
            o := 9 * 60
            c := 17 * 60
            e := c
        Exchange.CRYPTO =>
            // 00:00 to 24:00. The 1440 is deliberate: a next-midnight close,
            // which session_close rolls onto the following day, since hour 24
            // does not exist on a clock.
            o := 0
            c := 24 * 60
            e := c
        => runtime.error("session_minutes: unhandled Exchange. This is a library bug")
    [o, c, e]

// @function        The instant an exchange's session opens on a date. For CME this is 17:00 Chicago on the previous calendar day, because a Globex session opens the evening before the date it settles on.
// @param this      The trading date.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when the exchange is closed that day.
export method session_open(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int out = na
    if this.is_trading_day(ex)
        [o, c, e] = session_minutes(ex, this.Year)
        int base_day = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        int mins = o
        int shift_days = 0
        if o < 0
            shift_days := -1
            mins := o + 24 * 60
        DateTime d = unix_to_date((base_day + shift_days) * MS_DAY)
        Zone tz = ex.zone()
        out := tz.to_unix(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day, int(mins / 60), mins % 60)
    out

// @function        The instant an exchange's session closes on a date, accounting for early closes.
// @param this      The trading date.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when the exchange is closed that day.
export method session_close(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int out = na
    if this.is_trading_day(ex)
        [o, c, e] = session_minutes(ex, this.Year)
        int mins = c
        if this.is_early_close(ex)
            mins := e
        // A 24:00 close is the following midnight: hour 24 does not exist, so
        // the day advances and the clock reads 00:00. The counterpart of
        // session_open's negative-open shift; only CRYPTO uses it.
        int shift_days = 0
        if mins >= 1440
            shift_days := 1
            mins := mins - 1440
        DateTime d = unix_to_date((days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day) + shift_days) * MS_DAY)
        Zone tz = ex.zone()
        out := tz.to_unix(d.Year, d.Month, d.Day, int(mins / 60), mins % 60)
    out

// There is no instant-plus-Exchange layer: no is_market_open(t, ex), no
// time_to_close(t, ex). Those questions belong to Session:
//   session_of(SessionId.US_REGULAR).is_open(t)
//   session_of(SessionId.US_REGULAR).time_to_close(t)
//   session_of(SessionId.US_REGULAR).is_first_bar(time, time_close)
//   session_of(SessionId.US_REGULAR).is_last_bar(time, time_close)
// A Session names its calendar explicitly rather than inferring one from an
// Exchange, which matters for CME, where RTH and ETH are different sessions
// on the same calendar and the library should not pick one on the caller's
// behalf.

// ------------------------------------------------------- business days --

// @function        Move forward or backward a number of trading days, skipping weekends and holidays. Zero returns the same date whether or not it trades; use adjusted for that. Named plus_trading_days rather than add_trading_days to join the nine plus_* methods above: one family of movers, one verb.
// @param this      The starting date.
// @param n         Trading days to move; negative moves backward.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on the resulting trading day. RAISES when the bounded walk of |n| * 2 + 30 calendar days does not contain |n| trading days. No shipped calendar can do that, so reaching the error means the calendar data is wrong.
export method plus_trading_days(DateTime this, int n, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int cur = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
    int step = 1
    if n < 0
        step := -1
    int remaining = math.abs(n)
    // Bounded so a mis-specified calendar cannot spin forever, but scaled to
    // the request rather than fixed: a flat 400 calendar days holds only ~274
    // trading days, so a request for two years of them would walk off the end
    // of the loop. Roughly 1.45 calendar days per trading day is the worst
    // real case, so double plus a holiday-run margin clears any calendar;
    // running out means the calendar is wrong, and that raises rather than
    // returning the cursor, which would be neither the answer nor necessarily
    // a trading day.
    int cap = math.abs(n) * 2 + 30
    for i = 1 to cap
        if remaining > 0
            cur := cur + step
            DateTime c = unix_to_date(cur * MS_DAY)
            if c.is_trading_day(ex)
                remaining := remaining - 1
    if remaining > 0
        runtime.error("plus_trading_days: could not resolve " + str.tostring(n) + " trading days within " + str.tostring(cap) + " calendar days")
    unix_to_date(cur * MS_DAY)

// @function        Move backward a number of trading days. Delegates to plus_trading_days with -n, so it shares the bounded walk, the zero-returns-the-same-date rule, and the RAISE.
// @param this      The starting date.
// @param n         Trading days to move back; a negative n moves forward.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on the resulting trading day. RAISES exactly as plus_trading_days does when the bounded walk cannot resolve.
export method minus_trading_days(DateTime this, int n, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    this.plus_trading_days(-n, ex)

// @function        The previous trading day strictly before a date.
// @param this      The starting date.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on that trading day. RAISES exactly as plus_trading_days does; this is its one-step walk.
export method prev_trading_day(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    this.plus_trading_days(-1, ex)

// @function        The next trading day strictly after a date, skipping weekends and scheduled holidays. The exact inverse of prev_trading_day: both route through is_trading_day, so both honour the ad-hoc closure table that no rule generates: the 2001 September closure and Hurricane Sandy are trading days to a rule and are not trading days here.
// @param this      The starting date.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on that trading day. RAISES exactly as plus_trading_days does; this is its one-step walk.
export method next_trading_day(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    this.plus_trading_days(1, ex)

// @function        Count of trading days between two dates. The span counted is always half-open on the earlier of the two (the earlier date counts when it trades, the later never does), and the result is negated when other comes first. Half-open so consecutive spans add up; anchored on the earlier date rather than on `this` so the function is exactly antisymmetric, as a signed difference has to be. One consequence: a reversed pair counts its end, so with 1 January a holiday, trading_days_between(5 Jan, 1 Jan) is -1 and not -2.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Signed count; negative when other is earlier.
export method trading_days_between(DateTime this, DateTime other, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int a = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
    int b = days_from_civil(other.Year, other.Month, other.Day)
    int lo = math.min(a, b)
    int hi = math.max(a, b)
    // Closed form rather than a walk: the weekdays in the span, less the
    // holidays that land on one. Weekend holidays are already absent from the
    // weekday count and must not be subtracted twice.
    int n = weekdays_to(hi) - weekdays_to(lo)
    if ex == Exchange.BSE
        // The two Saturday sessions the weekday count cannot see.
        array<int> extra = array.new<int>()
        table_days_between(BSE_OPEN_ANYWAY, lo, hi, extra)
        n := n + extra.size()
    array<int> hol = holiday_days_between_days(ex, lo, hi)
    if hol.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to hol.size() - 1
            Weekday w = weekday_from_int(pmod(hol.get(i) + EPOCH_DOW, 7))
            if w != Weekday.SATURDAY and w != Weekday.SUNDAY
                n := n - 1
    int out = n
    if b < a
        out := -n
    out

// @function        Count of weekdays (Monday through Friday) between two dates, closed form: no loop and no calendar. This is exactly what trading_days_between degenerates to when no exchange calendar applies, and it keeps that function's whole contract: the span counted is half-open on the earlier date (the earlier date counts when it is a weekday, the later never does), and the result is negated when other comes first, so the function is exactly antisymmetric.
// @param this      The starting DateTime.
// @param other     The ending DateTime.
// @returns         Signed count; negative when other is earlier.
export method weekdays_between(DateTime this, DateTime other) =>
    int a = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
    int b = days_from_civil(other.Year, other.Month, other.Day)
    int n = weekdays_to(math.max(a, b)) - weekdays_to(math.min(a, b))
    int out = n
    if b < a
        out := -n
    out

// @function        The first trading day of a month.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on that day. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12], failing in the caller's own terms rather than from days_in_month a call deeper. RAISES when the whole month holds no trading day, which no shipped calendar can produce and therefore means the calendar is wrong.
export first_trading_day_of_month(int Year, int Month, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("first_trading_day_of_month: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    int base = days_from_civil(Year, Month, 1)
    DateTime out = na
    // The range is the month's literal length, never empty, so no down-count
    // guard is needed.
    for k = 0 to days_in_month(Year, Month) - 1
        if na(out)
            DateTime c = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
            if c.is_trading_day(ex)
                out := c
    if na(out)
        runtime.error("first_trading_day_of_month: no trading day in " + str.tostring(Year) + "-" + str.tostring(Month) + ". The exchange calendar is wrong")
    out

// @function        The last trading day of a month. The month-end anchor of turn-of-month effects and the settlement date of anything that marks on the month's final print.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on that day. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12], and when the whole month holds no trading day: impossible with the shipped calendars, so it signals broken calendar data.
export last_trading_day_of_month(int Year, int Month, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("last_trading_day_of_month: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    int base = days_from_civil(Year, Month, 1)
    DateTime out = na
    // The same forward walk as first_trading_day_of_month with the last match
    // kept, rather than a backward range a reader has to think twice about.
    // The range is the month's literal length, never empty, so no down-count
    // guard is needed.
    for k = 0 to days_in_month(Year, Month) - 1
        DateTime c = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
        if c.is_trading_day(ex)
            out := c
    if na(out)
        runtime.error("last_trading_day_of_month: no trading day in " + str.tostring(Year) + "-" + str.tostring(Month) + ". The exchange calendar is wrong")
    out

// @function        How many trading days a month holds. The monthly slice of the ACT/252 denominator, and the per-month budget number for anything drawn once per session.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         The count: 19 to 23 on the equity calendars, up to 31 under CRYPTO, where every calendar day trades. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12].
export trading_days_in_month(int Year, int Month, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("trading_days_in_month: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    int base = days_from_civil(Year, Month, 1)
    int n = 0
    // The range is the month's literal length, never empty, so no down-count
    // guard is needed.
    for k = 0 to days_in_month(Year, Month) - 1
        DateTime c = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
        if c.is_trading_day(ex)
            n := n + 1
    n

// @function        The ordinal of a date among its month's trading days: 1 on the month's first trading day, trading_days_in_month on its last. The number "T+n of the month" strategies key on.
// @param this      The date to ask about.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Ordinal in [1, trading_days_in_month(...)], or na when the date is not itself a trading day: a Saturday holds no position in a sequence it is not part of, and 0 would be an in-band stand-in for "none", which the header's error rules forbid.
export method trading_day_of_month(DateTime this, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int out = na
    if this.is_trading_day(ex)
        int base = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, 1)
        int here = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
        int n = 0
        // The range runs from the 1st to the receiver itself, so it is never
        // empty and needs no down-count guard.
        for d = base to here
            DateTime c = unix_to_date(d * MS_DAY)
            if c.is_trading_day(ex)
                n := n + 1
        out := n
    out

// @function        Roll a date onto a trading day using an ISDA business-day convention. The modified variants refuse to cross a month boundary and turn back instead, which is what makes them "modified". Named as a participle, like normalized() and negated(): it returns a rolled date and leaves the receiver alone.
// @param this      The date to roll.
// @param conv      The convention.
// @param ex        The exchange. Default NYSE.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on the adjusted date: midnight, not this receiver's time of day, because a business-day roll is a question about the day. RAISES if the 30-day walk finds no trading day; the shipped calendars cannot produce that, so hitting it means the calendar data is broken.
export method adjusted(DateTime this, BusinessDay conv, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    int Year = this.Year
    int Month = this.Month
    int Day = this.Day
    DateTime out = unix_to_date(days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day) * MS_DAY)
    if conv != BusinessDay.UNADJUSTED and not this.is_trading_day(ex)
        bool forward = conv == BusinessDay.FOLLOWING or conv == BusinessDay.MOD_FOLLOWING
        int step = -1
        if forward
            step := 1
        int cur = days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)
        for i = 1 to 30
            DateTime c = unix_to_date(cur * MS_DAY)
            if not c.is_trading_day(ex)
                cur := cur + step
        out := unix_to_date(cur * MS_DAY)
        // Unreachable with the calendars this library ships: none of them
        // contains thirty consecutive non-trading days. Reaching here means
        // the calendar data is broken, so it raises rather than returning the
        // cursor, which would be a well-formed date that is not the answer
        // and need not even be a trading day. plus_trading_days raises on the
        // same grounds.
        if not out.is_trading_day(ex)
            runtime.error("adjusted: no trading day within 30 days of " + str.tostring(Year) + "-" + str.tostring(Month) + "-" + str.tostring(Day) + ". The exchange calendar is wrong")
        bool modified = conv == BusinessDay.MOD_FOLLOWING or conv == BusinessDay.MOD_PRECEDING
        if modified and out.Month != Month
            // Crossed the month; go the other way from the original date.
            int back = days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day)
            for i = 1 to 30
                DateTime c = unix_to_date(back * MS_DAY)
                if not c.is_trading_day(ex)
                    back := back - step
            out := unix_to_date(back * MS_DAY)
            if not out.is_trading_day(ex)
                runtime.error("adjusted: no trading day within 30 days back from " + str.tostring(Year) + "-" + str.tostring(Month) + "-" + str.tostring(Day) + ". The exchange calendar is wrong")
    out

// ---------------------------------------------------------- day counts --

// The 30/360 family differs only in how it flattens a 31st, so the shared
// numerator lives here and each convention supplies its own adjusted days.
d30_numerator(int Y1, int M1, int d1, int Y2, int M2, int d2) =>
    360 * (Y2 - Y1) + 30 * (M2 - M1) + (d2 - d1)

// @function        Year fraction between two instants under a day-count convention. ACT/365F and ACT/360 use exact elapsed milliseconds, which is what an option tenor needs; the 30/360 family and ACT/ACT are bond conventions evaluated on civil dates; ACT/252 counts trading days on the given exchange.
// @param t0        Start instant, Unix milliseconds.
// @param t1        End instant, Unix milliseconds.
// @param basis     The day-count convention. Default ACT/365F.
// @param ex        The exchange this tenor is measured in: its calendar for ACT/252, and its clock for every convention that counts days rather than milliseconds. Default NYSE. ACT/365F and ACT/360 are pure elapsed milliseconds and ignore it entirely. Under CRYPTO, ACT/252 counts every calendar day: see the DayCount notes; the convention crypto actually uses is ACT/365F.
// @returns         Year fraction; negative when t1 is before t0.
export year_fraction(int t0, int t1, DayCount basis = DayCount.ACT_365F, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    // One rule instead of a per-convention default: the endpoints always
    // resolve in the market's own zone.
    Zone civil = ex.zone()
    DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(t0, civil)
    DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(t1, civil)
    float out = 0.0
    switch basis
        DayCount.ACT_365F => out := (t1 - t0) / (365.0 * MS_DAY)
        DayCount.ACT_360 => out := (t1 - t0) / (360.0 * MS_DAY)
        DayCount.D30_360 =>
            // ISDA 30/360 Bond Basis: a 31st becomes a 30th, and the end date
            // only flattens when the start date already has.
            int d1 = a.Day
            int d2 = b.Day
            if d1 == 31
                d1 := 30
            if d2 == 31 and d1 == 30
                d2 := 30
            out := d30_numerator(a.Year, a.Month, d1, b.Year, b.Month, d2) / 360.0
        DayCount.D30E_360 =>
            // 30E/360 flattens both ends unconditionally.
            int d1 = a.Day
            int d2 = b.Day
            if d1 == 31
                d1 := 30
            if d2 == 31
                d2 := 30
            out := d30_numerator(a.Year, a.Month, d1, b.Year, b.Month, d2) / 360.0
        DayCount.ACT_ACT_ISDA =>
            // Days falling in a leap year are divided by 366 and the rest by
            // 365, so the two halves of a span straddling New Year differ.
            int da = days_from_civil(a.Year, a.Month, a.Day)
            int db = days_from_civil(b.Year, b.Month, b.Day)
            int lo = math.min(da, db)
            int hi = math.max(da, db)
            float acc = 0.0
            DateTime clo = unix_to_date(lo * MS_DAY)
            DateTime chi = unix_to_date(hi * MS_DAY)
            if clo.Year == chi.Year
                acc := (hi - lo) / (days_in_year(clo.Year) + 0.0)
            else
                acc := (days_from_civil(clo.Year + 1, 1, 1) - lo) / (days_in_year(clo.Year) + 0.0)
                acc := acc + (hi - days_from_civil(chi.Year, 1, 1)) / (days_in_year(chi.Year) + 0.0)
                // Same guard: adjacent years leave an empty range, and Pine
                // would count down through it rather than skip it.
                if chi.Year - 1 >= clo.Year + 1
                    for y = clo.Year + 1 to chi.Year - 1
                        acc := acc + 1.0
            out := acc
            if db < da
                out := -acc
        DayCount.ACT_252 =>
            out := a.trading_days_between(b, ex) / 252.0
    out

// @function        The whole-day count a convention measures between two instants: the numerator year_fraction divides, exposed because for the 30/360 family it is not recoverable from the fraction alone. The ACT conventions count actual civil days between the endpoints resolved in the exchange's zone; the 30/360 pair counts its flattened numerator with the same end-date rules year_fraction applies; ACT/252 counts trading days. Two caveats: year_fraction's ACT/365F and ACT/360 divide exact elapsed milliseconds rather than this whole-day count, so days over 365 reproduces the fraction only when both endpoints share a wall-clock time; and ACT/ACT ISDA has no single denominator, so its count is the actual days but no one division rebuilds the fraction.
// @param t0        Start instant, Unix milliseconds.
// @param t1        End instant, Unix milliseconds.
// @param basis     The day-count convention. Default ACT/365F.
// @param ex        The exchange whose zone resolves the endpoints into civil dates, and whose calendar counts ACT/252. Default NYSE.
// @returns         Signed day count; negative when t1 is before t0.
export day_count_days(int t0, int t1, DayCount basis = DayCount.ACT_365F, Exchange ex = Exchange.NYSE) =>
    Zone civil = ex.zone()
    DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(t0, civil)
    DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(t1, civil)
    int out = 0
    switch basis
        DayCount.ACT_365F => out := a.days_between(b)
        DayCount.ACT_360 => out := a.days_between(b)
        DayCount.ACT_ACT_ISDA => out := a.days_between(b)
        DayCount.D30_360 =>
            // Same flattening as year_fraction's D30_360 arm, applied to t0's
            // date as the start whichever endpoint is earlier, mirroring that
            // arm exactly is the contract, so the two can never disagree.
            int d1 = a.Day
            int d2 = b.Day
            if d1 == 31
                d1 := 30
            if d2 == 31 and d1 == 30
                d2 := 30
            out := d30_numerator(a.Year, a.Month, d1, b.Year, b.Month, d2)
        DayCount.D30E_360 =>
            int d1 = a.Day
            int d2 = b.Day
            if d1 == 31
                d1 := 30
            if d2 == 31
                d2 := 30
            out := d30_numerator(a.Year, a.Month, d1, b.Year, b.Month, d2)
        DayCount.ACT_252 => out := a.trading_days_between(b, ex)
    out

// ============================================================================
// EXPIRIES
//
// Five listed cycles under one ExpiryKind, so a scanner takes the cycle as
// data. DAILY is the 0DTE reality: SPX, NDX and XSP list an expiry every
// session, so that cycle is the trading calendar itself. WEEKLY, MONTHLY and
// QUARTERLY are the classic equity cycles, and every one of the four settles
// at the close, which is why they share next_expiry_after and its Hour
// parameter. VIX is the odd one out in both dimensions at once: its date is
// anchored in the following month (the Wednesday thirty days before that
// month's third Friday), and it settles in the morning, at the 09:30 New York
// SPX opening auction that prints the SOQ. A close-shaped Hour cannot name
// that moment, so VIX keeps its own instant pair: vix_settlement and
// next_vix_settlement_after, and joins the others where only the date
// matters: is_expiry_day and expiries_between.
// ============================================================================

// @enum             A listed expiry cycle. The first four are the p.m.-settled US equity cycles and share a close-shaped clock; VIX is a.m.-settled off the following month's third Friday, which is why it alone keeps its own instant functions; see the section banner above.
// @field DAILY      Every trading day: SPX, NDX and XSP list an expiry each session, so this cycle is the trading calendar itself.
// @field WEEKLY     The week's Friday, stepped back to the previous trading day when that Friday does not trade.
// @field MONTHLY    The classic third-Friday monthly, stepped to the previous day when that Friday is a scheduled holiday.
// @field QUARTERLY  The monthly of March, June, September and December, the triple-witching months.
// @field VIX        The VIX final-settlement cycle: the Wednesday thirty days before the following month's third Friday, stepped to the previous business day on the two-branch holiday rule.
export enum ExpiryKind
    DAILY = "Daily"
    WEEKLY = "Weekly"
    MONTHLY = "Monthly"
    QUARTERLY = "Quarterly"
    VIX = "VIX"

// @function        The expiry of the weekly contract covering a date: that week's Friday, rolled back to the previous trading day when the Friday does not trade.
// @param this      Any date in the week.
// @returns         A DateTime at midnight on the weekly expiry date.
export method weekly_expiry(DateTime this) =>
    int here = days_from_civil(this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
    int fwd = this.weekday().days_until(Weekday.FRIDAY)
    DateTime fri = unix_to_date((here + fwd) * MS_DAY)
    DateTime out = fri
    if not fri.is_trading_day()
        out := fri.prev_trading_day()
    out

// @function        VIX final settlement day for a contract month: the Wednesday thirty days before the following month's third Friday, stepped to the business day immediately preceding it when that Wednesday or that Friday is a Cboe holiday. Both holiday branches occur in practice: Good Friday on the third Friday moved 2022-03 and 2025-03 to Tuesday, Juneteenth on the computed Wednesday moved 2024-06, and Juneteenth observed on the Friday moves 2026-05. The rule reproduces every settlement on the published Cboe calendars 2021-2026, the four Tuesday exceptions included. Scheduled-holiday rules only, like monthly_expiry_day and for the same reason: the exchange knew the calendar when it listed the contract, and an unscheduled closure moves settlement by circular, which no rule predicts. The Cboe holiday set is the NYSE rule set this library already carries, Juneteenth epoch 2022 and the Saturday-observed-Friday rule included: the 2026-05 and projected 2027-05 exceptions both hang on those two details. Source: the Cboe VIX fact-sheet wording plus circular C2024051300.
// @param Year      Calendar year of the settlement.
// @param Month     Month of the settlement, 1-12. Also the contract month: the anchor Friday lives in the following month, but thirty days back always lands in this one, so the walked answer cannot leave it either.
// @returns         Day of month on which the VIX contract settles: mid-month, a Wednesday unless a holiday stepped it. RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12]: a 13th month would compute the third Friday of a 14th, and days_in_month would then refuse a call the caller never made. RAISES when no business day precedes the computed Wednesday within a week: a full week of consecutive mid-month closures does not happen, so this signals a broken holiday table.
export vix_settlement_day(int Year, int Month) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("vix_settlement_day: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    int y2 = Year
    int m2 = Month + 1
    if m2 > 12
        m2 := 1
        y2 := y2 + 1
    // Thirty days back from a Friday is a Wednesday by identity: 30 is two
    // days past four whole weeks, so W needs no weekday check. When a
    // holiday steps settlement to Tuesday, the constituent SPX options are 31
    // days out rather than 30: the 30-day span anchors the Wednesday and is
    // not re-anchored by the step (Cboe circular C2024051300 says so in as
    // many words for June 2024).
    int f = days_from_civil(y2, m2, nth_weekday_of_month(y2, m2, Weekday.FRIDAY, 3))
    int w = f - 30
    DateTime wd = unix_to_date(w * MS_DAY)
    DateTime fd = unix_to_date(f * MS_DAY)
    int cur = w
    if nyse_rule_holiday(wd.Year, wd.Month, wd.Day) or nyse_rule_holiday(fd.Year, fd.Month, fd.Day)
        // "Business day immediately preceding" walks, it does not subtract:
        // every realized case lands on the Tuesday, but the rule's wording is
        // a walk, and a Monday holiday adjacent to a Tuesday one would expose
        // the difference. The bound is fixed, so no down-count guard is
        // needed.
        bool walking = true
        for k = 1 to 7
            if walking
                DateTime c = unix_to_date((w - k) * MS_DAY)
                Weekday dow = c.weekday()
                bool closed = dow == Weekday.SATURDAY or dow == Weekday.SUNDAY or nyse_rule_holiday(c.Year, c.Month, c.Day)
                if not closed
                    cur := w - k
                    walking := false
        if walking
            runtime.error("vix_settlement_day: no business day in the week before the computed Wednesday. The holiday calendar is wrong")
    DateTime res = unix_to_date(cur * MS_DAY)
    res.Day

// @function        Unix timestamp of a VIX final settlement: the Special Opening Quotation moment, when Cboe's opening auction in the constituent SPX series prints the settlement value. VIX is a.m.-settled: the default is 09:30 New York, the SPX opening auction, the same instant as Cboe's native 08:30 Chicago under both standard and daylight time, not 16:00, which is why this function does not share monthly_expiry's default. It takes a Minute because the settlement falls on the half hour; the close-settled expiries need none. Two trading facts a caller may want alongside the instant: the expiring VX future stops trading at 08:00 Chicago that morning, and the last day to trade expiring VIX options is the business day before this date.
// @param Year      Calendar year of the settlement.
// @param Month     Month of the settlement, 1-12.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the settlement moment. Default 9.
// @param Minute    Local minute of the settlement moment. Default 30.
// @param z         The zone the settlement time is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK, matching the convention here that US-listed expiry times are quoted in New York.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, resolved through Resolver.COMPATIBLE. At the 09:30 default no US transition is reachable, so the resolver never fires. RAISES as vix_settlement_day does, on a Month outside [1, 12].
export vix_settlement(int Year, int Month, int Hour = 9, int Minute = 30, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    z.to_unix(Year, Month, vix_settlement_day(Year, Month), Hour, Minute)

// @function        Whether a date is an expiry of a given cycle. DAILY answers is_trading_day on the US listing calendar: SPX, NDX and XSP list an expiry every session, and a day the market never opened settles nothing, so the tabled unscheduled closures answer false. WEEKLY and MONTHLY are the weekly_expiry and monthly_expiry_day rules; QUARTERLY is MONTHLY confined to the four witching months; VIX is the monthly VIX settlement date by vix_settlement_day's rule; the weekly VX cycle is not modelled.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @param kind      The expiry cycle to test against. Default MONTHLY, the classic third-Friday cycle.
// @returns         true when the date is an expiry of that cycle. RAISES on an ExpiryKind the switch does not handle, a library bug, and a loud one by design, because a new cycle silently answering false would read as "never expires".
export method is_expiry_day(DateTime this, ExpiryKind kind = ExpiryKind.MONTHLY) =>
    bool out = false
    switch kind
        ExpiryKind.DAILY =>
            out := this.is_trading_day()
        ExpiryKind.WEEKLY =>
            DateTime w = this.weekly_expiry()
            out := w.Year == this.Year and w.Month == this.Month and w.Day == this.Day
        ExpiryKind.MONTHLY =>
            out := this.Day == monthly_expiry_day(this.Year, this.Month)
        ExpiryKind.QUARTERLY =>
            bool witching = this.Month == 3 or this.Month == 6 or this.Month == 9 or this.Month == 12
            out := witching and this.Day == monthly_expiry_day(this.Year, this.Month)
        ExpiryKind.VIX =>
            out := this.Day == vix_settlement_day(this.Year, this.Month)
        => runtime.error("is_expiry_day: unhandled ExpiryKind. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        Whether a date is a quarterly expiry, when index futures, index options and equity options all expire together. The QUARTERLY arm of is_expiry_day under its traditional name; one rule, two spellings, and this one routes through the other so they cannot drift apart.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @returns         true on a March, June, September or December expiry.
export method is_triple_witching(DateTime this) =>
    this.is_expiry_day(ExpiryKind.QUARTERLY)

// @function        Unix timestamp of a quarterly expiry.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @param Q         Quarter, 1-4.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the close. Default 16.
// @param z         The zone the close time is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK. Forwarded to monthly_expiry, which is the only reason a quarterly expiry could be computed in New York alone.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds. RAISES when Q is outside [1, 4]. Q is not a month: a 5 would multiply into month 15, which days_in_month refuses several calls deeper, naming a function the caller never invoked. Checking the bound here fails in the caller's own terms.
export quarterly_expiry(int Year, int Q, int Hour = 16, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    if Q < 1 or Q > 4
        runtime.error("quarterly_expiry: Q must be in [1, 4], got " + str.tostring(Q))
    monthly_expiry(Year, Q * 3, Hour, z)

// @function        The next expiry of a close-settled cycle strictly after an instant. One function rather than one per cycle because the four equity cycles genuinely share this signature: all four settle at the close, so one Hour quotes them all. VIX does not; see kind.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the close. Default 16.
// @param z         The zone the instant is read in and the close time is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK.
// @param kind      The expiry cycle to walk. Default MONTHLY, the classic third-Friday cycle. RAISES on ExpiryKind.VIX: VIX settles at 09:30 in the morning, an instant a close-shaped Hour cannot name, and silently ignoring the Hour would be worse than an error; use next_vix_settlement_after, which quotes the settlement moment in its own terms.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds of the next expiry of that cycle. RAISES on ExpiryKind.VIX, see kind. RAISES under DAILY when a 30-day walk finds no trading day, which only broken calendar data can cause.
export next_expiry_after(int unix_ms, int Hour = 16, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK, ExpiryKind kind = ExpiryKind.MONTHLY) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z)
    int out = na
    switch kind
        ExpiryKind.DAILY =>
            // Today's close counts when it is still ahead; k = 0 asks exactly
            // that, and every later trading day's close is ahead by
            // construction. The bound is fixed, so no down-count guard is
            // needed, and the longest real closure run is far shorter than 30
            // calendar days.
            int d = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
            for k = 0 to 30
                if na(out)
                    DateTime c = unix_to_date((d + k) * MS_DAY)
                    if c.is_trading_day()
                        int t = z.to_unix(c.Year, c.Month, c.Day, Hour)
                        if t > unix_ms
                            out := t
            if na(out)
                runtime.error("next_expiry_after: no trading day within 30 days. The exchange calendar is wrong")
        ExpiryKind.WEEKLY =>
            // The current week answers unless its expiry has passed, which a
            // Thursday expiry can have done while its Friday has not, so the
            // probe steps whole weeks. The second probe always resolves; the
            // third is margin, and the bound is fixed so no guard is needed.
            int d = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
            for k = 0 to 3
                if na(out)
                    DateTime probe = unix_to_date((d + 7 * k) * MS_DAY)
                    DateTime wx = probe.weekly_expiry()
                    int t = z.to_unix(wx.Year, wx.Month, wx.Day, Hour)
                    if t > unix_ms
                        out := t
        ExpiryKind.MONTHLY =>
            out := monthly_expiry(here.Year, here.Month, Hour, z)
            if out <= unix_ms
                DateTime nxt = new_datetime(here.Year, here.Month, 1).plus_months(1)
                out := monthly_expiry(nxt.Year, nxt.Month, Hour, z)
        ExpiryKind.QUARTERLY =>
            // The current quarter's witching month is never behind the
            // instant's month, so one conditional step to the next quarter is
            // the whole walk, exactly as the MONTHLY arm steps one month.
            int qm = quarter(here.Month) * 3
            out := monthly_expiry(here.Year, qm, Hour, z)
            if out <= unix_ms
                int y2 = here.Year
                int qm2 = qm + 3
                if qm2 > 12
                    qm2 := 3
                    y2 := y2 + 1
                out := monthly_expiry(y2, qm2, Hour, z)
        ExpiryKind.VIX =>
            runtime.error("next_expiry_after: ExpiryKind.VIX is a.m.-settled and has no close-shaped Hour. Use next_vix_settlement_after")
        => runtime.error("next_expiry_after: unhandled ExpiryKind. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        The instant of the next VIX final settlement strictly after a given one. The VIX member of the next_expiry_after family, split out because its settlement is a morning auction rather than a close; next_expiry_after's kind parameter says why one Hour default cannot serve both.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the settlement moment. Default 9.
// @param Minute    Local minute of the settlement moment. Default 30.
// @param z         The zone the instant is read in and the settlement time is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds of the next VIX settlement. The one-month step is exhaustive: a settlement lands mid-month, so the following month's is strictly ahead of any instant this month's has already passed.
export next_vix_settlement_after(int unix_ms, int Hour = 9, int Minute = 30, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z)
    int out = vix_settlement(here.Year, here.Month, Hour, Minute, z)
    if out <= unix_ms
        DateTime nxt = new_datetime(here.Year, here.Month, 1).plus_months(1)
        out := vix_settlement(nxt.Year, nxt.Month, Hour, Minute, z)
    out

// @function        Every expiry of a cycle whose date falls in a span. Returns dates rather than instants, which is what lets one function serve all five kinds: the a.m.-settled VIX and the close-settled equity cycles do not share an hour, but they all own a calendar day. One guarded walk routed through is_expiry_day rather than five steppers, so this function cannot disagree with the predicate it enumerates.
// @param from_ms   Start of the span, Unix milliseconds. Inclusive by date: an expiry on the civil date this instant falls on in z is reported, whatever the time of day.
// @param to_ms     End of the span, Unix milliseconds. Exclusive by date: an expiry on the civil date this instant falls on is not reported.
// @param kind      The expiry cycle to enumerate.
// @param z         The zone the endpoints are resolved to civil dates in. Default NEW_YORK, where every cycle here is listed.
// @returns         Array of DateTimes at midnight, ascending, possibly empty; an empty span holds no expiries. RAISES when to_ms is before from_ms, which is not a span, and when the span exceeds 20000 calendar days, refused outright, not silently truncated.
export expiries_between(int from_ms, int to_ms, ExpiryKind kind, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    if to_ms < from_ms
        runtime.error("expiries_between: the span runs backward (to_ms is before from_ms)")
    DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(from_ms, z)
    DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(to_ms, z)
    int lo = days_from_civil(a.Year, a.Month, a.Day)
    int hi = days_from_civil(b.Year, b.Month, b.Day)
    if hi - lo > 20000
        runtime.error("expiries_between: span of " + str.tostring(hi - lo) + " days exceeds the 20000-day bound")
    array<DateTime> out = array.new<DateTime>()
    // Guarded because Pine counts down through an empty range: from and to on
    // the same civil date must yield nothing, not a backward walk.
    if hi > lo
        for d = lo to hi - 1
            DateTime c = unix_to_date(d * MS_DAY)
            if c.is_expiry_day(kind)
                out.push(c)
    out

// @function        Parse a tenor string such as "1D", "2W", "3M" or "1Y" into a Period. Case-insensitive. Rejects by returning na, like every other parser here: a parser's input is by definition untrusted, and raising would have made this the one function you cannot safely point at an input.string.
// @param tenor     The tenor string: an integer followed by D, W, M or Y.
// @returns         The equivalent Period, or na when the string is not a tenor. A week becomes seven days; a month stays a month, because they are not the same kind of quantity.
export parse_tenor(string tenor) =>
    string t = str.upper(tenor)
    int n = str.length(t)
    Period out = na
    if n >= 2
        float qty = str.tonumber(str.substring(t, 0, n - 1))
        string unit = str.substring(t, n - 1, n)
        if not na(qty)
            if unit == "D"
                out := Period.new(0, 0, int(qty))
            else if unit == "W"
                out := Period.new(0, 0, int(qty) * 7)
            else if unit == "M"
                out := Period.new(0, int(qty), 0)
            else if unit == "Y"
                out := Period.new(int(qty), 0, 0)
    out

// ============================================================================
// SESSIONS
//
// A session is a recurring local-time window on selected weekdays, resolved
// against a real calendar. Defined this way instead of as a pair of
// timestamps, it survives daylight saving, holidays and half days without
// any of them being special-cased at the call site.
//
// Why is_last_bar exists, and why it is not a bar late.
//   The usual Pine idiom infers the session end from the data, some form of
//   "was in session last bar, not in session now", which cannot be true until
//   the next bar exists. So the flag lands one bar after the bar it describes,
//   and on the final bar of the chart it never lands at all.
//
//   This library computes the close instant from the calendar instead. So
//   is_last_bar compares the bar's own [open, close) window against that
//   instant and answers on the bar itself, with no lookahead, identically on
//   historical and realtime bars. Feed it Pine's `time` and `time_close`.
//
//   It is also correct on half days, because the bound comes from
//   session_close(), which already knows the day after Thanksgiving closes at
//   13:00. A session drawn from fixed hours does not.
//
// A session that ends at or before it starts crosses midnight, and is labelled
// by the date it started on. That is derived from the times, not stored, for
// the same reason the weekday is: a stored flag can disagree with the fields
// it describes, and eventually does.
// ============================================================================

// @enum                     Standard trading sessions, ready to use. Times are the market conventions, not an exchange feed.
// @field US_PREMARKET       04:00-09:30 New York, NYSE calendar.
// @field US_REGULAR         09:30-16:00 New York, NYSE calendar, half days respected.
// @field US_AFTERHOURS      16:00-20:00 New York, NYSE calendar.
// @field US_EXTENDED        04:00-20:00 New York, the whole extended day.
// @field FX_SYDNEY          21:00-06:00 UTC, opens Sunday evening.
// @field FX_TOKYO           00:00-09:00 UTC.
// @field FX_LONDON          08:00-17:00 UTC.
// @field FX_NEWYORK         13:00-22:00 UTC.
// @field FX_LONDON_NY       13:00-17:00 UTC, the overlap where liquidity peaks.
// @field KZ_ASIAN           20:00-00:00 New York, the Asian range.
// @field KZ_LONDON          02:00-05:00 New York.
// @field KZ_NEWYORK         07:00-10:00 New York.
// @field KZ_LONDON_CLOSE    10:00-12:00 New York.
// @field CME_RTH            08:30-15:15 Chicago, CME calendar, half days respected.
// @field CME_ETH            17:00-16:00 Chicago, the Globex day, CME calendar, half days respected.
// @field LSE_REGULAR        08:00-16:30 London, LSE calendar, half days respected.
// @field XETRA_REGULAR      09:00-17:30 Frankfurt, the German cash market. Named for Xetra rather than Eurex because these are the cash hours; Eurex derivatives are EUREX_REGULAR below. German cash-market holidays are not modelled, and this preset carries no calendar at all rather than borrowing the derivatives one: Xetra closes on days Eurex trades.
// @field HKEX_REGULAR       09:30-16:00 Hong Kong with lunch 12:00-13:00, the schedule from 7 March 2011, HKEX calendar, half days honoured. Before that date the open was 10:00; a preset is one set of hours, so the older era opens half an hour early. On a half day the close comes in to 12:00 and the lunch break disappears with it, which is what the exchange actually does.
// @field ASX_REGULAR        10:00-16:00 Sydney, ASX calendar, half days honoured. The half day ends at 14:10 and falls on the last session before Christmas and the last of the year, so it moves off 24 and 31 December when those land on a weekend.
// @field TSX_REGULAR        09:30-16:00 Toronto, TSX calendar, half days honoured. Christmas Eve only, at 13:00, and only when 24 December is itself a session.
// @field SSE_REGULAR        09:30-15:00 Shanghai with lunch 11:30-13:00, SSE calendar. The longest lunch break of any market here, and the only preset besides HKEX and TSE to carry one.
// @field BSE_REGULAR        09:15-15:30 Mumbai, BSE calendar, no lunch break. The Diwali Muhurat evening session is not modelled.
// @field SGX_REGULAR        09:00-17:00 Singapore, SGX calendar, no lunch break: the reference calendar models none in this era, and the pre-2011 one it dropped is not reconstructed here.
// @field TSE_REGULAR        09:00-15:30 Tokyo with lunch 11:30-12:30, the schedule from 5 November 2024, JPX calendar. Before that date the close was 15:00; a preset is one set of hours, so the older era is wrong by 30 minutes. The lunch break has held at these times for as long as the exchange calendars record.
// @field EUREX_REGULAR      08:00-22:00 Berlin, EUREX calendar: the real derivatives session, TARGET2-shaped holidays, no half days.
// @field CRYPTO_DAY         00:00-24:00 UTC, all seven days, CRYPTO calendar: the UTC day boundary crypto convention settles on. Always open, which makes is_first_bar and is_last_bar the daily-boundary markers.
export enum SessionId
    US_PREMARKET = "US pre-market"
    US_REGULAR = "US regular"
    US_AFTERHOURS = "US after-hours"
    US_EXTENDED = "US extended"
    FX_SYDNEY = "FX Sydney"
    FX_TOKYO = "FX Tokyo"
    FX_LONDON = "FX London"
    FX_NEWYORK = "FX New York"
    FX_LONDON_NY = "FX London/New York overlap"
    KZ_ASIAN = "Asian range"
    KZ_LONDON = "London killzone"
    KZ_NEWYORK = "New York killzone"
    KZ_LONDON_CLOSE = "London close"
    CME_RTH = "CME regular"
    CME_ETH = "CME Globex"
    LSE_REGULAR = "LSE regular"
    XETRA_REGULAR = "Xetra regular"
    TSE_REGULAR = "TSE regular"
    EUREX_REGULAR = "Eurex regular"
    HKEX_REGULAR = "HKEX regular"
    ASX_REGULAR = "ASX regular"
    TSX_REGULAR = "TSX regular"
    SSE_REGULAR = "SSE regular"
    BSE_REGULAR = "BSE regular"
    SGX_REGULAR = "SGX regular"
    CRYPTO_DAY = "Crypto day"

// @type              A recurring local-time window, resolved against a calendar.
// @field Name        Human-readable label.
// @field Tz          The zone the start and end times are quoted in.
// @field StartMin    Start, in minutes past local midnight.
// @field EndMin      End, in minutes past local midnight. When it is at or before StartMin the window crosses midnight and is labelled by the date it started on.
// @field DayMask     Which weekdays a session may start on, as a bit per weekday: bit 0 Sunday through bit 6 Saturday. Monday to Friday is 62. Build it with day_mask() rather than writing the number.
// @field Cal         The exchange calendar governing holidays and half days, or na for no calendar at all, which is what foreign exchange, and any market whose holidays this library does not model, actually wants. There is no separate "holiday aware" flag: a session either names a calendar or it does not, and the pair of a flag and a calendar could hold the state "calendar off, calendar NYSE", which is not a thing a session can be.
// @field EarlyClose  When true the end is pulled in to the exchange's actual close on a half day. This is what makes a session box shrink correctly on the day after Thanksgiving. Requires a Cal, since there is nothing to ask otherwise.
// @field BreakStartMin  Start of the intraday break, in minutes past local midnight, or na for a session that trades straight through. Given with BreakEndMin or not at all.
// @field BreakEndMin    End of the intraday break, where trading resumes. The pair is a hole inside one window rather than a boundary between two, which is how TradingView's own session strings read it: "0900-1130,1230-1530" is a single session. So bounds() still reports the whole envelope, one window per start date, and every window accessor keeps counting the day once. Only one break per session, which is all any market modelled here takes.
export type Session
    string Name = "Session"
    Zone Tz = Zone.NEW_YORK
    int StartMin = 570
    int EndMin = 960
    int DayMask = 62
    Exchange Cal = na
    bool EarlyClose = false
    int BreakStartMin = na
    int BreakEndMin = na

// Bit test for the weekday mask.
day_allowed(int mask, Weekday d) =>
    int bit = int(math.pow(2, d.to_int()))
    mask % (bit * 2) >= bit

// @function        The weekday bitmask for a contiguous run of days, from one weekday through another inclusive, wrapping past Saturday if it has to. Every preset in this file is such a run, and this exists so that none of them, and none of your sessions, is written as a bare number nobody can read: day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY) is 62, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY) is 63, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.THURSDAY) is 31.
// @param from_dow  First weekday of the run.
// @param to_dow    Last weekday of the run, inclusive. Equal to from_dow means a single day.
// @returns         A mask in [1, 127], bit 0 Sunday through bit 6 Saturday.
export day_mask(Weekday from_dow, Weekday to_dow) =>
    int out = 0
    for k = 0 to from_dow.days_until(to_dow)
        out := out + int(math.pow(2, pmod(from_dow.to_int() + k, 7)))
    out

// @function        Build a custom session.
// @param Name      Human-readable label.
// @param Tz        The zone the times are quoted in.
// @param StartMin  Start, minutes past local midnight.
// @param EndMin    End, minutes past local midnight; at or before StartMin means it crosses midnight.
// @param DayMask   Weekday bitmask, bit 0 Sunday. Default 62, which is day_mask(MONDAY, FRIDAY), spelled as a literal only because Pine requires a parameter default to be constant.
// @param Cal       The exchange calendar, or na for none. Default na: a session built from bare times says nothing about holidays, and an NYSE default would shut new_session("Tokyo", Zone.TOKYO, 540, 930) on Thanksgiving and trade it through Golden Week.
// @param EarlyClose Whether to pull the end in on a half day. Default false. Raises if set without a Cal.
// @param BreakStartMin Start of an intraday break, minutes past local midnight, or na for none. Default na.
// @param BreakEndMin   End of that break. Default na. Given with BreakStartMin or not at all.
// @returns         The Session. RAISES on five things a Session cannot mean: StartMin or EndMin outside a day, a DayMask outside [0, 127], EarlyClose set without a Cal to read the early close from, half a break pair, and a break that is not strictly inside the window. All five are caller errors, not missing data.
export new_session(string Name, Zone Tz, int StartMin, int EndMin, int DayMask = 62, Exchange Cal = na, bool EarlyClose = false, int BreakStartMin = na, int BreakEndMin = na) =>
    if StartMin < 0 or StartMin > 1439 or EndMin < 0 or EndMin > 1440
        runtime.error("new_session: minutes must be within a day, got " + str.tostring(StartMin) + " and " + str.tostring(EndMin))
    // A mask outside 7 bits is not a session that never runs, it is a typo:
    // day_allowed tests it with a modulo, and a negative mask makes that test
    // meaningless rather than merely empty.
    if DayMask < 0 or DayMask > 127
        runtime.error("new_session: DayMask must be in [0, 127], got " + str.tostring(DayMask))
    if EarlyClose and na(Cal)
        runtime.error("new_session: EarlyClose needs a Cal. There is no calendar to ask which days close early")
    // Half a pair reads as no break at all, since every break accessor tests
    // BreakStartMin, so the session would quietly trade through a lunch the
    // caller believes they set.
    if na(BreakStartMin) != na(BreakEndMin)
        runtime.error("new_session: BreakStartMin and BreakEndMin go together. One of them alone is not a break")
    // One relation covers both hazards. It refuses a break hanging outside the
    // window, and since a session crossing midnight has EndMin <= StartMin it
    // refuses any break there at all: no market modelled here pauses inside an
    // overnight session, and the accessors date a break by the day its window
    // opens, which only means one thing while the window stays inside a day.
    // Written against the times rather than through crosses_midnight() because
    // that method is defined below this one.
    if not na(BreakStartMin) and not (StartMin < BreakStartMin and BreakStartMin < BreakEndMin and BreakEndMin < EndMin)
        runtime.error("new_session: the break must satisfy StartMin < BreakStartMin < BreakEndMin < EndMin, got " + str.tostring(StartMin) + " < " + str.tostring(BreakStartMin) + " < " + str.tostring(BreakEndMin) + " < " + str.tostring(EndMin))
    Session.new(Name, Tz, StartMin, EndMin, DayMask, Cal, EarlyClose, BreakStartMin, BreakEndMin)

// @function        Whether this session's window crosses local midnight. Derived from the times rather than stored, so it cannot disagree with them.
// @param this      The Session.
// @returns         true when the end is at or before the start.
export method crosses_midnight(Session this) =>
    this.EndMin <= this.StartMin

// @function        The nominal length of this session in minutes, before any half-day adjustment.
// @param this      The Session.
// @returns         Minutes from open to close.
export method length_minutes(Session this) =>
    int out = this.EndMin - this.StartMin
    if this.crosses_midnight()
        out := this.EndMin + 1440 - this.StartMin
    out

// @function        One of the built-in standard sessions.
// @param id        Which session.
// @returns         The Session.
export session_of(SessionId id) =>
    Session out = na
    switch id
        SessionId.US_PREMARKET => out := Session.new("US pre-market", Zone.NEW_YORK, 240, 570, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.NYSE, false)
        SessionId.US_REGULAR => out := Session.new("US regular", Zone.NEW_YORK, 570, 960, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.NYSE, true)
        // The extended-hours rows keep EarlyClose false deliberately: a half
        // day ends the regular session early, it does not move the boundaries
        // of pre-market or after-hours trading.
        SessionId.US_AFTERHOURS => out := Session.new("US after-hours", Zone.NEW_YORK, 960, 1200, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.NYSE, false)
        SessionId.US_EXTENDED => out := Session.new("US extended", Zone.NEW_YORK, 240, 1200, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.NYSE, false)
        // Foreign exchange runs through equity holidays, so these carry no
        // calendar and only the weekday mask applies. Sydney opens on Sunday
        // evening UTC, which is why its run starts on Sunday and ends Thursday.
        SessionId.FX_SYDNEY => out := Session.new("FX Sydney", Zone.UTC, 1260, 360, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.THURSDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.FX_TOKYO => out := Session.new("FX Tokyo", Zone.UTC, 0, 540, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.FX_LONDON => out := Session.new("FX London", Zone.UTC, 480, 1020, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.FX_NEWYORK => out := Session.new("FX New York", Zone.UTC, 780, 1320, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.FX_LONDON_NY => out := Session.new("FX London/New York overlap", Zone.UTC, 780, 1020, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.KZ_ASIAN => out := Session.new("Asian range", Zone.NEW_YORK, 1200, 0, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.THURSDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.KZ_LONDON => out := Session.new("London killzone", Zone.NEW_YORK, 120, 300, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.KZ_NEWYORK => out := Session.new("New York killzone", Zone.NEW_YORK, 420, 600, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.KZ_LONDON_CLOSE => out := Session.new("London close", Zone.NEW_YORK, 600, 720, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        // Both CME rows honour early closes. They name the CME calendar, and
        // that calendar reports roughly nine half days a year at 12:00 Chicago;
        // a box drawn to 15:15 or 16:00 on one of them draws past a market
        // this same library says has already shut.
        SessionId.CME_RTH => out := Session.new("CME regular", Zone.CHICAGO, 510, 915, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.CME, true)
        SessionId.CME_ETH => out := Session.new("CME Globex", Zone.CHICAGO, 1020, 960, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.CME, true)
        SessionId.LSE_REGULAR => out := Session.new("LSE regular", Zone.LONDON, 480, 990, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.LSE, true)
        // The German cash market's holiday calendar is not modelled, so Xetra
        // carries no calendar and runs on the weekday mask alone; it will be
        // wrong on German holidays. It does not borrow the EUREX calendar
        // below, because Xetra closes on days Eurex trades. TSE runs on the
        // JPX calendar and breaks for lunch from 11:30 to 12:30; EarlyClose is
        // false because JPX has no half days.
        SessionId.XETRA_REGULAR => out := Session.new("Xetra regular", Zone.FRANKFURT, 540, 1050, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), na, false)
        SessionId.TSE_REGULAR => out := Session.new("TSE regular", Zone.TOKYO, 540, 930, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.JPX, false, 690, 750)
        SessionId.EUREX_REGULAR => out := Session.new("Eurex regular", Zone.FRANKFURT, 480, 1320, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.EUREX, false)
        // EarlyClose is true and the lunch break is set: on a Hong Kong half
        // day the close comes in to 12:00, which is exactly where the break
        // would start, and break_bounds reports no break at all rather than
        // one hanging outside the window.
        SessionId.HKEX_REGULAR => out := Session.new("HKEX regular", Zone.HONG_KONG, 570, 960, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.HKEX, true, 720, 780)
        // Neither of these has a lunch break, and both honour their half days.
        SessionId.ASX_REGULAR => out := Session.new("ASX regular", Zone.SYDNEY, 600, 960, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.ASX, true)
        SessionId.TSX_REGULAR => out := Session.new("TSX regular", Zone.TORONTO, 570, 960, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.TSX, true)
        // EarlyClose is false on all three: none of the three exchanges
        // shortens a session, so there is nothing for the flag to pull in.
        SessionId.SSE_REGULAR => out := Session.new("SSE regular", Zone.SHANGHAI, 570, 900, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.SSE, false, 690, 780)
        SessionId.BSE_REGULAR => out := Session.new("BSE regular", Zone.MUMBAI, 555, 930, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.BSE, false)
        SessionId.SGX_REGULAR => out := Session.new("SGX regular", Zone.SINGAPORE, 540, 1020, day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY), Exchange.SGX, false)
        // All seven days, and an EndMin of 1440: the exclusive next-midnight
        // bound, which bounds() already treats as the day's true end. With
        // the CRYPTO calendar under it this window exists for every calendar
        // day, so consecutive windows tile the timeline exactly.
        SessionId.CRYPTO_DAY => out := Session.new("Crypto day", Zone.UTC, 0, 1440, day_mask(Weekday.SUNDAY, Weekday.SATURDAY), Exchange.CRYPTO, false)
    out

// @function        The instants this session occupies on a given start date, or na when it does not run that day. For a session that crosses midnight the end lands on the following calendar day, and that day is the one the holiday and half-day tests are asked about, because it is the day the session settles into.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param on        The session's start date. Only its Year, Month and Day are read: a session's window comes from its own times and zone, so any time of day or offset the record carries is not part of the question.
// @returns         A half-open Interval of instants, or na when the session does not run.
export method bounds(Session this, DateTime on) =>
    int Year = on.Year
    int Month = on.Month
    int Day = on.Day
    Interval out = na
    if day_allowed(this.DayMask, on.weekday())
        // A window is labelled by its start date but belongs to the date it
        // ends on: a Globex session opening Sunday evening is Monday's trading
        // day, and one that would open Friday evening has no Saturday to
        // settle into. Testing the start date instead shifts the whole
        // schedule a calendar day and makes the Sunday bit of DayMask 63
        // unreachable, since no exchange counts Sunday as a trading day.
        int shift = 0
        if this.crosses_midnight()
            shift := 1
        DateTime ends = unix_to_date((days_from_civil(Year, Month, Day) + shift) * MS_DAY)
        bool runs = true
        if not na(this.Cal) and not ends.is_trading_day(this.Cal)
            runs := false
        if runs
            int start = this.Tz.to_unix(Year, Month, Day, int(this.StartMin / 60), this.StartMin % 60)
            int emin = this.EndMin
            if emin == 1440
                emin := 1439
            int fin = this.Tz.to_unix(ends.Year, ends.Month, ends.Day, int(emin / 60), emin % 60)
            if this.EndMin == 1440
                fin := fin + MS_MINUTE
            // A half day pulls the close in. Without this a session drawn from
            // fixed hours runs three hours past a market that already shut.
            // Same date as the holiday test above, for the same reason: it is
            // the closing day whose close is being shortened.
            if this.EarlyClose and ends.is_early_close(this.Cal)
                int actual = ends.session_close(this.Cal)
                if not na(actual) and actual < fin
                    fin := actual
            if fin > start
                out := Interval.new(start, fin)
    out

// @function        The intraday break inside this session's window on a given start date. The break is a hole in one window, not a gap between two: bounds() reports the whole envelope around it, and this reports the part of that envelope nobody trades. is_open() is the two put together.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param on        The session's start date, read exactly as bounds() reads it: only Year, Month and Day.
// @returns         A half-open Interval, or na when the session carries no break, does not run that day, or shuts early enough that the break no longer fits inside the window.
export method break_bounds(Session this, DateTime on) =>
    Interval out = na
    if not na(this.BreakStartMin)
        Interval w = this.bounds(on)
        if not na(w)
            int bs = this.Tz.to_unix(on.Year, on.Month, on.Day, int(this.BreakStartMin / 60), this.BreakStartMin % 60)
            int be = this.Tz.to_unix(on.Year, on.Month, on.Day, int(this.BreakEndMin / 60), this.BreakEndMin % 60)
            // Whole or nothing against the envelope. A half day that shuts at
            // the lunch bell has no lunch, and one that shuts partway through
            // it has none either: what would survive the clip is the run-up to
            // a close, not a pause with trading on the far side of it.
            if bs >= w.FromMS and be <= w.ToMS
                out := Interval.new(bs, be)
    out

// @function        The session window containing an instant, if any. Checks the window dated that day and the one dated the day before, so a session that began the previous evening is found.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @returns         The Interval in progress, or na when the session is not running.
export method window_at(Session this, int unix_ms) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, this.Tz)
    int base = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    Interval out = na
    for k = 0 to 1
        DateTime d = unix_to_date((base - k) * MS_DAY)
        Interval w = this.bounds(d)
        if na(out) and not na(w) and w.contains(unix_ms)
            out := w
    out

// @function        Every window of this session that intersects a span, in ascending order. Routed through bounds(), so each window carries the same holiday and half-day treatment every other accessor here reports. Intersection is on instants, not dates: a window that crosses midnight and opened the evening before from_ms is included when it runs into the span, which is why the scan starts one start date early.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param from_ms   Start of the span, Unix milliseconds. Inclusive.
// @param to_ms     End of the span, Unix milliseconds. Exclusive: a window beginning exactly at to_ms is not reported, and an empty span intersects nothing.
// @returns         Array of Intervals, ascending by start, possibly empty. RAISES when to_ms is before from_ms, which is not a span, and when the span covers more than 20000 start dates: the same cap expiries_between draws, stated here because a caller sizing a drawing pool from this array needs the bound that caps it: at most one window per start date over a scan that begins one date early, so never more than 20002 entries.
export method windows_between(Session this, int from_ms, int to_ms) =>
    if to_ms < from_ms
        runtime.error("windows_between: the span runs backward (to_ms is before from_ms)")
    DateTime a = unix_to_date_zone(from_ms, this.Tz)
    DateTime b = unix_to_date_zone(to_ms, this.Tz)
    int lo = days_from_civil(a.Year, a.Month, a.Day)
    int hi = days_from_civil(b.Year, b.Month, b.Day)
    if hi - lo > 20000
        runtime.error("windows_between: span of " + str.tostring(hi - lo) + " days exceeds the 20000-day bound")
    array<Interval> out = array.new<Interval>()
    // Guarded: an empty span must yield nothing, and without the gate a
    // window straddling the shared instant would pass the overlap test. The
    // scan itself starts at lo - 1 for the crossing-midnight case above and
    // can never run backward, since hi is at least lo.
    if to_ms > from_ms
        for d = lo - 1 to hi
            DateTime c = unix_to_date(d * MS_DAY)
            Interval w = this.bounds(c)
            if not na(w) and w.FromMS < to_ms and w.ToMS > from_ms
                out.push(w)
    out

// @function        Whether this session is trading at an instant. An intraday break is not trading, so a session with a lunch answers false through it while bounds() still reports the envelope around it.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @returns         true when inside the session and outside its break.
export method is_open(Session this, int unix_ms) =>
    Interval w = this.window_at(unix_ms)
    bool out = not na(w)
    if out and not na(this.BreakStartMin)
        // A break is dated by the window holding it, and a session carrying one
        // cannot cross midnight, so the window's local open date is that date.
        Interval b = this.break_bounds(unix_to_date_zone(w.FromMS, this.Tz))
        if not na(b) and b.contains(unix_ms)
            out := false
    out

// @function        The session window a bar touches, which is not the window that contains one of the bar's edges. A bar can be larger than the session: a 4h bar 09:30-13:30 New York straddles the whole of a 10:00-12:00 window, and then neither edge is inside the session, so an edge probe finds nothing and the bar registers as neither the first nor the last of a session it wholly contains. Overlap is the correct test. Exported because it is the accessor is_first_bar and is_last_bar answer from, and without it a caller who gets true from is_first_bar has no way to ask what window that was: window_at is instant-keyed, so on a 1H bar 09:00-10:00 New York, whose open precedes the session open, it returns na for the very bar the predicate just flagged. Scans the same neighbourhood window_at walks plus the following day, so a bar crossing local midnight still finds the window it runs into.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param bar_open  The bar's opening time, Unix milliseconds. Pine's `time`.
// @param bar_close The bar's closing time, Unix milliseconds. Pine's `time_close`.
// @returns         The Interval the bar overlaps, or na when the bar touches no session window.
export method window_of_bar(Session this, int bar_open, int bar_close) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(bar_open, this.Tz)
    int base = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    Interval out = na
    for k = -1 to 1
        DateTime d = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
        Interval w = this.bounds(d)
        if na(out) and not na(w) and w.FromMS < bar_close and w.ToMS > bar_open
            out := w
    out

// @function        Whether a bar is the first bar of this session. Pass Pine's `time` and `time_close`.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param bar_open  The bar's opening time, Unix milliseconds.
// @param bar_close The bar's closing time, Unix milliseconds.
// @returns         true when the bar contains the session open.
export method is_first_bar(Session this, int bar_open, int bar_close) =>
    Interval w = this.window_of_bar(bar_open, bar_close)
    bool out = false
    if not na(w)
        out := bar_open <= w.FromMS and bar_close > w.FromMS
    out

// @function        Whether a bar is the last bar of this session, answered on that bar, not one bar later. Pass Pine's `time` and `time_close`. The usual Pine idiom compares this bar's session state with the previous bar's, which cannot resolve until the next bar exists; this compares the bar against a close instant computed from the calendar, so it needs no lookahead and behaves the same on historical and realtime bars. Half days are handled, because the close comes from the calendar rather than from fixed hours.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param bar_open  The bar's opening time, Unix milliseconds.
// @param bar_close The bar's closing time, Unix milliseconds.
// @returns         true when the session's close falls inside this bar.
export method is_last_bar(Session this, int bar_open, int bar_close) =>
    Interval w = this.window_of_bar(bar_open, bar_close)
    bool out = false
    if not na(w)
        out := bar_open < w.ToMS and bar_close >= w.ToMS
    out

// @function        How far through this session's envelope an instant sits. Wall-clock, so it runs on through an intraday break instead of pausing at it: plotted, it stays monotone across lunch. Measure against traded time yourself, off break_bounds, if that is the reading you want.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @returns         A fraction in [0, 1), or na when no window contains the instant.
export method progress(Session this, int unix_ms) =>
    Interval w = this.window_at(unix_ms)
    float out = na
    if not na(w) and w.ToMS > w.FromMS
        out := (unix_ms - w.FromMS) / (w.ToMS - w.FromMS + 0.0)
    out

// @function        Milliseconds until this session's envelope closes. Measured through an intraday break rather than around it, for the same reason progress is wall-clock.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @returns         Milliseconds remaining, or na when no window contains the instant.
export method time_to_close(Session this, int unix_ms) =>
    Interval w = this.window_at(unix_ms)
    int out = na
    if not na(w)
        out := w.ToMS - unix_ms
    out

// @function        The most recent window of this session that has already ended at an instant: the completed-previous window. While the session runs, the running window is not this answer, because it has not completed; window_at holds it. The look-back counterpart of window_after below.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds. An instant exactly at a close belongs to the window it closes (the windows are half-open), so that window already counts as completed.
// @param max_days  How many start dates back to search. Default 10, enough to clear a holiday weekend.
// @returns         The completed Interval nearest before the instant, or na when none ends within max_days, the one meaning na carries here.
export method window_before(Session this, int unix_ms, int max_days = 10) =>
    Interval out = na
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, this.Tz)
    int base = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    // Clamped because a negative max_days would make Pine walk the range
    // backwards, exactly as in window_after below. Nearest first: windows of
    // one session close in start-date order, so the first completed window
    // the backward scan meets is the latest one.
    for k = 0 to math.max(0, max_days)
        if na(out)
            DateTime d = unix_to_date((base - k) * MS_DAY)
            Interval w = this.bounds(d)
            if not na(w) and w.ToMS <= unix_ms
                out := w
    out

// @function        The next window of this session that opens strictly after an instant: the upcoming-next window. While the session runs, the answer is the window after the running one; window_at holds the running one. This is the window time_to_open measures the distance to: that function answers when, this answers what, and the one routes through the other so they cannot disagree.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param max_days  How many start dates ahead to search. Default 10, enough to clear a holiday weekend.
// @returns         The next upcoming Interval, or na when none opens within max_days; na means only that.
export method window_after(Session this, int unix_ms, int max_days = 10) =>
    Interval out = na
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, this.Tz)
    int base = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    // Clamped because a negative max_days would make Pine walk the range
    // backwards. An earlier window can never satisfy the "opens after now"
    // test, so the clamp changes no answer; it keeps this loop on the same
    // guard convention as every other computed range in the file.
    for k = 0 to math.max(0, max_days)
        if na(out)
            DateTime d = unix_to_date((base + k) * MS_DAY)
            Interval w = this.bounds(d)
            if not na(w) and w.FromMS > unix_ms
                out := w
    out

// @function        Milliseconds until this session's next open, strictly after the given instant. It answers the same question whether or not the session is currently running: while it runs, the answer is the distance to the next open, not zero. Ask is_open() whether the session is open now; a zero here would make one number do two jobs, a real distance and a flag. Strictly-after matches next_trading_day and next_fomc_after. Routed through window_after, whose result this is the distance to, so the two cannot disagree.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param max_days  How many days ahead to search. Default 10, enough to clear a holiday weekend.
// @returns         Milliseconds until the next open, or na when no open falls within max_days.
export method time_to_open(Session this, int unix_ms, int max_days = 10) =>
    Interval w = this.window_after(unix_ms, max_days)
    int out = na
    if not na(w)
        out := w.FromMS - unix_ms
    out

// @function        How many chart bars of a given timeframe this session holds on a date, counting a clipped final bar as a bar. The duration comes from bounds(), so a half day counts its shortened length (42 rather than 78 five-minute bars when the NYSE closes at 13:00), and a day the session does not run counts nothing. This is the number to size per-session drawing budgets from, e.g. against max_boxes_count = 500; sizing from fixed hours instead miscounts on the days the calendar shortens.
// @param this      The Session.
// @param on        The session's start date, as bounds() reads it: only Year, Month and Day are read.
// @param timeframe_ms  The bar interval in milliseconds.
// @returns         The bar count, or na when the session does not run that day. RAISES when timeframe_ms is not positive: a zero-width bar is not a timeframe.
export method bars_per_session(Session this, DateTime on, int timeframe_ms) =>
    if timeframe_ms <= 0
        runtime.error("bars_per_session: timeframe_ms must be positive, got " + str.tostring(timeframe_ms))
    Interval w = this.bounds(on)
    int out = na
    if not na(w)
        Interval b = this.break_bounds(on)
        // Ceiling division: a window that is not a whole number of bars still
        // ends inside one, and that clipped bar exists on the chart.
        if na(b)
            out := fdiv(w.duration() + timeframe_ms - 1, timeframe_ms)
        else
            // Each side of a break rounds up on its own, because the chart
            // starts a fresh bar at the afternoon open rather than carrying the
            // morning's alignment across. Subtracting the break from the
            // envelope and rounding once undercounts: two-hour bars over a
            // 150-minute morning and a 180-minute afternoon are 2 + 2, not the
            // 3 that 330 minutes of traded time would give.
            out := fdiv(b.FromMS - w.FromMS + timeframe_ms - 1, timeframe_ms) + fdiv(w.ToMS - b.ToMS + timeframe_ms - 1, timeframe_ms)
    out

// A "HHMM-HHMM" clock range as a pair of minute counts, or [na, na] when the
// string is not one. Each field is bounded from both sides, and the end on its
// total, so "-100-1200" (a start of -60 minutes) and "1800-2430" (an end of
// 1470) fail here rather than reaching a constructor that would refuse them.
// Both halves of a spec carrying a break have this shape, so it is written once.
spec_range(string s) =>
    int a = na
    int b = na
    if str.length(s) == 9 and str.substring(s, 4, 5) == "-"
        float sh = str.tonumber(str.substring(s, 0, 2))
        float sm = str.tonumber(str.substring(s, 2, 4))
        float eh = str.tonumber(str.substring(s, 5, 7))
        float em = str.tonumber(str.substring(s, 7, 9))
        bool timed = not na(sh) and not na(sm) and not na(eh) and not na(em)
        if timed and sh >= 0 and sh <= 23 and sm >= 0 and sm <= 59 and eh >= 0 and eh <= 24 and em >= 0 and em <= 59 and eh * 60 + em <= 1440
            a := int(sh) * 60 + int(sm)
            b := int(eh) * 60 + int(em)
    [a, b]

// A minute count as "HHMM". 1440 prints as "2400", the spelling of an end at
// midnight that spec_range reads back.
spec_clock(int m) =>
    str.format("{0,number,00}{1,number,00}", int(m / 60), m % 60)

// @function        Parse a TradingView-style session string such as "0930-1600" or "0930-1600:23456", where the digits after the colon are weekdays with 1 meaning Sunday. A single comma in the time part marks an intraday break, as in "0900-1130,1230-1530". Recognising the format Pine users already write avoids inventing a second one.
// @param spec      The session string.
// @param Tz        The zone the times are quoted in.
// @param Name      Label for the session. Default the spec itself.
// @param Cal       The exchange calendar, or na for none. Default na, since a bare time range says nothing about holidays.
// @param EarlyClose Whether to pull the end in on a half day. Default false. Raises if set without a Cal.
// @returns         The Session, or na when the string is malformed or the times are not a legal clock range. "2430" and "-100" are na rather than a Session with 1470 or -60 minutes in it: this returns only Sessions new_session would have built. A string with two or more commas is na too: TradingView writes those, and this model has one break to put them in.
export parse_session(string spec, Zone Tz, string Name = "", Exchange Cal = na, bool EarlyClose = false) =>
    Session out = na
    string body = spec
    int mask = day_mask(Weekday.MONDAY, Weekday.FRIDAY)
    bool ok = true
    if str.contains(spec, ":")
        int c = str.pos(spec, ":")
        body := str.substring(spec, 0, c)
        string days = str.substring(spec, c + 1, str.length(spec))
        if str.length(days) < 1
            ok := false
        else
            mask := 0
            for i = 0 to str.length(days) - 1
                float v = str.tonumber(str.substring(days, i, i + 1))
                if na(v) or v < 1 or v > 7
                    ok := false
                else
                    mask := mask + int(math.pow(2, int(v) - 1))
    int start_min = na
    int end_min = na
    int break_start = na
    int break_end = na
    if ok
        if str.contains(body, ",")
            // One comma splits the window into a morning and an afternoon
            // around a break. A second comma leaves a half that is not a clock
            // range, so a three-part string falls out as na through
            // spec_range rather than needing a count of its own.
            int c2 = str.pos(body, ",")
            [m_open, m_close] = spec_range(str.substring(body, 0, c2))
            [a_open, a_close] = spec_range(str.substring(body, c2 + 1, str.length(body)))
            start_min := m_open
            break_start := m_close
            break_end := a_open
            end_min := a_close
        else
            [s_open, s_close] = spec_range(body)
            start_min := s_open
            end_min := s_close
    // The relation new_session raises on, tested here as a refusal instead: a
    // parser answers na for a string it cannot hold, and "1200-1100,1000-0900"
    // is a well-formed pair of clock ranges that is not a session. Everything
    // it does accept still goes through the constructor, so there is one
    // definition of a legal Session and no spec can produce an illegal one.
    if not na(break_start) and not (start_min < break_start and break_start < break_end and break_end < end_min)
        ok := false
    if ok and not na(start_min) and not na(end_min)
        string nm = Name
        if str.length(nm) == 0
            nm := spec
        out := new_session(nm, Tz, start_min, end_min, mask, Cal, EarlyClose, break_start, break_end)
    out

// @function        This session as a TradingView-style string, e.g. "0930-1600:23456", or "0900-1130,1230-1530:23456" when the session breaks for lunch. The format is TradingView's, so it carries exactly what TradingView's carries (the window, the break and the day mask), and not Tz, Cal or EarlyClose. Those are parameters of parse_session rather than fields of the string, so a full round-trip is parse_session(s.to_spec(), s.Tz, s.Name, s.Cal, s.EarlyClose); pass the string alone and you get the defaults, which for a US_REGULAR session means one that trades on Christmas. The string stays narrow so that it can be pasted into, or out of, a Pine session input.
// @param this      The Session.
// @returns         The session string.
export method to_spec(Session this) =>
    string days = ""
    for i = 0 to 6
        if day_allowed(this.DayMask, weekday_from_int(i))
            days := days + str.tostring(i + 1)
    string body = spec_clock(this.StartMin) + "-" + spec_clock(this.EndMin)
    if not na(this.BreakStartMin)
        body := spec_clock(this.StartMin) + "-" + spec_clock(this.BreakStartMin) + "," + spec_clock(this.BreakEndMin) + "-" + spec_clock(this.EndMin)
    body + ":" + days

// --------------------------------------------------- economic calendar --
// The distinction this section exists to make: a rule extrapolates, a
// published list does not. Everything above this line is rules, and answers
// for any year. FOMC meeting dates are set by the committee and published a
// year or two ahead (no rule generates them), so they are a table, and the
// table has an end.
//
// Past that end the answer is UNKNOWN, never NO: the table not listing
// 2031-06-17 does not mean the committee will not meet that day, only that
// it has not published that far ahead.
//
// The mechanism is the three-valued Known enum rather than na, because Pine's
// bool cannot hold na at all: `bool x = na` is a compile error, though int,
// enum and object types accept it. The enum is also the stronger contract:
// UNKNOWN will not type-check anywhere a bool is wanted, so reading "not
// known" as "no" has to be written down, via is_yes(), rather than happening
// by default.
//
// Nothing else is here because CPI and Employment Situation dates, though
// equally list-shaped (published annually by the BLS), have no complete
// authoritative source to table from, so they are absent, not approximated. The "nonfarm payrolls is the first Friday" rule
// of thumb fails roughly once a year: the January 2026 release is Friday the
// 9th, the second Friday, because the reference week sits inside the
// holidays. If you want the convention anyway, it is already available as
// nth_weekday_of_month(Y, M, Weekday.FRIDAY, 1).
//
// The coverage accessors are named for their source (fomc_known_from,
// fomc_known_through), rather than taking a source enum, so another release
// calendar would arrive with its own pair beside them and nothing existing
// would change.

// @function        Collapse a three-valued answer to a bool, treating UNKNOWN as false. Only call this after checking coverage first (fomc_known_from and fomc_known_through, or calendar_from and calendar_through), because it reads "not known" as "no".
// @param this      The answer.
// @returns         true only for YES.
export method is_yes(Known this) =>
    this == Known.YES

// Second day of each scheduled FOMC meeting: the day the statement lands.
// Every meeting in this window runs two consecutive calendar days, so the
// first day is this minus one and does not need its own table.
// Source: federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm, retrieved
// 2026-08-12. Excludes the 2025-08-22 special notation vote, which was not a
// meeting.
const string FOMC_DECISION = ",ee6,efj,egp,ei2,ej8,eks,ely,en4,eoa,epn,er0,es6,etc,euw,ew2,ex8,eyl,ezy,f14,f2a,f3g,f50,f66,f7c,f8p,fa2,fb8,fce,fdr,ff4,fgi,fhn,fit,fk6,flj,fmp,fnv,fp8,fqe,frk,fsx,fua,fvg,fwt,fxz,fzc,g0i,g1o,g31,g4e,g5k,g6q,g83,g9g,gam,gbs,"
const int FOMC_FROM = 2021
const int FOMC_THROUGH = 2027

// @function        The last year for which the FOMC meeting table is known. Outside the window the predicates answer Known.UNKNOWN, a value, not na. Test it as one: `if x == Known.UNKNOWN`, or gate on this year first. `if na(x)` is dead code, because na(Known.UNKNOWN) is false: the branch never runs and the UNKNOWN flows on as though it had been checked.
// @returns         Calendar year of the last covered date.
export fomc_known_through() =>
    FOMC_THROUGH

// @function        The first year for which the FOMC meeting table is known.
// @returns         Calendar year of the first covered date.
export fomc_known_from() =>
    FOMC_FROM

// @function        Whether a date is the second day of an FOMC meeting, when the statement is released. No is_ prefix, because it does not return a bool: every other is_ name here promises one, and `if d.is_fomc_decision_day()` would look as though it should compile when it cannot.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @returns         YES, NO, or UNKNOWN when the date lies outside the published window. UNKNOWN is not a no.
export method fomc_decision_day(DateTime this) =>
    Known out = Known.UNKNOWN
    if this.Year >= FOMC_FROM and this.Year <= FOMC_THROUGH
        out := Known.NO
        if in_date_table(FOMC_DECISION, this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)
            out := Known.YES
    out

// @function        Whether a date falls on either day of a scheduled FOMC meeting. No is_ prefix, for the same reason as fomc_decision_day: the answer is a Known, not a bool.
// @param this      The date to test.
// @returns         YES, NO, or UNKNOWN when the date lies outside the published window. UNKNOWN is not a no.
export method fomc_day(DateTime this) =>
    Known out = Known.UNKNOWN
    if this.Year >= FOMC_FROM and this.Year <= FOMC_THROUGH
        DateTime tomorrow = this.plus_days(1)
        out := Known.NO
        if in_date_table(FOMC_DECISION, this.Year, this.Month, this.Day) or in_date_table(FOMC_DECISION, tomorrow.Year, tomorrow.Month, tomorrow.Day)
            out := Known.YES
    out

// @function        The instant of the next FOMC decision strictly after a given one.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param Hour      Local hour of the statement. Default 14, when the statement is normally released.
// @param z         The zone the instant is read in and the statement hour is quoted in. Default NEW_YORK, which is where the Fed publishes. A parameter because the hour and the zone are one fact ("14:00 New York"), and the three other exports that pair an Hour with an instant (monthly_expiry, quarterly_expiry, next_expiry_after) take both.
// @returns         Unix timestamp in milliseconds, or na when the next meeting falls outside the published window.
export next_fomc_after(int unix_ms, int Hour = 14, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    DateTime here = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z)
    int out = na
    int cursor = days_from_civil(here.Year, here.Month, here.Day)
    // A scheduled gap never exceeds three months; 400 days is generous.
    for i = 0 to 400
        if na(out)
            DateTime c = unix_to_date((cursor + i) * MS_DAY)
            if c.Year >= FOMC_FROM and c.Year <= FOMC_THROUGH
                if in_date_table(FOMC_DECISION, c.Year, c.Month, c.Day)
                    int t = z.to_unix(c.Year, c.Month, c.Day, Hour)
                    if t > unix_ms
                        out := t
    out

// @function        FTSE Russell annual reconstitution: the last Friday in June, one of the highest-volume closes of the year. This one is a rule, so unlike the FOMC table it answers for any year. One exception is not modelled: since 2018 FTSE Russell moves reconstitution to the preceding Friday when the last Friday falls on 29 or 30 June, so this function is a week late for such years: 2018 and 2023 realized; 2028 and 2029 come next.
// @param Year      Calendar year.
// @returns         Day of month in June, by the unmodified last-Friday rule.
export russell_rebalance_day(int Year) =>
    nth_weekday_of_month(Year, 6, Weekday.FRIDAY, -1)

// ------------------------------------------------------------- formatting --

// @function        Format a timestamp as the six-character yyMMdd date fragment used by OPRA-style option symbols, resolved in a zone you name rather than in the chart's, so changing symbol cannot change the date this prints.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone to resolve the date in. Default NEW_YORK, which is where OPRA quotes an expiry. A Zone rather than a fixed offset, so US daylight saving is applied by rule instead of being the caller's problem.
// @returns         Six-character string, e.g. "260821".
export format_yymmdd(int unix_ms, Zone z = Zone.NEW_YORK) =>
    DateTime d = unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z)
    str.format("{0,number,00}{1,number,00}{2,number,00}", d.Year % 100, d.Month, d.Day)

// @function        Format a DateTime as an ISO-8601 date-time string without the offset designator, i.e. as a local wall-clock reading. Lossy on purpose: two different instants print identically, so this is for a human reading a label, never for anything that will be parsed back. to_iso is the round-tripping form.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS".
export method to_iso_local(DateTime this) =>
    str.format("{0,number,0000}-{1,number,00}-{2,number,00}T{3,number,00}:{4,number,00}:{5,number,00}", this.Year, this.Month, this.Day, this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second)

// @function        Format a DateTime as an ISO-8601 date only.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-MM-DD".
export method to_iso_date(DateTime this) =>
    str.format("{0,number,0000}-{1,number,00}-{2,number,00}", this.Year, this.Month, this.Day)

// @function        Format a DateTime as an ISO-8601 time only.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "HH:MM:SS".
export method to_iso_time(DateTime this) =>
    str.format("{0,number,00}:{1,number,00}:{2,number,00}", this.Hour, this.Minute, this.Second)

// The UTC designator for an offset. Minutes are carried because real offsets
// are not all whole hours: India is +05:30, Nepal +05:45, Chatham +12:45.
offset_designator(float utc) =>
    string out = "Z"
    if utc != 0.0
        string sign = "+"
        if utc < 0
            sign := "-"
        float mag = math.abs(utc)
        int oh = int(math.floor(mag))
        int om = int(math.round((mag - oh) * 60.0))
        out := str.format("{0}{1,number,00}:{2,number,00}", sign, oh, om)
    out

// @function        Format a DateTime as a full ISO-8601 date-time with its offset designator: the form that survives a round trip, so parse_iso(d.to_iso()) gives back d. The short name belongs to the offset-carrying form: it keeps the offset the type carries, and it matches Interval's to_iso, which also emits offsets. The offset-less spelling is to_iso_local.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS+HH:MM", or ending in "Z" at UTC.
export method to_iso(DateTime this) =>
    this.to_iso_local() + offset_designator(this.UTC)

// @function        This interval in ISO-8601 interval form, "start/end", with both endpoints in UTC.
// @param this      The Interval.
// @returns         A string such as "2026-08-21T13:30:00Z/2026-08-21T20:00:00Z".
export method to_iso(Interval this) =>
    unix_to_date(this.FromMS).to_iso() + "/" + unix_to_date(this.ToMS).to_iso()

// @function        A span of milliseconds in ISO-8601 duration form, e.g. "P2DT4H13M9S". Distinct from a Period's ISO form: this is exact machine time, so it never contains years or months, which have no fixed length. Sub-second spans keep a decimal fraction on the seconds field (500 is "PT0.5S", 1500 is "PT1.5S"), so that "PT0S" is the spelling of zero and of nothing else; truncating instead would print 500 as "PT0S" and -500 as "-PT0S", which is not a duration at all.
// @param ms        The span in milliseconds; negative spans are prefixed with a minus sign. na in gives na out, the same guard format_duration carries and for the same reason.
// @returns         The ISO-8601 duration string, or na when ms is na.
export format_iso_duration(int ms) =>
    string out = na
    // na is not zero: "PT0S" is reserved for a real zero, so an absent span
    // answers na, otherwise a caller could not tell a real zero from a
    // missing answer. format_duration carries the same guard.
    if not na(ms)
        int a = math.abs(ms)
        int d = int(a / MS_DAY)
        int rem = a % MS_DAY
        int h = int(rem / MS_HOUR)
        rem := rem % MS_HOUR
        int mi = int(rem / MS_MINUTE)
        rem := rem % MS_MINUTE
        int s = int(rem / MS_SECOND)
        int frac = rem % MS_SECOND
        // Trailing zeros are trimmed by choosing the width, rather than by
        // padding to three and cutting back: "0.5" and "0.500" are the same
        // duration, and the short one is what a reader expects. pad_num lives
        // further down the file, so the widths are spelled out here.
        string fs = ""
        if frac != 0
            if frac % 100 == 0
                fs := "." + str.tostring(int(frac / 100))
            else if frac % 10 == 0
                fs := "." + str.format("{0,number,00}", int(frac / 10))
            else
                fs := "." + str.format("{0,number,000}", frac)
        out := "P"
        if d != 0
            out := out + str.tostring(d) + "D"
        if h != 0 or mi != 0 or s != 0 or frac != 0
            out := out + "T"
            if h != 0
                out := out + str.tostring(h) + "H"
            if mi != 0
                out := out + str.tostring(mi) + "M"
            if s != 0 or frac != 0
                out := out + str.tostring(s) + fs + "S"
        if str.length(out) == 1
            out := "PT0S"
        if ms < 0
            out := "-" + out
    out

// @function        Format a DateTime as an ISO-8601 week date. Note the year shown is the week-based year, which for early January can differ from the calendar year.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-Www-D".
export method to_iso_week_date(DateTime this) =>
    str.format("{0,number,0000}-W{1,number,00}-{2,number,0}", this.iso_week_year(), this.iso_week(), this.weekday().to_iso_dow())

// @function        Format a DateTime as an ISO-8601 ordinal date.
// @param this      The DateTime to format.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-DDD".
export method to_iso_ordinal_date(DateTime this) =>
    str.format("{0,number,0000}-{1,number,000}", this.Year, this.day_of_year())

// @function        The IANA identifier for a zone, e.g. "America/New_York". This is the bridge to the built-ins: hand it to timestamp(), str.format_time() or any other Pine function that wants a timezone string. Written out rather than read off the enum's string value so that renaming a label can never silently change output.
// @param this      The zone.
// @returns         The IANA identifier. RAISES on a Zone the switch does not handle, a library bug. A silent fallback would hand a wrong timezone string on to timestamp() and str.format_time().
export method to_iana(Zone this) =>
    string out = na
    switch this
        Zone.UTC => out := "UTC"
        Zone.NEW_YORK => out := "America/New_York"
        Zone.CHICAGO => out := "America/Chicago"
        Zone.LONDON => out := "Europe/London"
        Zone.FRANKFURT => out := "Europe/Berlin"
        Zone.SYDNEY => out := "Australia/Sydney"
        Zone.TORONTO => out := "America/Toronto"
        Zone.SHANGHAI => out := "Asia/Shanghai"
        Zone.TOKYO => out := "Asia/Tokyo"
        Zone.HONG_KONG => out := "Asia/Hong_Kong"
        Zone.SINGAPORE => out := "Asia/Singapore"
        Zone.MUMBAI => out := "Asia/Kolkata"
        Zone.DUBAI => out := "Asia/Dubai"
        => runtime.error("to_iana: unhandled Zone. This is a library bug")
    out

// @function        Format an instant in the RFC 9557 extended format, which appends the zone in brackets so the reader knows which rules produced the offset. This is the form the JS Temporal proposal adopted, and it survives a round trip that a bare offset does not: "+01:00" could be London in summer or Berlin in winter.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone to express it in.
// @returns         A string of the form "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS+HH:MM[Area/Location]".
export format_ixdtf(int unix_ms, Zone z) =>
    unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z).to_iso() + "[" + z.to_iana() + "]"

// @function        The English name of a month.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @returns         The full name, e.g. "August". RAISES when Month is outside [1, 12]. The switch is seeded "December" so that arm needs no case of its own; without the bound check, a 13 would print December.
export month_name(int Month) =>
    if Month < 1 or Month > 12
        runtime.error("month_name: Month must be in [1, 12], got " + str.tostring(Month))
    string out = "December"
    switch Month
        1 => out := "January"
        2 => out := "February"
        3 => out := "March"
        4 => out := "April"
        5 => out := "May"
        6 => out := "June"
        7 => out := "July"
        8 => out := "August"
        9 => out := "September"
        10 => out := "October"
        11 => out := "November"
    out

// @function        The three-letter English abbreviation of a month.
// @param Month     Month of year, 1-12.
// @returns         The abbreviation, e.g. "Aug".
export month_abbr(int Month) =>
    str.substring(month_name(Month), 0, 3)

// @function        The English name of a weekday.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @returns         The full name, e.g. "Friday".
export method name(Weekday this) =>
    string out = "Sunday"
    switch this
        Weekday.MONDAY => out := "Monday"
        Weekday.TUESDAY => out := "Tuesday"
        Weekday.WEDNESDAY => out := "Wednesday"
        Weekday.THURSDAY => out := "Thursday"
        Weekday.FRIDAY => out := "Friday"
        Weekday.SATURDAY => out := "Saturday"
    out

// @function        The three-letter English abbreviation of a weekday.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @returns         The abbreviation, e.g. "Fri".
export method abbr(Weekday this) =>
    str.substring(this.name(), 0, 3)

// ============================================================================
// PATTERN FORMATTING, PARSING, AND INTEROP WITH THE BUILT-INS
//
// This section makes the library self-sufficient, not a supplement.
// format() reads the same pattern letters as str.format_time, so a pattern
// moves between them unchanged, but the functions do not. format() works from
// a DateTime record rather than a timestamp plus a timezone string, format_time
// takes a Zone where the built-in takes a string, and `Y` means different years
// in the two. Swap one for the other and you get a compile error at best and a
// wrong year at worst.
//
// Two things they do that str.format_time cannot:
//   * `Y` is the ISO week-based year, distinct from `y`. A week-year quietly
//     disagrees with the calendar year for a few days each January, which is
//     what makes "YYMMdd" for "yyMMdd" such a well-camouflaged bug.
//   * It formats a DateTime whose offset was computed by this library, so the
//     offset designator reflects the zone rules actually applied, not the
//     chart's timezone.
//
// parse_iso goes the other way and accepts more than timestamp() does: the
// extended and basic forms, week dates, ordinal dates, fractional seconds, all
// offset spellings, and the RFC 9557 bracketed-zone suffix.
//
// A general pattern-driven parser is deliberately absent. parse(str, pattern)
// is not something the built-ins offer either, ISO covers the formats
// machine-written dates actually arrive in, and a partial one would fail in
// ways that look like data problems. parse_iso returns na instead of guessing.
// ============================================================================

const string FMT_LETTERS = "GyYMdDEeuwQHkhKmsSaXZzV"
// Real DateTimeFormatter fields this library does not implement. They are held
// apart from the pass-through because the two failures look nothing alike: an
// unrecognised character is usually punctuation the caller wants printed;
// the pass-through exists so that the T in "yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss" survives
// it. One of these is a field the caller expects to be filled in, and
// printing it back as literal text would be a wrong answer, so it raises.
// Quote it if you meant it literally: "'Week' w" formats, "Week w" raises.
const string FMT_RESERVED = "LBFWOpxgnNcq"

// Zero-pad a number to a width, keeping any minus sign outside the padding.
pad_num(int v, int width) =>
    string s = str.tostring(math.abs(v))
    string out = s
    int deficit = width - str.length(s)
    if deficit > 0
        for i = 1 to deficit
            out := "0" + out
    if v < 0
        out := "-" + out
    out

// Offset designator in one of the ISO spellings. style 1 = +HH, 2 = +HHMM,
// 3 = +HH:MM. Minutes are carried because real offsets are not all whole
// hours: India is +05:30, Nepal +05:45, Chatham +12:45.
iso_offset(float utc, int style, bool use_z) =>
    string out = ""
    if utc == 0.0 and use_z
        out := "Z"
    else
        string sign = "+"
        if utc < 0
            sign := "-"
        float mag = math.abs(utc)
        int oh = int(math.floor(mag))
        int om = int(math.round((mag - oh) * 60.0))
        if style == 1
            out := sign + pad_num(oh, 2)
        else if style == 2
            out := sign + pad_num(oh, 2) + pad_num(om, 2)
        else
            out := sign + pad_num(oh, 2) + ":" + pad_num(om, 2)
    out

// Render one run of pattern letters.
fmt_field(DateTime d, string c, int cnt, string zone_name) =>
    string out = ""
    if c == "G"
        out := "AD"
        if d.Year <= 0
            out := "BC"
    else if c == "y"
        int v = d.Year
        if cnt == 2
            out := pad_num(pmod(v, 100), 2)
        else
            out := pad_num(v, cnt)
    else if c == "Y"
        int v = d.iso_week_year()
        if cnt == 2
            out := pad_num(pmod(v, 100), 2)
        else
            out := pad_num(v, cnt)
    else if c == "M"
        if cnt >= 4
            out := month_name(d.Month)
        else if cnt == 3
            out := month_abbr(d.Month)
        else
            out := pad_num(d.Month, cnt)
    else if c == "d"
        out := pad_num(d.Day, cnt)
    else if c == "D"
        out := pad_num(d.day_of_year(), cnt)
    else if c == "E"
        Weekday w = d.weekday()
        if cnt >= 4
            out := w.name()
        else
            out := w.abbr()
    else if c == "u"
        out := pad_num(d.weekday().to_iso_dow(), cnt)
    else if c == "e"
        out := pad_num(d.weekday().to_int() + 1, cnt)
    else if c == "w"
        out := pad_num(d.iso_week(), cnt)
    else if c == "Q"
        int q = quarter(d.Month)
        if cnt >= 3
            out := "Q" + str.tostring(q)
        else
            out := pad_num(q, cnt)
    else if c == "H"
        out := pad_num(d.Hour, cnt)
    else if c == "k"
        int v = d.Hour
        if v == 0
            v := 24
        out := pad_num(v, cnt)
    else if c == "h"
        int v = pmod(d.Hour, 12)
        if v == 0
            v := 12
        out := pad_num(v, cnt)
    else if c == "K"
        out := pad_num(pmod(d.Hour, 12), cnt)
    else if c == "m"
        out := pad_num(d.Minute, cnt)
    else if c == "s"
        out := pad_num(d.Second, cnt)
    else if c == "S"
        string ms3 = pad_num(d.MS, 3)
        if cnt >= 3
            out := ms3
            if cnt > 3
                for i = 1 to cnt - 3
                    out := out + "0"
        else
            out := str.substring(ms3, 0, cnt)
    else if c == "a"
        out := "AM"
        if d.Hour >= 12
            out := "PM"
    else if c == "X"
        int style = cnt
        if style > 3
            style := 3
        out := iso_offset(d.UTC, style, true)
    else if c == "Z"
        out := iso_offset(d.UTC, 2, false)
    else
        // z and V: the zone identifier when one is known, otherwise the offset,
        // because a DateTime carries an offset and not a zone.
        out := zone_name
        if str.length(zone_name) == 0
            out := iso_offset(d.UTC, 3, false)
    out

// Walk the pattern: runs of the same letter are fields, single quotes escape
// literal text, '' is a literal quote, everything else passes through.
fmt_core(DateTime d, string pattern, string zone_name) =>
    string out = ""
    int i = 0
    int n = str.length(pattern)
    while i < n
        string c = str.substring(pattern, i, i + 1)
        if c == "'"
            bool handled = false
            if i + 1 < n and str.substring(pattern, i + 1, i + 2) == "'"
                out := out + "'"
                i := i + 2
                handled := true
            if not handled
                int j = i + 1
                while j < n and str.substring(pattern, j, j + 1) != "'"
                    out := out + str.substring(pattern, j, j + 1)
                    j := j + 1
                if j >= n
                    runtime.error("format pattern: unterminated quote in \"" + pattern + "\"")
                i := j + 1
        else if str.contains(FMT_LETTERS, c)
            int j = i
            while j < n and str.substring(pattern, j, j + 1) == c
                j := j + 1
            out := out + fmt_field(d, c, j - i, zone_name)
            i := j
        else if str.contains(FMT_RESERVED, c)
            runtime.error("format pattern: \"" + c + "\" is a letter this library does not implement, in \"" + pattern + "\". Quote it ('" + c + "') if you meant it as literal text.")
        else
            out := out + c
            i := i + 1
    out

// @function        Format this date-time with a pattern. The pattern letters are str.format_time's, so a pattern carries across unchanged, but the two are not substitutes for each other; see the section note above. Supports G y Y M d D E e u w Q H k h K m s S a X Z z V, single-quoted literals, and '' for a literal quote. Note y is the calendar year and Y is the ISO week-based year: they differ for a few days each January, the confusion behind the "YYYY versus yyyy" bug.
// @param this      The DateTime.
// @param pattern   The format pattern, e.g. "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss".
// @returns         The formatted string. Characters outside the pattern language pass through as literals (that is what keeps the T in "yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss"), with one exception: the reserved letters L B F W O p x g n N c q raise, because they are real DateTimeFormatter fields this library does not implement, and printing one back as text would look like an answer. Single-quote any of them you want literally. RAISES on an unterminated quote too.
export method format(DateTime this, string pattern) =>
    fmt_core(this, pattern, "")

// @function        Format an instant in a zone. Takes a Zone rather than a timezone string, so it is not a drop-in for str.format_time despite the matching shape: pass syminfo.timezone here and it will not compile; a Zone is checked at compile time and a string is not. Unlike the built-in, `z` and `V` resolve to the IANA identifier and `Y` gives the true ISO week-based year.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param pattern   The format pattern.
// @param z         The zone to express the instant in. Required, with no default. This is the migration path from str.format_time, whose own default is the chart's timezone: a UTC default here would let a ported call compile cleanly and then silently mislabel every evening New York bar. Use z.to_iana() when you need the string form for a built-in.
// @returns         The formatted string. RAISES as format does: a reserved pattern letter, or an unterminated quote.
export format_time(int unix_ms, string pattern, Zone z) =>
    fmt_core(unix_to_date_zone(unix_ms, z), pattern, z.to_iana())

// ------------------------------------------------------------- parsing --

// Read `len` digits at `from` as an int, or na when they are not all digits.
num_at(string s, int from, int len) =>
    int out = 0
    bool ok = str.length(s) >= from + len and len > 0
    if ok
        for i = 0 to len - 1
            float v = str.tonumber(str.substring(s, from + i, from + i + 1))
            if na(v)
                ok := false
            else
                out := out * 10 + int(v)
    int r = na
    if ok
        r := out
    r

// Fractional seconds from `from` to the end, scaled to whole milliseconds. na
// when any character is not a digit: a fraction is the one field whose length
// is open-ended, so the consumed-length check below cannot police it, and
// num_at only ever inspects the three digits it keeps.
frac_ms(string s, int from) =>
    int n = str.length(s)
    bool ok = n > from
    if ok
        for i = from to n - 1
            if na(str.tonumber(str.substring(s, i, i + 1)))
                ok := false
    int out = na
    if ok
        int take = n - from
        if take > 3
            take := 3
        out := num_at(s, from, take)
        // Guarded: with three digits already taken the range is empty, and
        // Pine would count down through it and scale the value twice.
        if take < 3
            for i = 1 to 3 - take
                out := out * 10
    out

// @function        Parse an ISO-8601 date-time. Accepts more than timestamp() does: extended ("2026-08-21") and basic ("20260821") forms, week dates ("2026-W34-5"), ordinal dates ("2026-233"), an optional time after T or a space, fractional seconds, every offset spelling (Z, +05, +0530, +05:30), and an RFC 9557 bracketed zone suffix, which is read and discarded.
// @param s         The string to parse.
// @returns         A DateTime carrying any offset found, or na when the string is not valid ISO-8601. Returns na rather than guessing.
export parse_iso(string s) =>
    string t = s
    // RFC 9557 appends the zone in brackets; the offset already carries the
    // information needed to place the instant, so the suffix is dropped.
    if str.contains(t, "[")
        t := str.substring(t, 0, str.pos(t, "["))
    int n = str.length(t)
    int Y = na
    int M = 1
    int D = 1
    int datelen = 0
    bool ok = true

    if n >= 8 and str.substring(t, 4, 5) == "-" and str.substring(t, 5, 6) == "W"
        // 2026-W34-5
        int wy = num_at(t, 0, 4)
        int wk = num_at(t, 6, 2)
        int wd = 1
        int used = 8
        if n >= 10 and str.substring(t, 8, 9) == "-"
            wd := num_at(t, 9, 1)
            used := 10
        if na(wy) or na(wk) or na(wd) or wk < 1 or wk > 53 or wd < 1 or wd > 7
            ok := false
        else if wk > iso_weeks_in_year(wy)
            // 53 is the ceiling across all years, not the ceiling of this one:
            // most ISO years hold 52 weeks. Without the real bound "2025-W53-1"
            // reaches date_from_iso_week, which raises, and a raise from a parser
            // takes the whole script down over a string it is meant to reject.
            ok := false
        else
            DateTime wdt = date_from_iso_week(wy, wk, iso_dow_to_weekday(wd))
            Y := wdt.Year
            M := wdt.Month
            D := wdt.Day
            datelen := used
    else if n >= 10 and str.substring(t, 4, 5) == "-" and str.substring(t, 7, 8) == "-"
        Y := num_at(t, 0, 4)
        M := num_at(t, 5, 2)
        D := num_at(t, 8, 2)
        datelen := 10
    else if n >= 8 and str.substring(t, 4, 5) == "-"
        // ordinal: 2026-233
        int doy = num_at(t, 5, 3)
        Y := num_at(t, 0, 4)
        if na(Y) or na(doy) or doy < 1 or doy > days_in_year(Y)
            ok := false
        else
            DateTime od = date_from_day_of_year(Y, doy)
            M := od.Month
            D := od.Day
            datelen := 8
    else if n >= 8
        Y := num_at(t, 0, 4)
        M := num_at(t, 4, 2)
        D := num_at(t, 6, 2)
        datelen := 8
    else
        ok := false

    if ok and (na(Y) or na(M) or na(D))
        ok := false
    if ok and not is_valid_date(Y, M, D)
        ok := false

    int hh = 0
    int mi = 0
    int ss = 0
    int ms = 0
    float utc = 0.0

    if ok and n > datelen
        string sep = str.substring(t, datelen, datelen + 1)
        if sep != "T" and sep != " " and sep != "t"
            ok := false
        else
            string rest = str.substring(t, datelen + 1, n)
            int rn = str.length(rest)
            // A separator with nothing after it is not a time. Without this
            // "2026-08-21T" slips past the shape gate below (rn == 0 skips it)
            // and parses as midnight, inventing a precision the string does
            // not carry.
            if rn == 0
                ok := false
            // Trailing offset. Lowercase "z" is matched on purpose: RFC 3339
            // spells the designator case-insensitively, the same licence under
            // which "t" is accepted as the separator above. A garbage tail
            // ("...T13:30:00xyz") cannot set the offset: the consumed-length
            // check on the time part below rejects it.
            int cut = rn
            int oi = -1
            // Guarded for the same reason: an empty remainder would make Pine
            // walk the range backwards rather than skip it.
            if rn > 0
                for k = 0 to rn - 1
                    string ch = str.substring(rest, k, k + 1)
                    if oi < 0 and k > 0 and (ch == "Z" or ch == "z" or ch == "+" or ch == "-")
                        oi := k
            if oi >= 0
                string off = str.substring(rest, oi, rn)
                cut := oi
                string o0 = str.substring(off, 0, 1)
                if o0 == "Z" or o0 == "z"
                    utc := 0.0
                    if str.length(off) != 1
                        ok := false
                else
                    int on = str.length(off)
                    int oh = num_at(off, 1, 2)
                    int om = 0
                    if on == 6 and str.substring(off, 3, 4) == ":"
                        om := num_at(off, 4, 2)
                    else if on == 5
                        om := num_at(off, 3, 2)
                    else if on != 3
                        ok := false
                    if na(oh) or na(om)
                        ok := false
                    else
                        utc := oh + om / 60.0
                        if o0 == "-"
                            utc := -utc
            string tp = str.substring(rest, 0, cut)
            int tn = str.length(tp)
            if ok and tn > 0
                bool ext = str.contains(tp, ":")
                // Reading fields at fixed offsets under "tn >= 5" would say
                // nothing about the characters between and after them: "13:"
                // would parse as 13:00 and "13:30:00abc" would quietly drop
                // its tail. A time is only as legal as its consumed length:
                // extended form ends at 2, 5 or 8 characters, basic at 2, 4
                // or 6, and either may carry a fraction on the seconds.
                int fsep = 6
                if ext
                    fsep := 8
                bool has_frac = false
                if tn > fsep + 1
                    string fc = str.substring(tp, fsep, fsep + 1)
                    has_frac := fc == "." or fc == ","
                bool shape = tn == 2 or tn == 4 or tn == 6 or has_frac
                if ext
                    bool c2 = tn > 2 and str.substring(tp, 2, 3) == ":"
                    bool c5 = tn > 5 and str.substring(tp, 5, 6) == ":"
                    shape := tn == 2 or (tn == 5 and c2) or ((tn == 8 or has_frac) and c2 and c5)
                if not shape
                    ok := false
                else if ext
                    hh := num_at(tp, 0, 2)
                    if tn >= 5
                        mi := num_at(tp, 3, 2)
                    if tn >= 8
                        ss := num_at(tp, 6, 2)
                    if has_frac
                        ms := frac_ms(tp, 9)
                else
                    hh := num_at(tp, 0, 2)
                    if tn >= 4
                        mi := num_at(tp, 2, 2)
                    if tn >= 6
                        ss := num_at(tp, 4, 2)
                    // The basic form takes a fraction too:
                    // "20260821T133000.5" carries ms = 500.
                    if has_frac
                        ms := frac_ms(tp, 7)
                if na(hh) or na(mi) or na(ss) or na(ms)
                    ok := false

    DateTime out = na
    if ok and hh >= 0 and hh <= 23 and mi >= 0 and mi <= 59 and ss >= 0 and ss <= 59
        out := new_datetime(Y, M, D, hh, mi, ss, ms, utc)
    out

// @function        Parse an ISO-8601 duration such as "P2DT4H13M9S", "PT1.5S" or "-PT10M" (the form format_iso_duration emits) into exact milliseconds, round-tripping everything that formatter can produce. Years, months and weeks are refused even though ISO allows them: a duration is machine time, and those are calendar amounts whose meaning depends on a reference date: parse_iso_period reads them into a Period instead. Both "." and "," start a fraction, the same licence parse_iso grants, and fraction digits past the third are truncated the same way; the fraction is accepted on the seconds field only, the one place the formatter puts one. A leading "-" negates the whole span, as the formatter spells it; per-component signs are java.time's Duration.toString dialect, which nothing here emits, and are not read.
// @param s         The string to parse.
// @returns         The span in milliseconds, or na when the string is not a valid date-part-free ISO duration. na and never a raise: malformed input is data rather than a caller bug, the clause every parser in this file follows.
export parse_iso_duration(string s) =>
    string t = str.upper(s)
    int sign = 1
    if str.startswith(t, "-")
        sign := -1
        t := str.substring(t, 1, str.length(t))
    int out = na
    // The outer length check doubles as the loop guard: a bare "P" would make
    // the range below run backwards rather than skip, exactly the trap
    // parse_iso_period documents.
    if str.startswith(t, "P") and str.length(t) > 1
        bool ok = true
        bool in_time = false
        bool time_seen = false
        bool seen_digit = false
        bool seen_part = false
        bool in_frac = false
        int frac_digits = 0
        int frac = 0
        int num = 0
        int acc = 0
        for i = 1 to str.length(t) - 1
            string ch = str.substring(t, i, i + 1)
            float v = str.tonumber(ch)
            if not na(v)
                if in_frac
                    frac_digits := frac_digits + 1
                    // First three fraction digits weigh 100, 10 and 1 ms;
                    // anything finer is sub-millisecond precision an integer
                    // millisecond count cannot hold, dropped as frac_ms drops it.
                    if frac_digits == 1
                        frac := frac + int(v) * 100
                    else if frac_digits == 2
                        frac := frac + int(v) * 10
                    else if frac_digits == 3
                        frac := frac + int(v)
                else
                    num := num * 10 + int(v)
                seen_digit := true
            else if (ch == "." or ch == ",") and in_time and seen_digit and not in_frac
                in_frac := true
            else if ch == "T" and not in_time and not seen_digit
                in_time := true
            else if not seen_digit
                ok := false
            else if ch == "D" and not in_time and not in_frac
                acc := acc + num * MS_DAY
                seen_part := true
                num := 0
                seen_digit := false
            else if ch == "H" and in_time and not in_frac
                acc := acc + num * MS_HOUR
                time_seen := true
                seen_part := true
                num := 0
                seen_digit := false
            else if ch == "M" and in_time and not in_frac
                // An M before the T would be a month, and falls through to the
                // rejection arm below with Y and W; this arm is minutes only.
                acc := acc + num * MS_MINUTE
                time_seen := true
                seen_part := true
                num := 0
                seen_digit := false
            else if ch == "S" and in_time and (not in_frac or frac_digits > 0)
                acc := acc + num * MS_SECOND + frac
                time_seen := true
                seen_part := true
                num := 0
                frac := 0
                frac_digits := 0
                in_frac := false
                seen_digit := false
            else
                ok := false
        // A trailing number without a designator, a dangling fraction, a bare
        // "PT", or a T followed by nothing are all shapes the formatter cannot
        // produce and ISO does not define, so they are refused with the rest.
        if ok and seen_part and not seen_digit and not in_frac and (not in_time or time_seen)
            out := sign * acc
    out

// ------------------------------------------------- built-in interop --

// @function        This weekday in Pine's own numbering, where Sunday is 1, the scale used by the dayofweek.* constants and returned by the dayofweek built-in. Distinct from to_int(), which is 0-based.
// @param this      The weekday.
// @returns         An integer in [1, 7] comparable with dayofweek.monday and friends.
export method to_pine_dow(Weekday this) =>
    this.to_int() + 1

// @function        The Weekday for a value from Pine's dayofweek built-in or the dayofweek.* constants, where Sunday is 1.
// @param n         Pine day-of-week value in [1, 7].
// @returns         The Weekday.
export weekday_from_pine_dow(int n) =>
    weekday_from_int(n - 1)

// @function        The Zone for an IANA identifier, so a string from syminfo.timezone or a user input can be turned into something the rule engine understands.
// @param name      The IANA identifier, e.g. "America/New_York". Case-sensitive.
// @returns         The Zone, or na when the identifier is not one this library models. na means "not modelled", not UTC.
export zone_from_iana(string name) =>
    Zone out = na
    if name == "UTC" or name == "Etc/UTC" or name == "GMT"
        out := Zone.UTC
    if name == "America/New_York"
        out := Zone.NEW_YORK
    if name == "America/Chicago"
        out := Zone.CHICAGO
    if name == "Europe/London"
        out := Zone.LONDON
    if name == "Europe/Berlin"
        out := Zone.FRANKFURT
    if name == "Australia/Sydney"
        out := Zone.SYDNEY
    if name == "America/Toronto"
        out := Zone.TORONTO
    if name == "Asia/Shanghai"
        out := Zone.SHANGHAI
    if name == "Asia/Tokyo"
        out := Zone.TOKYO
    if name == "Asia/Hong_Kong"
        out := Zone.HONG_KONG
    if name == "Asia/Singapore"
        out := Zone.SINGAPORE
    // Calcutta is the tzdb's older spelling of the same zone, still emitted
    // by feeds that predate the rename, so both names resolve.
    if name == "Asia/Kolkata" or name == "Asia/Calcutta"
        out := Zone.MUMBAI
    if name == "Asia/Dubai"
        out := Zone.DUBAI
    out

// @function        The offset in force in a zone at an instant, spelled the way Pine's timezone arguments accept it. Useful when a built-in needs a fixed offset rather than a zone name.
// @param unix_ms   Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
// @param z         The zone.
// @returns         A string such as "UTC-04:00".
export zone_offset_string(int unix_ms, Zone z) =>
    "UTC" + iso_offset(offset_at(unix_ms, z), 3, false)

// @function        The month number for an English month name or three-letter abbreviation, case-insensitive.
// @param name      The month name, e.g. "August" or "Aug".
// @returns         Month in [1, 12], or na when unrecognised.
export month_from_name(string name) =>
    string t = str.upper(name)
    int out = na
    for m = 1 to 12
        if na(out)
            string full = str.upper(month_name(m))
            if t == full or t == str.substring(full, 0, 3)
                out := m
    out

// @function        The Weekday for an English day name or three-letter abbreviation, case-insensitive.
// @param name      The day name, e.g. "Friday" or "Fri".
// @returns         The Weekday, or na when unrecognised.
export weekday_from_name(string name) =>
    string t = str.upper(name)
    Weekday out = na
    for i = 0 to 6
        Weekday w = weekday_from_int(i)
        string full = str.upper(w.name())
        if t == full or t == str.substring(full, 0, 3)
            out := w
    out

// @function        Format a span of milliseconds as a human-readable duration, largest unit first. str.format_time cannot do this: it wraps past 24 hours and has no day field, so a 50-hour countdown formats as two hours. For a countdown, pass target - timenow.
// @param ms        The span in milliseconds; negative spans are prefixed with a minus sign. na in gives na out, rather than a string built around the word "NaN".
// @param parts     How many units to show, 1-4. Default 2, so "2d 4h" rather than "2d 4h 13m 9s".
// @returns         A string such as "2d 4h", "13m 9s" or "0s", or na when ms is na. RAISES when parts is outside [1, 4]: parts is the caller's formatting choice rather than data, so it is a bug, which is why it raises while an na span does not.
export format_duration(int ms, int parts = 2) =>
    if parts < 1 or parts > 4
        runtime.error("format_duration: parts must be in [1, 4], got " + str.tostring(parts))
    // na propagates as na rather than degrading into a string built around the
    // word "NaN". The natural caller is format_duration(sess.time_to_close(t)),
    // and that returns na whenever the market is shut, which is most of the
    // day, so an unguarded formatter would put garbage on a label far more
    // often than it put a duration there.
    string out = na
    if not na(ms)
        int a = math.abs(ms)
        int d = int(a / MS_DAY)
        int rem = a % MS_DAY
        int h = int(rem / MS_HOUR)
        rem := rem % MS_HOUR
        int mi = int(rem / MS_MINUTE)
        rem := rem % MS_MINUTE
        int s = int(rem / MS_SECOND)

        array<int> vals = array.from(d, h, mi, s)
        array<string> units = array.from("d", "h", "m", "s")
        // Start at the largest non-zero unit, so a short span does not print as
        // "0d 0h" and a long one does not lead with seconds.
        int first = -1
        for i = 0 to 3
            if first < 0 and vals.get(i) > 0
                first := i
        if first < 0
            first := 3
        int last = math.min(first + parts - 1, 3)
        out := ""
        for i = first to last
            if str.length(out) > 0
                out := out + " "
            out := out + str.tostring(vals.get(i)) + units.get(i)
        if ms < 0
            out := "-" + out
    out

// @function        An instant described relative to another, the way a human says it: "3d 4h ago" for the past, "in 2h 15m" for the future, "now" within a second either way. A wrapper over format_duration that folds the sign into direction words, because "-2h 15m" is arithmetic rather than language. The sub-second band exists because format_duration spells anything under a second "0s", and "in 0s" is not a thing anyone says.
// @param unix_ms   The instant being described, Unix milliseconds.
// @param now_ms    The instant it is measured from, typically timenow.
// @param parts     How many units to show, 1-4, passed through to format_duration. Default 2.
// @returns         A string such as "3d 4h ago", "in 2h 15m" or "now", or na when either instant is na, matching format_duration. RAISES when parts is outside [1, 4]. The check is here, not just in the delegate: the "now" branch returns before format_duration runs, and a bad parts value should fail on every call, not only once the instants drift more than a second apart.
export format_relative(int unix_ms, int now_ms, int parts = 2) =>
    if parts < 1 or parts > 4
        runtime.error("format_relative: parts must be in [1, 4], got " + str.tostring(parts))
    string out = na
    if not na(unix_ms) and not na(now_ms)
        int delta = unix_ms - now_ms
        if math.abs(delta) < MS_SECOND
            out := "now"
        else if delta < 0
            out := format_duration(-delta, parts) + " ago"
        else
            out := "in " + format_duration(delta, parts)
    out
